window.FOVAL_CONTENT["clear-writing"] = {"lessons":{"01-what-happens-when-someone-reads-you":{"content":"<p>A friend forwards you a 900-word email from her landlord and asks what it means. You read it. Somewhere in the sixth paragraph there is a date, and the date is in nine days, and everything else in the email is context. Your friend had already read it twice and missed the date both times.</p>\n<p>The landlord isn&#39;t a bad person and the email isn&#39;t badly spelled. It&#39;s written in the order the landlord thought about the problem, which isn&#39;t the order your friend needed to meet it. That mismatch is what this course is about.</p>\n<p>Before any advice about how to write, you need to know what actually happens on the other end. Not what should happen. What the evidence says does.</p>\n<h2>Nobody is obliged to keep reading</h2>\n<p>Start with the one measurement of ordinary reading that most writers have heard of, and then take it apart, because it&#39;s quoted far more often than it&#39;s understood.</p>\n<p>In 2008 Jakob Nielsen published <a href=\"https://www.nngroup.com/articles/how-little-do-users-read/\">an analysis of how little people read online</a>. The underlying data came from a study by Weinreich and colleagues, who &quot;instrumented 25 users&#39; browsers and recorded extended information about everything they did&quot; as those people went about their normal web use. After cleaning, the analysis covered 45,237 page views. That isn&#39;t a laboratory task; it&#39;s a record of people doing whatever they were already doing.</p>\n<p>Two numbers came out of it. A page visit takes about <strong>25 seconds</strong> whatever is on the page, and each additional 100 words adds about <strong>4.4 seconds</strong>. Be careful with the first number: Nielsen&#39;s own reading of it is that &quot;people spend some of their time understanding the page layout and navigation features, as well as looking at the images.&quot; The 25 seconds isn&#39;t reading. It&#39;s arriving.</p>\n<div class=\"think predict\"><b>Predict first</b><p class=\"think-q\">A page ten times as long gets how much more attention: ten times, twice, or about the same?</p><details><summary>Show the answer</summary><p>Nowhere near ten times. Take the 25 seconds off, since it goes on the page rather than the words, and what&#39;s left is 4.4 seconds per hundred words. Nielsen assumed his readers managed 250 words a minute, which is 24 seconds per hundred. So each extra hundred words gets under a fifth of the time it would take to read. A page ten times as long gets roughly twice the total attention, most of it still the arrival cost, and the shortfall grows with every paragraph rather than staying put.</p>\n</details></div>\n\n<p>Nielsen worked through what that means for the page his readers actually met, which averaged 593 words. His conclusion, in his words: &quot;On average, users will have time to read 28% of the words if they devote all of their time to reading. More realistically, users will read about 20% of the text on the average page.&quot; The &quot;if&quot; matters, and it&#39;s the part that gets dropped when the figure is quoted. And one more line from the same analysis: &quot;Users read half the information only on those pages with 111 words or less.&quot;</p>\n<h2>The two lines, and the gap between them</h2>\n<p>Put the two rates on the same axes and the finding stops being a slogan.</p>\n<p>One line is the time a reader would <strong>need</strong> in order to read every word, at 250 words a minute. The other is the time the reader actually <strong>gives</strong>: 25 seconds, plus 4.4 seconds per 100 words. Both come straight out of the numbers above.</p>\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 420 300\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:420px;display:block;margin:1.5em auto;font-family:system-ui,sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"budget-title budget-desc\">\n<title id=\"budget-title\">Time a reader needs, against time a reader gives</title>\n<desc id=\"budget-desc\">Two straight lines on axes of document length in words against seconds. The line for time needed rises steeply, reaching 288 seconds at 1,200 words, because reading every word at 250 words a minute costs 24 seconds per hundred words. The line for time given rises gently from 25 seconds at zero words to about 78 seconds at 1,200 words, because a reader gives only 4.4 seconds more per hundred words, and the first 25 seconds go on the page rather than the words. A dashed marker at 593 words, the average page in the study, shows the reader giving about 51 seconds against 142 needed. The gap widens steadily; at 1,200 words the reader is giving roughly a quarter of the time the document would take to read.</desc>\n<text x=\"8\" y=\"22\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"var(--text, #111418)\">The time given never catches up</text>\n<text x=\"8\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\">On the average page, a quarter of it at most</text>\n<line x1=\"50\" y1=\"250\" x2=\"400\" y2=\"250\" stroke=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n<line x1=\"50\" y1=\"250\" x2=\"50\" y2=\"60\" stroke=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n<line x1=\"223\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"223\" y2=\"250\" stroke=\"var(--gold, #b08d2a)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/>\n<line x1=\"50\" y1=\"250\" x2=\"400\" y2=\"68\" stroke=\"var(--oxblood, #8b1e2d)\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n<line x1=\"50\" y1=\"234\" x2=\"400\" y2=\"201\" stroke=\"var(--navy, #0f2a4a)\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n<circle cx=\"223\" cy=\"160\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"var(--oxblood, #8b1e2d)\"/>\n<circle cx=\"223\" cy=\"218\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"var(--navy, #0f2a4a)\"/>\n<text x=\"62\" y=\"100\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--oxblood, #8b1e2d)\">time it takes to read it all</text>\n<text x=\"232\" y=\"238\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--navy, #0f2a4a)\">time the reader gives</text>\n<text x=\"50\" y=\"268\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\">0</text>\n<text x=\"223\" y=\"268\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\">593, the average page</text>\n<text x=\"400\" y=\"268\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\">1200</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"288\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\">length of the document, in words</text>\n<text x=\"42\" y=\"179\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\">120</text>\n<text x=\"42\" y=\"103\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\">240</text>\n<text x=\"56\" y=\"78\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\">seconds</text>\n</svg>\n\n<p>The lines never meet again once they part, and they part almost at once. Run the arithmetic on the landlord&#39;s email from the top of this lesson, which is 900 words. Time given: 25 seconds, plus 4.4 for each of nine hundreds, which is 25 + 39.6, call it 65 seconds. Time needed at 250 words a minute: 900 divided by 250 is 3.6 minutes, which is 216 seconds. Sixty-five over 216 is 30 percent, and that&#39;s the ceiling, before a single second goes on working out where the paragraphs start. Something like a fifth of that email is what a reader who hasn&#39;t yet decided it matters will actually take in. The date was in paragraph six.</p>\n<p><strong>Past the first hundred words or so, you aren&#39;t deciding whether the reader misses something. You&#39;re deciding what.</strong> A landlord who buries a date in paragraph six has made that decision without noticing.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><b>Where these lines come from</b><p>The 25 seconds and the 4.4 seconds are Nielsen&#39;s, from the browsing data. The 250 words a minute is also his, and he&#39;s explicit that it&#39;s generous: &quot;Usually, I assume a reading speed of 200 words per minute (WPM), but because the users in this study are highly literate, I&#39;ll go with 250 WPM.&quot; At 200 words a minute the red line is steeper and the gap wider. The shape doesn&#39;t change, and the shape is the point.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Where the number does not apply</h2>\n<p>The quotation usually stops before the caveats, and leaving them out turns a useful estimate into a bad rule.</p>\n<p>Nielsen says two things about his own data that limit it. The participants &quot;had above-average intelligence, with several being university employees.&quot; And he treated the very long pages in the data as beside the point: &quot;Pages with a huge word count are probably not &#39;real&#39; pages anyway,&quot; he wrote, more likely academic papers or terms and conditions than pages anyone was reading as a page.</p>\n<p>So the finding is about <strong>capable people skimming ordinary web pages.</strong> It isn&#39;t a law about reading.</p>\n<p>Think about how differently the same person reads in a single day. They skim a news page over breakfast and take in a fifth of it. Then a letter arrives from the hospital about their mother, and they read every word of it twice, then read it again to someone on the phone. Same reader, same eyes. What changed is that the second document was about something they needed.</p>\n<p>This isn&#39;t a hole in the research. It&#39;s the research telling you what question it answered. The right use of the 20 percent figure is as a <strong>default assumption for a reader who has not yet decided your document matters to them</strong>, which describes most documents most of the time. The wrong use is cutting a surgical consent form to two sentences.</p>\n<div class=\"think checkpoint\"><b>Check yourself</b><p class=\"think-q\">Which of these should you plan for using the 20 percent assumption, and which should you not?</p><details><summary>Show the answer</summary><p>An internal announcement about a change to the expenses system: <strong>use it.</strong> Nobody woke up wanting to read this, and it competes with everything else in the inbox.</p>\n<p>A letter telling someone their benefit claim has been refused and how to appeal: <strong>don&#39;t use it.</strong> This reader will read every word, several times, possibly while distressed. Length isn&#39;t the enemy here; ambiguity is, and so is making them hunt for the deadline.</p>\n<p>A product page for a kettle: <strong>use it.</strong> Classic skimming, and the reader is comparing rather than reading.</p>\n<p>The instructions on a fire door: <strong>neither.</strong> Nobody is reading this at all until the moment they are, and then they need one instruction they can take in at a glance. That&#39;s a design problem before it&#39;s a writing problem, which is lesson 7.</p>\n</details></div>\n\n<h2>Who the document is actually for</h2>\n<p>The reading budget tells you that you have less room than you thought. It doesn&#39;t tell you what to put in the room. For that you have to know who is reading, and most writers answer that question with a category rather than a person.</p>\n<p>&quot;Residents of the borough&quot; is a category. It can&#39;t change a single sentence of your letter. Compare it with: <em>people who owe the new charge, who need to know by when, and what happens if they can&#39;t pay.</em> That version tells you what the first paragraph is, what the second paragraph is, and what can be cut.</p>\n<p>The test for whether you&#39;ve named your audience properly is simple. <strong>If knowing the answer wouldn&#39;t change a sentence, you haven&#39;t answered it.</strong></p>\n<p>Two failures come up so often they feel like normal practice.</p>\n<p>The first is writing for the approver. A great many documents are shaped by what a manager, a legal team or a communications lead will accept, and this pressure is real, but the approver isn&#39;t the reader and the two want different things. The approver wants nothing wrong in it. The reader wants to know what to do. A document optimised entirely for the first is how you get nine hundred words of context and a date in paragraph six.</p>\n<p>The second is writing for yourself six months ago. It&#39;s hard to remember not knowing something. You write &quot;submit form CT2 by the deadline&quot; because you know what CT2 is and when the deadline is, and the sentence looks complete to you because your own knowledge quietly fills the gaps as you reread it. This is why rereading your own work is such a poor test of whether it&#39;s clear, and it&#39;s why this course ends with getting an actual other person to tell you what they think it said.</p>\n<h2>Plain does not mean simple, and it is not optional</h2>\n<p>There&#39;s a suspicion that writing plainly is a kind of condescension, that it means writing down to people. It needs answering, because it stops good writers from doing the thing that would help most.</p>\n<p>The UK government publishes its writing standards in the open. <a href=\"https://guidance.publishing.service.gov.uk/writing-to-gov-uk-standards/writing-guidelines/clear-language/\">Its guidance on clear language</a> gives a reason for the policy that isn&#39;t about elegance at all: <strong>&quot;1 in 6 adults in England have very poor literacy skills.&quot;</strong> For a government, writing a form that a sixth of the population can&#39;t use isn&#39;t a stylistic failure. It&#39;s a failure to deliver the service.</p>\n<p>Then the same guidance answers the condescension objection directly, reporting the research behind its policy: <strong>&quot;people with higher levels of literacy prefer plain English because it allows them to understand the information as quickly as possible.&quot;</strong></p>\n<p>A specialist doesn&#39;t read your plain sentence and feel patronised. They read it, get it, and move on to the part they actually needed their expertise for. Complexity in the writing doesn&#39;t signal complexity in the thought; it just spends the reader&#39;s attention on the wrong thing.</p>\n<p>None of this is one country&#39;s taste, either. In the United States the <a href=\"https://digital.gov/guides/plain-language\">Plain Writing Act of 2010</a> &quot;established the requirement that content for the public is written for its specific audience&quot;, and plain English is mandatory across the whole of GOV.UK. Two governments, two drafting traditions, the same conclusion.</p>\n<h2>Difficulty is not only a property of your document</h2>\n<p>Readability scores. You&#39;ve probably been told to trust them.</p>\n<p>There&#39;s a large industry built on scoring text for reading level. Paste a page in, get a grade. The scores come from formulas that count surface features: syllables per word, words per sentence. They&#39;re quick, and they&#39;re the reason people believe difficulty is something a document has, like a word count.</p>\n<p>In 2021 Gooding, Berzak, Mak and Sharifi ran <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.06354\">a study of 518 readers</a> that measured how people scrolled through texts of different difficulty. Two findings matter here. Reading behaviour differs measurably with text difficulty, so a text&#39;s level is real and shows up in how people move through it. And <strong>reader background influences both how people read and what makes a text difficult for them.</strong></p>\n<p>Put those together and difficulty is a relation between a text and a reader, not something the text carries on its own. The pension page that&#39;s transparent to someone in financial services and impenetrable to everyone else has one text, meeting different readers. Whether formulas measure anything useful is argued about, and this lesson doesn&#39;t settle it. What it does say is narrower: a formula that knows nothing about the reader can&#39;t tell you whether the reader understood. It can only tell you about syllables. You can improve a score by chopping a sentence in half at the comma and changing nothing whatever about what a reader takes away.</p>\n<div class=\"think predict\"><b>Predict first</b><p class=\"think-q\">A colleague reports that a rewrite moved the page from &quot;grade 12&quot; to &quot;grade 8&quot;. What have you learned about the rewrite?</p><details><summary>Show the answer</summary><p>That the words got shorter, the sentences got shorter, or both. Nothing else follows. You haven&#39;t learned whether a reader can now find the deadline, whether the main point moved to the front, whether the technical term that actually blocked people is still there, or whether the shorter sentences broke the connections between the ideas and made the passage harder to follow. The score went down. The question of whether anyone understands it better is untouched, and lesson 9 is about how to answer it properly.</p>\n</details></div>\n\n<h2>What people get wrong</h2>\n<p><strong>&quot;If they cared, they would read it properly.&quot;</strong> They might. But you don&#39;t get to choose whether your reader is tired, interrupted, on a phone with 4% battery, or reading in their second language. Writing that only works on an attentive reader is writing that fails exactly when it matters.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Shorter is always better.&quot;</strong> The findings above are about attention, not about length as a virtue. A 100-word notice that omits the appeal deadline hasn&#39;t saved the reader anything. Later lessons are almost entirely about making the same content easier to take in, which is a different move from having less content.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;My readers are like me.&quot;</strong> They know less about your subject, care less about it, and arrived from somewhere you didn&#39;t anticipate. The whole of lesson 9 exists because this belief survives everything except meeting an actual reader.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;A readability score measures clarity.&quot;</strong> It estimates one input to clarity by counting proxies. When the estimate and a real reader disagree, the reader is right.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Plain writing is for people who struggle.&quot;</strong> Answered above, by the people whose job it is to write for a whole country.</p>\n<h2>Practice</h2>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><b>Measure one thing you wrote</b><p>Take 25 minutes over this. Do it on something real: an email you sent, a report, a message to a group. It should be at least 300 words, and it should be something someone actually had to act on.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p>Count the words. Most editors will do it; otherwise paste it somewhere that will.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Work out the two numbers from this lesson, the way the landlord&#39;s email was worked above. Time the reader gives: 25 seconds, plus 4.4 seconds for every 100 words. Time the document needs: the word count divided by 250, times 60. Write both down in seconds.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Divide the first by the second. That&#39;s the most a distracted reader could have taken in, and the real share is lower, because some of the first 25 seconds went on the page and not the words.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Now the part that matters. Read your own document and mark the <strong>one sentence</strong> you&#39;d most want a reader to land on if they read only that share of it. Just one. Then find where it currently sits: how many words in, and how far down the screen on a phone.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Write one line naming the reader. Not a category. Who they are, what they came needing, and what they&#39;ll do next if they understand you. Then check your line against the test in this lesson: if knowing this wouldn&#39;t change a sentence, you haven&#39;t answered it yet.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Keep this document.</strong> It&#39;s the one you&#39;ll carry through the whole course, and it&#39;s the thing you&#39;ll hand to another person in lesson 9.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Where this goes next</h2>\n<p>You now have the constraint. A reader gives you a fixed and small amount of attention, they give it before deciding whether your document deserves more, and what they find hard depends on who they are rather than on a score.</p>\n<p>Everything after this answers that constraint. The next lesson is the most obvious answer: if the reader only gets part of your document, put the part that matters where they&#39;ll be. After that the course goes below the level of arrangement and into the sentence itself.</p>\n<h2>Go deeper</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Read <a href=\"https://www.nngroup.com/articles/how-little-do-users-read/\">Nielsen&#39;s article</a> in full, chart included. It&#39;s short, and the caveats this lesson leans on are all in it.</li>\n<li>The study Nielsen reanalysed: Harald Weinreich, Hartmut Obendorf, Eelco Herder and Matthias Mayer, &quot;Not Quite the Average: An Empirical Study of Web Use&quot;, <em>ACM Transactions on the Web</em> 2(1), 2008. Linked from Nielsen&#39;s article.</li>\n<li>The <a href=\"https://guidance.publishing.service.gov.uk/writing-to-gov-uk-standards/writing-guidelines/clear-language/\">GOV.UK clear language guidance</a> links the legal-language research behind its plain English policy.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.06354\">Gooding et al. (2021)</a>, for the scrolling method and the full findings; this lesson used the abstract.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Jakob Nielsen, <a href=\"https://www.nngroup.com/articles/how-little-do-users-read/\">&quot;How Little Do Users Read?&quot;</a>, Nielsen Norman Group, 5 May 2008. The 25-second and 4.4-second figures, the 593-word average page, the 45,237 page views, the 28 percent &quot;if they devote all of their time to reading&quot;, the 20 percent, the 111-word figure, the 250 words a minute and his usual 200, and his caveats about his participants and about very long pages.</li>\n<li>GOV.UK, <a href=\"https://guidance.publishing.service.gov.uk/writing-to-gov-uk-standards/writing-guidelines/clear-language/\">&quot;Use clear language&quot;</a>. The literacy figure, the mandate, and the research it reports about high-literacy readers preferring plain English.</li>\n<li>Digital.gov, <a href=\"https://digital.gov/guides/plain-language\">the Plain Language guide series</a>, including the Plain Writing Act of 2010.</li>\n<li>Sian Gooding, Yevgeni Berzak, Tony Mak and Matt Sharifi, <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.06354\">&quot;Predicting Text Readability from Scrolling Interactions&quot;</a> (2021), 518 participants. Read at abstract level. Reader background shapes both reading behaviour and what makes a text difficult; behaviour also differs with text difficulty.</li>\n</ul>\n","quiz":[{"q":"Your team's intranet carries a 600-word announcement about a new expenses system. Nobody has to open it. Using this lesson's figures, what should you expect of a typical colleague who does?","options":["They'll read about a fifth of it, so the part that matters has to sit where a skimmer lands","They'll read about half of it, since the study found that most pages get read to their midpoint","They'll read all of it, because the study measured people who chose to open each page themselves","They'll read none of it, because anything past 111 words is abandoned before it starts"],"answer":0,"explain":"600 words is almost exactly Nielsen's average page. The time given is 25 seconds plus 26 for the words, about 51 seconds, against 144 seconds to read it all, so 28 percent at the very most and about 20 percent once you allow for time spent on layout and images. That is A. B applies the \"half\" figure to the wrong length; readers got through half only on pages of 111 words or fewer. C mistakes opening a page for deciding to read it. D turns the 111-word figure into a cliff, and the study found nothing of the kind."},{"q":"A hospital sends a letter telling a patient how to prepare for an operation. A colleague says the 20 percent finding means the letter should be cut to two sentences. What is wrong with that reasoning?","options":["Nothing is wrong; a shorter letter is better in every setting, and two sentences is the safest length","The finding is about medical writing specifically, so it applies here more strongly than elsewhere","Cutting is right but two sentences is arbitrary; the correct target is always about 111 words","The finding came from people browsing web pages, and a patient reads such a letter quite differently"],"answer":3,"explain":"The study recorded people browsing the web in their own time, and its author said the figure describes a page a reader has not yet decided matters. A patient reading about their own operation has decided, and will read every word, so D is right. A generalises a browsing statistic into a rule for all writing. B has it backwards; nothing in the study was about medicine. C takes a figure from the same browsing setting and treats it as a target for every document."},{"q":"A mortgage offer is read by a conveyancing solicitor and by a first-time buyer. Which best describes what the research in this lesson says about the document's difficulty?","options":["It has one difficulty, fixed by the document, which both of these readers meet in the same measure","Its difficulty depends partly on the reader, so the same page is harder for one than the other","Its difficulty is set by the reader alone, so how the offer is worded makes no difference","Its difficulty is whatever a readability formula says, since that is the only measure we have"],"answer":1,"explain":"The scrolling study of 518 readers found that reader background shapes both how people read and what makes a text hard for them, so B is right. A is the assumption behind treating a readability score as a property of the text. C overcorrects into saying the wording does not matter, which the same study contradicts, since behaviour also changed with the text. D mistakes an estimate for a measurement."},{"q":"In a meeting, a senior engineer says plain writing would insult the specialists who read her team's reports. Which reply rests on evidence this lesson gave rather than on opinion?","options":["Specialists don't mind, because everybody secretly prefers simple words to complicated ones","Specialists shouldn't mind, since being understood matters more than anyone's feelings","Readers with higher literacy say they prefer plain English, because it's quicker to grasp","Specialists aren't the audience for plain writing, which exists for readers who struggle"],"answer":2,"explain":"GOV.UK's guidance reports research that people with higher levels of literacy prefer plain English because it lets them understand the information as quickly as possible, so C is the evidence-based reply. A asserts a universal preference nobody measured. B abandons the argument and appeals to duty instead. D concedes the engineer's premise, and it is the belief that keeps the myth alive."},{"q":"You're asked to name the audience for a school letter about a residential trip. Which answer does the job this lesson asks of you?","options":["Parents and carers of pupils in year six, since that is who the letter is addressed to","Parents who must decide whether their child goes, pay by a date, and know what to pack","Families across the town, because any of them might have a child at the school one day","The head teacher and governors, who decide whether the letter is good enough to send"],"answer":1,"explain":"Naming an audience is naming what they came needing, not naming a category, so B is the one that could change a sentence in the letter; it tells you what comes first, what comes second, and what can go. A and C are labels, and neither tells you what to put first. D names an approver rather than a reader, and writing for the approver instead of the reader is one of the commonest ways a document goes wrong."},{"q":"Two versions of the same 700-word page are tested. Version A scores better on a readability formula. Readers of version B answer more questions about it correctly. Which conclusion is best supported?","options":["Version A is clearer, since the formula measures clarity and readers are unreliable judges of it","The test is void, because the only trustworthy measure of writing is an expert's judgement","Neither version is clearer; the two results cancel out and nothing can be concluded","Version B is doing the job better, since being understood is what the formula only estimates"],"answer":3,"explain":"A formula counts surface features such as syllables and words per sentence and predicts comprehension; comprehension itself is the thing you actually wanted. When the estimate and the measurement disagree, the measurement wins, which is D. A trusts the proxy over the outcome. B dismisses reader evidence in favour of taste. C treats two different kinds of evidence as equal and opposite, which they are not."}]},"02-put-the-point-where-the-reader-is-looking":{"content":"<p>Read this paragraph. Don&#39;t skip it, and don&#39;t read ahead. Give it one honest pass, at normal speed.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Deal with everything in the space before you start, because afterwards it is far harder. Take out whatever will come out, and protect whatever will not. Attend to any irregularities early: they will be much more obvious at the end than they are now, and by then very little can be done about them. Always work downward. Going the other way means repeating what you have already finished. Use less material than feels right, since two light passes give a better result than one heavy one, which gathers and runs. Wait properly between passes, even though the waiting is the hardest part of it. When everything has settled, put back what you moved and clean what you used straight away, because a day later that is a much bigger job.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You understood every word. You couldn&#39;t tell anyone what it was about.</p>\n<div class=\"think predict\"><b>Predict first</b><p class=\"think-q\">Now read it again, knowing that the title is &quot;Painting a room&quot;. What changed?</p><details><summary>Show the answer</summary><p>Everything, and instantly. Taking out what will come out is the furniture. The irregularities are the cracks and nail holes you fill before you start, which are invisible now and glaring under fresh paint. Working downward is ceiling before walls, so you aren&#39;t dripping onto finished work. The two light passes are coats. The waiting is drying time. And cleaning what you used straight away is the brushes, which are easy tonight and ruined tomorrow.</p>\n<p>Nothing about the paragraph changed. Not one word was rewritten. The only thing that arrived was three words of context, and they arrived before the passage instead of after it. That is the whole of this lesson.</p>\n</details></div>\n\n<p>Every word in that paragraph was ordinary English. Every sentence was short. It scores 69 on the Flesch reading-ease scale, which is comfortably plain English, and it was still unreadable. That&#39;s the narrow thing a formula can&#39;t see: it counts syllables and sentence lengths, and the problem here was in neither.</p>\n<h2>Context first, then the thing it contextualises</h2>\n<p>Gopen and Swan state this as the sixth of their seven structural principles in <a href=\"https://wstomv.win.tue.nl/quotes/science-of-scientific-writing.html\">&quot;The Science of Scientific Writing&quot;</a>: <strong>&quot;In general, provide context for your reader before asking that reader to consider anything new.&quot;</strong></p>\n<p>Keep the &quot;In general&quot;. It&#39;s theirs, and they meant it: the same paper says <strong>&quot;None of these reader-expectation principles should be considered &#39;rules.&#39;&quot;</strong> We come back to that at the end of this lesson, and lesson 8 is about it.</p>\n<p>That sentence is doing more work than it looks. It isn&#39;t advice about being organised. It&#39;s a claim about what a reader is physically doing while they read you.</p>\n<p>The mechanism is this. A reader who doesn&#39;t yet know where a passage is going can&#39;t file anything. Every fact arrives and has to be held, unsorted, in case it turns out to matter. &quot;Attend to any irregularities early&quot; is a burden until the moment you know it&#39;s a room being painted, at which point it costs nothing at all, because it drops into a slot that was already there.</p>\n<p>So the cost of withholding context isn&#39;t that the reader is mildly annoyed. It&#39;s that you&#39;ve made them carry your whole document at once. And they won&#39;t do it for long. Lesson 1&#39;s finding was that a reader takes in about a fifth of an average page; the sharper version of the same measurement is that readers get through half the information only on pages of about 111 words or fewer. Spend those first hundred words giving them nothing to hold on to and you&#39;ve spent the part you could count on.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><b>Three words, not three paragraphs</b><p>Notice how little context that passage needed. Three words, before it. Worth remembering when you&#39;re tempted to write three paragraphs of background: the reader usually needs a frame, not a briefing. The frame is what lets them file everything that comes after. The briefing is what they have to hold.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Front-loading at three levels</h2>\n<p>Front-loading is the practical form of principle 6, and it applies at three scales. It&#39;s the same move each time, for the same reason.</p>\n<p><strong>The document.</strong> The reader should be able to state what this is and what it wants from them inside the first hundred words or so. In an email that means the request or the decision in the first sentence or two. In a report it means an opening that says what was found, not what was done.</p>\n<p><strong>The section.</strong> Each section should say early what it&#39;s for, and that&#39;s mostly a job for headings. <a href=\"https://guidance.publishing.service.gov.uk/writing-to-gov-uk-standards/writing-guidelines/clear-structure/\">GOV.UK&#39;s guidance on structure</a> asks for headings that are &quot;descriptive&quot; and &quot;frontloaded&quot;, tells writers to &quot;avoid generic headings like &#39;Introduction&#39;&quot;, and gives the same instruction for the body: &quot;Put the most important information first. The quicker you get to the point, the greater the chance your users will see the information you want them to.&quot; Lesson 7 takes headings properly. The principle is here: a section that opens with three sentences of preamble has recreated the whole problem in miniature.</p>\n<p><strong>The paragraph.</strong> A paragraph should usually declare its business in its first sentence. Not because of a rule about topic sentences, but because a reader who doesn&#39;t know what a paragraph is about is doing the painting-a-room paragraph again, at small scale, forty times a document.</p>\n<p>The pattern to notice is that the reason never changes. At every level, the question is whether the reader can file what comes next.</p>\n<h2>The email with five clauses of throat-clearing</h2>\n<p>This is the kind of thing that arrives every day.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Following our discussion last week and having reviewed the figures from Q2 as well as the projections that finance circulated on Monday, and taking into account the feedback from the regional teams who raised a number of concerns about capacity during the summer period, I wanted to reach out regarding the launch date and share some thoughts about how we might want to proceed given everything we now know.</p>\n<p>As you will remember, the regional teams have been flagging for some time that the summer months are difficult for them from a staffing point of view, and this year appears to be no different, with several of them noting that they expect to be running below their usual cover from late June through to the end of August.</p>\n<p>The Q2 figures, read alongside that, do seem to support what the regional teams are saying, in the sense that the support volumes we saw in Q2 were already at the upper end of what we had planned for. The projections finance circulated on Monday, while they do not really change the picture in either direction, at least do not contradict it.</p>\n<p>So on balance, and taking everything above together, I think the sensible course of action is probably for us to move the launch from May to July, unless there is something I have missed. Let me know what you think when you get a chance.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Two hundred and thirty-five words, and the decision arrives at word 211. A reader who gives this email the attention they give an average page has stopped somewhere in the second paragraph, and everything they read was preamble. They now know there was a discussion last week, that finance sent something, and that the regional teams have concerns. They don&#39;t know that anyone is proposing to move anything.</p>\n<p>Now the same email, front-loaded:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>We should move the launch from May to July. Here&#39;s why.</strong></p>\n<p>The regional teams expect to run below their usual cover from late June to the end of August, and the Q2 figures back them up: support volumes were already at the top of what we planned for, so we&#39;d be launching into our thinnest cover of the year. Finance&#39;s Monday projections don&#39;t change the case either way.</p>\n<p>If you want to keep May, the thing to solve is summer cover. Let me know by Thursday and I&#39;ll put options together.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Ninety-one words, and the decision is the first thing in it: it begins at word three and is complete by word nine. The whole email now fits inside the hundred words the original spent clearing its throat.</p>\n<div class=\"think predict\"><b>Predict first</b><p class=\"think-q\">What was actually cut between those two versions, and what was only moved?</p><details><summary>Show the answer</summary><p>A hundred and forty-four words were cut, and every one of them was hedging or throat-clearing. &quot;Following our discussion last week&quot; went, because the reader was at the discussion. &quot;I wanted to reach out&quot; went, because it describes the email rather than saying anything. &quot;As you will remember&quot;, &quot;on balance&quot;, &quot;taking everything above together&quot;, &quot;I think the sensible course of action is probably&quot; and &quot;unless there is something I have missed&quot; all went, and none of them carried information.</p>\n<p>Everything factual was moved, not cut. The regional teams, the summer cover, the Q2 figures, the finance projections and the launch date are all still there. What changed is that they now arrive after the reader knows what they&#39;re for. That&#39;s the difference between cutting and front-loading, and this course does both, in different lessons. The last paragraph also does something the original never did: it tells the reader what to do.</p>\n</details></div>\n\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 420 300\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:420px;display:block;margin:1.5em auto;font-family:system-ui,sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"frontload-title frontload-desc\">\n<title id=\"frontload-title\">Where the decision sits, in two versions of the same email</title>\n<desc id=\"frontload-desc\">Two vertical bars, each standing for one version of the email, read from the top down, drawn to the same scale in words. The version as written is 235 words tall and the band marking the decision sits near its foot, at word 211. The front-loaded version is 91 words tall and its decision band sits at the very top, at word 3. A dashed line crosses both at 111 words, the length at which readers get through half the information. The decision in the first version is far below that line; the whole of the second version is above it.</desc>\n<text x=\"8\" y=\"22\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"var(--text, #111418)\">Where the decision sits</text>\n<text x=\"8\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\">Same facts, different arrangement</text>\n<text x=\"90\" y=\"56\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\">as written</text>\n<text x=\"240\" y=\"56\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\">front-loaded</text>\n<rect x=\"90\" y=\"62\" width=\"90\" height=\"200\" fill=\"var(--navy-soft, #e8eef6)\" stroke=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<rect x=\"240\" y=\"62\" width=\"90\" height=\"77\" fill=\"var(--navy-soft, #e8eef6)\" stroke=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<rect x=\"90\" y=\"241\" width=\"90\" height=\"12\" fill=\"var(--oxblood, #8b1e2d)\"/>\n<rect x=\"240\" y=\"63\" width=\"90\" height=\"12\" fill=\"var(--oxblood, #8b1e2d)\"/>\n<line x1=\"20\" y1=\"156\" x2=\"400\" y2=\"156\" stroke=\"var(--gold, #b08d2a)\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 4\"/>\n<text x=\"8\" y=\"151\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"var(--gold, #b08d2a)\">111 words</text>\n<text x=\"186\" y=\"252\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"var(--oxblood, #8b1e2d)\">decision</text>\n<text x=\"336\" y=\"74\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"var(--oxblood, #8b1e2d)\">decision</text>\n<text x=\"90\" y=\"284\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\">they never reach it</text>\n<text x=\"240\" y=\"284\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\">they have it at once</text>\n</svg>\n\n<h2>The wrinkle: when the point should not come first</h2>\n<p>If this lesson stopped here you&#39;d have a rule, and Gopen and Swan&#39;s own caveat is that none of this should be taken as one. Front-loading is a default. There are cases where it&#39;s wrong, and they aren&#39;t rare.</p>\n<p><strong>Bad news that needs its reason to be understood.</strong> Take a letter refusing a benefit claim. Buried, it looks like this:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Thank you for your application of 4 March and for the documents you supplied. We have now completed our review of your circumstances, taking into account the information you provided about your household and your recent change of employment. Having considered all of this carefully, and applying the rules currently in force, we have concluded that your claim does not meet the qualifying conditions and has therefore been refused.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Front-loaded without its reason, it looks like this:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Your claim has been refused. You can appeal within one month.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is honest, and it&#39;s the sentence after which many readers stop reading and start reacting, which means they may never reach the part about appealing. The fix isn&#39;t to go back to burying it. It&#39;s to put the one reason that makes the decision intelligible next to the decision itself:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Your claim has been refused, because your savings are above the £16,000 limit. If that figure is wrong, you can appeal within one month and we will look again.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>One sentence of reason, not four paragraphs of context. The reader understands rather than only resents, and the deadline is still in the first thirty words.</p>\n<p><strong>An argument whose conclusion would be rejected on sight.</strong> If you&#39;re writing to someone who currently believes the opposite of your conclusion, opening with the conclusion invites them to spend the rest of the document arguing with you instead of reading it. Here the premises genuinely do come first, because the reader has to agree to each step before they&#39;ll accept where the steps lead. Notice this is still principle 6: you&#39;re providing the context that makes the conclusion intelligible before asking the reader to consider it.</p>\n<p><strong>Narrative, where the withholding is the point.</strong> A joke front-loaded is not a joke. Neither is a mystery. This course is about writing that has to be understood, not writing that has to be enjoyed, and the two have different rules. Worth knowing which one you&#39;re doing.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><b>What this course has and has not read on bad news</b><p>The three exceptions above are the drafter&#39;s working judgement, drawn from the principle rather than from a study. The claim that softening bad news with praise makes it worse, and the claim about arguing readers, are plausible and widely repeated, and this course has not read research on either. Treat them as reasoning you can check against your own readers, not as findings. If you know the literature here, the feedback form at the foot of this lesson is the place to say so.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"think checkpoint\"><b>Check yourself</b><p class=\"think-q\">Which of these four should be front-loaded, and what decides it in each case?</p><details><summary>Show the answer</summary><p><strong>A letter telling a tenant their rent is going up by 6% from June.</strong> Front-load. The reader needs the number and the date above everything else, and they&#39;ll be looking for exactly those two things. Withholding them isn&#39;t tact; it&#39;s making an anxious person hunt.</p>\n<p><strong>A proposal to a board that has rejected this idea twice before.</strong> Don&#39;t front-load the proposal itself. The deciding factor is that the reader arrives disagreeing, so the conclusion needs its premises to be intelligible. Front-load what has changed since the last rejection, which is the context that makes a third hearing reasonable.</p>\n<p><strong>A safety notice about a chemical spill procedure.</strong> Front-load, hard. The deciding factor is that this reader isn&#39;t reading, they&#39;re looking, under pressure, for one instruction. This is really a design problem, and lesson 7 takes it up.</p>\n<p><strong>An email to a colleague explaining why a shared deadline slipped.</strong> Genuinely a judgement call, and the deciding factor is what you want from them. If you want them to do something, front-load the ask. If the email exists so they aren&#39;t blindsided, the reason and the fact can arrive together, because there&#39;s no action to bury.</p>\n</details></div>\n\n<h2>What people get wrong</h2>\n<p><strong>&quot;The inverted pyramid is always right.&quot;</strong> Journalists put the most important information first, then detail, then background, and it works because a news reader may stop at any point. GOV.UK recommends the same shape for the same reason. It&#39;s one good instance of front-loading, not the definition of it. A document whose reader will finish it has different needs.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Front-loading means being blunt.&quot;</strong> The two are unrelated. &quot;We should move the launch from May to July, and here&#39;s why&quot; is front-loaded and perfectly courteous. Bluntness is about what you say of people; front-loading is about what order the reader meets things in.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;A summary at the end does the same job.&quot;</strong> It doesn&#39;t, and the reason is in this lesson&#39;s first paragraph. A summary at the end helps a reader who finished. Context at the start helps a reader read. The passage at the top of this lesson, with its title printed underneath instead of above, is still unreadable.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Front-loading means putting the conclusion first.&quot;</strong> Sometimes, and sometimes not. The principle underneath is context before the new thing. In an argument, the conclusion is the new thing and the premises are the context, which is why an unwelcome conclusion belongs after the reasons rather than before them.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;My document has an executive summary, so it&#39;s front-loaded.&quot;</strong> Only if the summary says what was found and what should happen. A great many executive summaries say what the document covers, which is a table of contents in prose and gives the reader nothing to file.</p>\n<h2>Practice</h2>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><b>Front-load three real documents, and find one that should not be</b><p>Take 30 minutes over this. Use the document you kept from lesson 1, plus two more you actually sent.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p>For each one, write down in a single sentence what the reader most needed to know. Not what the document is about. What they needed.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Find where that sentence currently appears, counted in words from the start. Write the number down.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Rewrite the opening so it arrives within the first hundred words, and preferably within the first 25. Move things; don&#39;t delete yet. Deleting is lesson 6, and doing both at once makes it impossible to tell which change helped.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Now the part that matters most. <strong>One of your three should be a case where you conclude front-loading is wrong.</strong> Find it, or find a fourth document where it is. Write down which of the three exceptions applies, and what you&#39;d put first instead.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Take the front-loaded version of one document and read only its first hundred words aloud, then stop. A reader who stopped there: would they do the right thing? That&#39;s what you&#39;re testing.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you can&#39;t find any document where front-loading is wrong, that&#39;s worth noticing too. It probably means everything you write is a request or a report, which is true of most working writers, and it means the default is safe for you.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Where this goes next</h2>\n<p>You now know where the point should go. You don&#39;t yet know how to write the sentence that carries it, and that&#39;s the harder problem: an email can be perfectly front-loaded and still be exhausting to read, because the trouble is inside the sentences rather than in their order.</p>\n<p>The next lesson goes down a level, to the single move that does more for a sentence than any other. Then lesson 5 comes back to arrangement, at the scale of the paragraph, and explains something this lesson has only asserted: why a passage can feel disconnected even when every sentence is in a sensible order.</p>\n<h2>Go deeper</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://wstomv.win.tue.nl/quotes/science-of-scientific-writing.html\">Gopen and Swan in full</a>. It&#39;s about scientific prose, but six of its seven principles apply to any writing, and the closing section on why they are not rules is the best short thing written on the subject.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://guidance.publishing.service.gov.uk/writing-to-gov-uk-standards/writing-guidelines/clear-structure/\">GOV.UK on structure</a>, which is front-loading written up as an operating standard for a whole government, with its heading rules and its own reading figures.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://digital.gov/guides/plain-language\">Digital.gov&#39;s plain language guides</a>, the American equivalent, which reach the same conclusions from a different drafting tradition.</li>\n<li>Bransford and Johnson&#39;s 1972 experiment, which did formally what the passage at the top of this lesson does to you. See the Sources note below on why this course links no copy of it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>George D. Gopen and Judith A. Swan, <a href=\"https://wstomv.win.tue.nl/quotes/science-of-scientific-writing.html\">&quot;The Science of Scientific Writing&quot;</a>, <em>American Scientist</em> 78 (1990). Principle 6, quoted in full with its &quot;In general&quot;, is the foundation of this lesson, and the paper&#39;s own statement that &quot;None of these reader-expectation principles should be considered &#39;rules.&#39;&quot; is quoted in the wrinkle section.</li>\n<li>GOV.UK, <a href=\"https://guidance.publishing.service.gov.uk/writing-to-gov-uk-standards/writing-guidelines/clear-structure/\">&quot;Create a clear structure for your content&quot;</a>. &quot;Put the most important information first&quot;, the inverted pyramid, and the requirement that headings be descriptive and front-loaded rather than generic.</li>\n<li>The reading figures are carried forward from lesson 1, where they come from Jakob Nielsen&#39;s <a href=\"https://www.nngroup.com/articles/how-little-do-users-read/\">&quot;How Little Do Users Read?&quot;</a> with his caveats: about a fifth of an average page, and half the information read only on pages of 111 words or fewer.</li>\n<li>The Flesch reading-ease score of 69 for the untitled passage was computed for this lesson.</li>\n<li>The three exceptions in the wrinkle section are reasoning from principle 6, not findings. The callout in that section says so.</li>\n<li>The untitled passage at the top of this lesson was written for this course, so that you could feel the effect on yourself rather than read about it. The demonstration is not original: a well-known 1972 experiment by Bransford and Johnson did the same thing formally with a passage about washing clothes, giving different groups its title before, after, or not at all, as secondary summaries describe it. The only freely available copies of that paper are scanned images, so this course has not read it, uses none of its text, and quotes no figure from it.</li>\n</ul>\n","quiz":[{"q":"An email opens with four sentences of background and puts the decision in the fifth. A colleague says this is fine because the decision is still in the first paragraph. What is the strongest objection?","options":["Decisions belong in a subject line, so no opening paragraph could ever be the right place for one","Paragraph position is the wrong unit; what matters is how many words the reader crosses first","Four sentences of background is always too many, whatever the document happens to be about","A first paragraph is read in full by almost nobody, so its contents make very little difference"],"answer":1,"explain":"A reader's attention is spent in words, not paragraphs, so \"in the first paragraph\" can still mean a hundred words in, past the point where half of them have stopped. B names that. A invents a rule about subject lines the lesson does not support. C fixes a number to a question that depends on the document. D overstates lesson 1, which found that readers take in about a fifth of a page, not that openings go unread."},{"q":"You must tell a supplier their contract will not be renewed. Which opening best fits what this lesson says about bad news?","options":["Give the decision in the first sentence, since front-loading is the rule and it applies here too","Open with praise for the work so far, then give the decision somewhere in the closing paragraph","Give the one reason that makes the decision intelligible, then the decision, in the first short paragraph","Avoid stating the decision at all, and let the absence of a renewal offer communicate it in time"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson treats bad news as the case where a reason has to arrive with the decision, so the reader understands it rather than only resenting it, which is C. A applies the default without noticing the exception this lesson exists to teach. B is the standard fudge, and it buries the one thing the reader needs. D is not a choice about arrangement; it is a refusal to write the letter."},{"q":"Which heading best does the job GOV.UK asks a heading to do?","options":["Background and context","Next steps and considerations","Update on the Q3 project","Why the launch moved to July"],"answer":3,"explain":"GOV.UK asks for headings that are descriptive and front-loaded, so a skimming reader can decide whether to stop without entering the section. D carries the information. A and B are the generic labels its guidance names directly. C names a topic but not what happened, so a reader still has to go in to find out whether it matters to them."},{"q":"A writer moves the conclusion of a long report from the end to the front, and readers say it is now harder to follow. What has most likely gone wrong?","options":["Moving the conclusion is always wrong in reports, because a report is an argument and arguments build","Nothing has gone wrong, and a reader's report of being confused is not evidence a writer should act on","The conclusion arrived before the context that makes it intelligible, so it landed on unprepared readers","The report was too long to begin with, and the length rather than the arrangement is the real problem"],"answer":2,"explain":"Front-loading is not the same as putting the last sentence first. The principle underneath it is context before the thing it contextualises, so a conclusion dropped in with no frame lands on a reader who cannot place it, which is C. A turns a default into a prohibition. B dismisses the only evidence available. D changes the subject to length, which the reader complaint does not point at."},{"q":"You are writing a paragraph inside a section a reader has already been reading for two minutes. Where should the paragraph's point go?","options":["First, because front-loading applies at the paragraph level as well as the document level","Last, because by this stage the reader is committed and will read the whole paragraph anyway","It does not matter within a paragraph, since front-loading is a document-level idea only","First if the paragraph runs long, last if it is short, since the reader's patience scales with length"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson applies front-loading at three levels, and the reason is the same at each, because a reader who does not know where a passage is going holds it all in suspense. A says that. B assumes commitment the reader has not given. C restricts the idea to one level the lesson explicitly extends. D invents a threshold; the reason to declare a paragraph's business early does not depend on how long it is."},{"q":"Which of these is the clearest sign that a document needs front-loading, rather than needing to be shorter?","options":["It runs to more than one page, which is the length at which readers reliably start to skim","Readers who finish it act correctly, but readers who stop halfway act on the wrong thing","It contains several long sentences, and long sentences are what makes a document hard to read","Readers say they enjoyed it, since enjoyment and comprehension pull against each other in prose"],"answer":1,"explain":"If finishing produces the right action and stopping early produces the wrong one, the content is doing its job and the order is not, which is exactly what front-loading fixes. That is B. A treats length as the diagnosis when the symptom has not been checked. C names a sentence-level issue that lesson 4 handles and that this evidence does not point to. D asserts a trade-off the lesson never claims."}]},"03-characters-and-actions":{"content":"<p>A letter about a planning application contained this sentence. It&#39;s grammatical, correctly spelled, and only twenty words long.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Determination of the application was undertaken following consideration of representations received during the consultation period by officers with delegated authority.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Read it twice and you can work out roughly what happened. That work you just did is the subject of this lesson, and the point is that you shouldn&#39;t have had to do it.</p>\n<p>Now the same event, in eleven words:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Officers with delegated authority decided the application after reading the responses.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>One thing did get dropped, and it&#39;s worth naming: the consultation period. Everything else is still there. The representations became &quot;responses&quot;, the delegated authority survived intact, and the determination became &quot;decided&quot;. If the period matters, put it back, and the sentence still runs to fourteen words: &quot;after reading the responses sent during the consultation&quot;. What changed isn&#39;t that anything was cut. It&#39;s that somebody is now doing something.</p>\n<h2>The move that does most of the work</h2>\n<p>Joseph Williams built his book <em>Style</em> around two principles, as everyone who has read it reports, including the sources this course has read. Gopen and Swan reach the same place from the reader&#39;s side in <a href=\"https://wstomv.win.tue.nl/quotes/science-of-scientific-writing.html\">their seven structural principles</a>, where they appear as numbers three and five. Put together they say:</p>\n<p><strong>Make the main character of your sentence its grammatical subject, and put that character&#39;s action in the verb.</strong></p>\n<p>Before you take that as a rule, take their own warning with it. The same paper says <strong>&quot;None of these reader-expectation principles should be considered &#39;rules.&#39;&quot;</strong> They&#39;re describing what readers expect, not issuing prohibitions, and a description of an expectation can be worth violating on purpose. Lesson 8 is about the difference, and this lesson&#39;s last section is about the cases where you should.</p>\n<p>A teacher of writers and two describers of readers landing in the same place is better evidence than either alone. Be careful how much you make of it, though: Gopen and Swan wrote nine years after Williams, and this course hasn&#39;t read Williams&#39;s own text to see whether he&#39;s behind them. What you can say is that the principle turns up independently of any one author&#39;s taste, which is more than most writing advice can claim.</p>\n<p>The mechanism is easy to state. A reader meets a sentence expecting a story, and a story needs somebody doing something. If your subject is a person or a thing that acts, and your verb is the acting, the reader gets the story for free. If not, they have to assemble it from the nouns, and they will, and it costs them.</p>\n<p>That&#39;s what happened to you in the planning sentence. &quot;Determination was undertaken&quot; gave you a subject that isn&#39;t a character and a verb that isn&#39;t the action. The real action, deciding, was sitting inside the noun <em>determination</em>. The real character, the officers, was parked at the very end behind the word <em>by</em>. You reconstructed the sentence in your head, and every sentence like it spends the reader&#39;s attention on assembly instead of on what you&#39;re telling them. That&#39;s the cost, and lesson 1 is about how little of it you have.</p>\n<h2>Diagnose before you rewrite</h2>\n<p>Almost everyone starts rewriting straight away, shuffling words until it sounds better. That produces an improvement you can&#39;t repeat.</p>\n<p>Do it in two steps instead, and write the first step down.</p>\n<p><strong>Step one, name the character and the action.</strong> Who or what is doing something, and what are they doing? Answer in ordinary words, not in the sentence&#39;s own vocabulary.</p>\n<p><strong>Step two, put them in the slots.</strong> Character becomes the subject. Action becomes the verb.</p>\n<p>Diagnose these five before rewriting any of them.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>Character</th>\n<th>Action</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Determination of the application was undertaken by officers.</td>\n<td>the officers</td>\n<td>deciding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>There was a failure of communication between the two departments.</td>\n<td>the two departments</td>\n<td>not telling each other</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The implementation of the new policy will take place in April.</td>\n<td>somebody unnamed</td>\n<td>starting the policy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>An assessment of the risks has been carried out.</td>\n<td>somebody unnamed</td>\n<td>assessing the risks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Our expectation is that completion of the works will occur by June.</td>\n<td>we, and the builders</td>\n<td>expecting, and finishing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Look at the third and fourth rows. The character came out as &quot;somebody unnamed&quot;, and that isn&#39;t a failure of the diagnosis. It <em>is</em> the diagnosis. Those sentences don&#39;t say who is implementing or who assessed, and you can&#39;t put a character in the subject slot until you decide who it is. That&#39;s the whole point of the harder example below.</p>\n<div class=\"think predict\"><b>Predict first</b><p class=\"think-q\">Diagnose this one before you rewrite it. &quot;A reduction in waiting times was achieved through the introduction of a new booking system.&quot; Character? Action?</p><details><summary>Show the answer</summary><p>The character is whoever runs the service, which the sentence never names. There are two actions, and both are buried in nouns. <em>Reduction</em> is really &quot;cut&quot; or &quot;brought down&quot;, and <em>introduction</em> is really &quot;brought in&quot; or &quot;started using&quot;.</p>\n<p>So a rewrite has to answer a question the original ducked. If it&#39;s the hospital, then: &quot;The hospital cut waiting times by bringing in a new booking system.&quot; If nobody knows who, the honest version says so, or names the service rather than a person: &quot;Waiting times fell after the new booking system started.&quot; Notice the second version drops the claim that the booking system <em>caused</em> the fall, which the original asserted with the word <em>achieved</em>. That isn&#39;t a side effect of tidying. Undoing a buried action often exposes a causal claim that was resting on nothing.</p>\n</details></div>\n\n<h2>Nominalisation, and why the -ion rule is only a detector</h2>\n<p>The noun that swallowed the verb has a name. A <strong>nominalisation</strong> is a verb or an adjective turned into a noun: <em>decide</em> becomes <em>decision</em>, <em>fail</em> becomes <em>failure</em>, <em>implement</em> becomes <em>implementation</em>, <em>reduce</em> becomes <em>reduction</em>.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://guidance.publishing.service.gov.uk/writing-to-gov-uk-standards/writing-guidelines/clear-language/\">GOV.UK&#39;s guidance</a> gives working writers a cheap detector for these. It says to watch words ending in <em>-ion</em> and <em>-ment</em>, because they &quot;tend to make sentences longer and more complicated&quot;.</p>\n<p>Take that for exactly what it is. It&#39;s a place to look, not a verdict. <em>Government</em>, <em>equipment</em>, <em>condition</em> and <em>region</em> all end that way and none of them is a buried verb. And plenty of nominalisations end some other way: <em>failure</em>, <em>analysis</em>, <em>growth</em>, <em>belief</em>, <em>refusal</em>. The ending finds you candidates faster than reading can, which is why it&#39;s worth knowing. Then you check each one by asking whether there&#39;s a verb hiding in it and a person who should be doing it.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><b>The quick sweep</b><p>Search a document for &quot;tion&quot; and for &quot;ment&quot;, and read each hit. Most will be innocent. The ones that aren&#39;t will usually be sitting next to a limp verb, because a buried action leaves the verb slot with nothing to do, and something has to fill it. Watch for <em>was made</em>, <em>was undertaken</em>, <em>took place</em>, <em>occurred</em>, <em>was carried out</em>, <em>there was</em>, and plain <em>is</em>. Those verbs are the fingerprints.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>The pair that is not about the passive</h2>\n<p>Slow down for this one, because it corrects something almost everybody has been taught.</p>\n<p>In 1918, William Strunk wrote in <a href=\"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/37134/pg37134.txt\"><em>The Elements of Style</em></a> that &quot;a common fault is to use as the subject of a passive construction a noun which expresses the entire action, leaving to the verb no function beyond that of completing the sentence.&quot; His example pair:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A survey of this region was made in 1900.</p>\n<p>This region was surveyed in 1900.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>He prefers the second. Now look carefully at both.</p>\n<div class=\"think predict\"><b>Predict first</b><p class=\"think-q\">What is the difference between those two sentences? Be precise before you open this.</p><details><summary>Show the answer</summary><p><strong>Both of them are passive.</strong> &quot;Was made&quot; is passive; &quot;was surveyed&quot; is passive. Neither names who did the surveying.</p>\n<p>So whatever Strunk was objecting to, it was not the passive voice, even though this passage sits inside the rule headed &quot;Use the active voice&quot;. What changed between the two versions is that <em>survey</em> stopped being a noun and became the verb. The action moved into the verb slot, and the limp <em>was made</em> disappeared because it was only ever there to hold the sentence together.</p>\n<p>His other pair makes the same point: &quot;Mobilization of the army was rapidly effected&quot; against &quot;The army was rapidly mobilized&quot;. Both passive. One has its action in a verb.</p>\n</details></div>\n\n<p>That&#39;s a useful thing to carry, and lesson 8 comes back to it properly. A great deal of advice that arrives labelled &quot;avoid the passive&quot; is really about buried actions, and the two get confused because they often show up together. They&#39;re separate problems with separate fixes, and if you only know the passive rule you&#39;ll fix the wrong one.</p>\n<p>Be fair to the other side while you&#39;re here. The same GOV.UK page this lesson took its detector from does tell writers to &quot;use the active voice&quot;, and it gives a reason: the active &quot;is more direct and puts the focus on the user&quot;. That&#39;s a defensible position and it isn&#39;t what this section is arguing against. The claim here is narrower, that the <em>particular</em> fault Strunk named is about buried actions rather than about the passive, and that treating the two as one thing makes you fix the wrong one. Whether &quot;avoid the passive&quot; survives contact with the evidence as a general rule is lesson 8&#39;s question.</p>\n<h2>The harder example: when the fix forces a decision</h2>\n<p>Reports about things that went wrong are full of sentences like this.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Following identification of the discrepancy, escalation was delayed pending clarification of the reporting requirements.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Three nominalisations, no character anywhere. Diagnose it and every row of the table comes out &quot;somebody unnamed&quot;.</p>\n<p>Try to rewrite it and you hit a wall at once, because you can&#39;t write &quot;X delayed escalating&quot; without knowing who X is. The sentence isn&#39;t merely badly arranged. <strong>It&#39;s arranged so that the question doesn&#39;t come up.</strong></p>\n<p>Sometimes that&#39;s carelessness and sometimes it isn&#39;t. A sentence with no character can&#39;t be checked against anyone, and the version that names one can. Compare:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The duty manager knew about the discrepancy on Tuesday and did not escalate it until Friday, because the reporting rules were unclear.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Same events. Now there&#39;s a person, two dates, and a reason. Every one of those is checkable, and one of them may be wrong, which is exactly what the original protected.</p>\n<div class=\"think checkpoint\"><b>Check yourself</b><p class=\"think-q\">You rewrite a sentence to name who acted, and you&#39;re asked to put it back the way it was. What should you conclude, and what should you do?</p><details><summary>Show the answer</summary><p>Conclude that the vagueness was probably doing work. Not necessarily dishonest work. Sometimes an organisation genuinely hasn&#39;t established who acted, and naming a person prematurely would be worse than saying nothing. Sometimes a document is deliberately written so that no individual is exposed, and that&#39;s a decision above your pay grade rather than a writing error.</p>\n<p>What to do is ask which it is, in those words. &quot;Do we not know who, or do we know and not want to say?&quot; The first has an honest fix, which is to write that it hasn&#39;t been established. The second isn&#39;t a writing problem and you should stop treating it as one.</p>\n<p>What you shouldn&#39;t do is quietly restore the original and file it. You&#39;ve found the one sentence in the document that somebody cares about.</p>\n</details></div>\n\n<h2>When to leave it alone</h2>\n<p>Undoing every nominalisation is a worse rule than the one it replaces, and it&#39;s the commonest overcorrection once this idea lands.</p>\n<p><strong>When the abstraction is genuinely the subject.</strong> &quot;Inflation fell to three percent&quot; is a sentence about inflation. Rewriting it as &quot;prices rose more slowly&quot; changes what the sentence is about. If your topic really is the decision, the failure or the reduction, then the noun belongs in the subject slot, and lesson 5 will show you the deeper reason why.</p>\n<p><strong>When the noun is a fixed term.</strong> <em>Planning permission</em>, <em>risk assessment</em>, <em>performance review</em> are names of things. Unpacking them into verbs produces a sentence that&#39;s technically clearer and unrecognisable to the people who have to act on it.</p>\n<p><strong>When it links two sentences.</strong> If you&#39;ve just written about the committee deciding, then opening the next sentence with &quot;That decision...&quot; puts old information first, which is doing a job. Lesson 5 is entirely about this, and it&#39;s the commonest reason a nominalisation earns its place.</p>\n<p><strong>When naming the character would be false precision.</strong> If it genuinely isn&#39;t known who assessed the risks, &quot;somebody assessed the risks&quot; invents a certainty the writer doesn&#39;t have. Write that it isn&#39;t recorded.</p>\n<h2>What people get wrong</h2>\n<p><strong>&quot;Nominalisations are bad.&quot;</strong> They&#39;re a normal and necessary part of English. The problem isn&#39;t the noun; it&#39;s a sentence whose action has left the verb, leaving <em>was made</em> or <em>took place</em> behind. Diagnose before you judge.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;This is a rule about words ending in -ion.&quot;</strong> That&#39;s a detector with a good hit rate and plenty of false positives, both ways. The test is whether there&#39;s an action buried and a character displaced.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Passive voice is the real problem here.&quot;</strong> Strunk&#39;s own pair disproves it, since both his sentences are passive and he preferred one of them. Buried actions and the passive are different faults that travel together.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Rewriting is the first step.&quot;</strong> Rewriting is the second step. Naming the character and the action in ordinary words is the first, and skipping it is why people&#39;s rewrites are inconsistent.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Being concrete means using shorter words.&quot;</strong> Concreteness is about whether a reader can picture who is doing what. &quot;Utilise&quot; for &quot;use&quot; is a vocabulary problem and a small one. &quot;Determination was undertaken&quot; is a structural problem and a large one, and every word in it is short.</p>\n<h2>Practice</h2>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><b>Diagnose ten, rewrite five, then find three of your own</b><p>Allow 50 minutes for this. Do the diagnosis in writing: the temptation is to fix these in your head, and if you do, you won&#39;t be able to repeat it on your own writing next week.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p>Make two columns, character and action, and diagnose all ten of these. Ordinary words only.</p>\n<p>(a) There was considerable disagreement among the trustees regarding the proposal.</p>\n<p>(b) Notification of the change was not received by residents until March.</p>\n<p>(c) An investigation into the cause of the outage is currently ongoing.</p>\n<p>(d) The provision of support to new members is the responsibility of the secretary.</p>\n<p>(e) Improvement in the results was observed after the intervention.</p>\n<p>(f) Inflation reached its highest level since 1982.</p>\n<p>(g) A decision on the appeal will be made in due course.</p>\n<p>(h) Cancellation of the service occurred due to a shortage of drivers.</p>\n<p>(i) The risk assessment was completed prior to commencement of the works.</p>\n<p>(j) Our recommendation is that consideration be given to a review of the policy.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Two of the ten should be left mostly as they are, and one of those two is only half a case. Find both, say which half, and write one line each saying why.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Rewrite five of the remaining eight, character in the subject, action in the verb. For any where the character isn&#39;t stated, write what you&#39;d need to find out before you could rewrite it honestly.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Now take the document you&#39;ve been carrying since lesson 1. Search it for &quot;tion&quot;, for &quot;ment&quot;, and for the limp verbs listed in the callout above. Find three sentences with a buried action. Diagnose each in writing, then rewrite it.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Read your three rewrites next to the originals, and check one thing. Did you change what the sentence claims? If a rewrite says something the original didn&#39;t, either the original was hiding something, which is worth noting, or you&#39;ve gone too far.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"think checkpoint\"><b>Check yourself</b><p class=\"think-q\">Which two did you mark to leave, and did you spot the one that is only half a case?</p><details><summary>Show the answer</summary><p>The two to leave are (f) and (i).</p>\n<p><strong>(f) Inflation reached its highest level since 1982.</strong> The abstraction is the subject of the sentence and the verb already carries an action. Rewriting it into &quot;prices rose faster than at any time since 1982&quot; isn&#39;t wrong, but it changes the topic from inflation to prices, and if the document is about inflation that&#39;s a loss.</p>\n<p><strong>(i) The risk assessment was completed prior to commencement of the works.</strong> This is the half case, and it&#39;s meant to be. <em>Risk assessment</em> is a fixed term and should stay. <em>Commencement</em> isn&#39;t, and &quot;before the works started&quot; is plainly better. If you marked the whole sentence for rewriting, or left the whole thing alone, reread it and find the seam.</p>\n<p>If you picked (g) as one to leave, that&#39;s a defensible reading, since &quot;a decision will be made&quot; is a fixed formula. But notice it&#39;s the sentence in the list that most conspicuously refuses to say who decides, which is the harder example again.</p>\n</details></div>\n\n<h2>Where this goes next</h2>\n<p>You now have the move that does most of the work at the sentence level. The next lesson is its companion and takes about half the time. Once you&#39;ve got the character into the subject slot, the reader wants the verb almost immediately, and a great many otherwise well-built sentences fail on that one point.</p>\n<p>Then lesson 5 goes underneath both of them, and answers the question this lesson kept deferring. Why does an abstraction sometimes belong in the subject after all, and why does a paragraph of good sentences sometimes read as a list of unrelated facts?</p>\n<h2>Go deeper</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://wstomv.win.tue.nl/quotes/science-of-scientific-writing.html\">Gopen and Swan in full</a>. Principles three and five are this lesson; the other five are the rest of the course, and the closing section on why none of them are rules is worth more than most books on writing.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/37134/pg37134.txt\">Strunk&#39;s <em>The Elements of Style</em></a> at Project Gutenberg, public domain. Rule 10 is two pages and contains all three pairs, including the one in this lesson&#39;s quiz. Lesson 8 comes back to what the rest of the book is worth.</li>\n<li>Joseph M. Williams, <em>Style: Lessons in Clarity and Grace</em>, Part Two, Clarity. The book this principle is usually credited to, and the one this course has not read. If you read one thing beyond this course on sentences, read that part.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://guidance.publishing.service.gov.uk/writing-to-gov-uk-standards/writing-guidelines/clear-language/\">GOV.UK on clear language</a>, where the detector comes from, alongside a dozen other rules written for people who have to get it right at scale.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Joseph M. Williams, <em>Style: Lessons in Clarity and Grace</em>, Part Two, Clarity, on making central characters the subjects of verbs and using verbs to name their actions. <strong>This course has read the book&#39;s structure and its two central principles as consistently reported by others, not the text itself</strong>, which is why no wording is quoted from it and no lesson number is cited.</li>\n<li>George D. Gopen and Judith A. Swan, <a href=\"https://wstomv.win.tue.nl/quotes/science-of-scientific-writing.html\">&quot;The Science of Scientific Writing&quot;</a>, <em>American Scientist</em> 78 (1990). Principle three, place the person or thing whose story a sentence is telling at the beginning, and principle five, articulate the action of every clause or sentence in its verb.</li>\n<li>William Strunk Jr., <a href=\"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/37134/pg37134.txt\"><em>The Elements of Style</em></a> (1918), rule 10, for the survey and mobilization pairs and for the sentence naming the fault, &quot;a common fault is to use as the subject of a passive construction a noun which expresses the entire action&quot;.</li>\n<li>GOV.UK, <a href=\"https://guidance.publishing.service.gov.uk/writing-to-gov-uk-standards/writing-guidelines/clear-language/\">&quot;Use clear language&quot;</a>, for the practical detector, that words ending in <em>-ion</em> and <em>-ment</em> &quot;tend to make sentences longer and more complicated&quot;. The same page tells writers to &quot;use the active voice&quot; because it &quot;is more direct and puts the focus on the user&quot;, which this lesson quotes rather than omits, since the passive section argues a narrower point than that guidance does.</li>\n</ul>\n","quiz":[{"q":"A report says, \"Consideration was given by the committee to a postponement of the vote.\" What are the character and the action?","options":["The character is consideration and the action is giving, since those are the subject and the verb","The character is the vote and the action is postponement, because they carry the real content","The character is the committee and the action is considering, though neither is in the right slot","There is no character, because the sentence is passive and passives have no one performing them"],"answer":2,"explain":"Ask who is doing something. The committee is, and what it did was consider. That is C. A names the grammatical subject and verb, which is exactly the mismatch the lesson is about, since the sentence's subject is not its character. B picks the buried action of the wrong clause. D confuses the passive with having no agent, and the agent is named here in the words \"by the committee\"."},{"q":"Which rewrite of that sentence best follows this lesson?","options":["The committee considered postponing the vote","A postponement of the vote was considered by the committee","Consideration of a vote postponement occurred in committee","The vote's postponement was considered"],"answer":0,"explain":"A puts the character in the subject slot and both actions in verbs, considered and postponing, which is the move the lesson teaches. B keeps the action in a noun and only rearranges. C buries the action twice over and adds occurred, a verb that names nothing. D is the shortest of the four and the second worst, because cutting words has removed the character entirely and left the action in a noun, which is what the lesson warns against when it says concision is a different move."},{"q":"A guidance page says to watch for words ending in -ion and -ment. A colleague concludes that every such word should be removed. What is wrong with that?","options":["Nothing is wrong, since those endings reliably mark the words that make writing hard to follow","The endings are the wrong ones to watch, and -ance and -ity are the endings that actually matter","Removing them is right in principle but impossible in practice, because English has too many of them","The endings are a detector for buried actions, and plenty of such words name things rather than actions"],"answer":3,"explain":"Government, condition and equipment end that way and name things, not buried actions, so the ending is a place to look rather than a verdict. That is D. A treats a rule of thumb as a definition. B swaps one list of endings for another and keeps the same mistake. C accepts the rule and pleads difficulty, which concedes the wrong point."},{"q":"Which of these is the best reason to leave a nominalisation alone?","options":["The sentence would get longer if it were rewritten, and shorter sentences are always preferable","The abstraction itself is what the sentence is about, so it belongs in the subject position","Undoing it would require naming who acted, and that information is unavailable to the writer","Formal documents call for formal wording, and nominalisations are what makes writing sound formal"],"answer":1,"explain":"When the idea is genuinely the topic, as in a sentence about inflation or about the postponement rather than about the committee, the noun belongs in the subject slot and the lesson says to leave it. That is B. A treats length as the criterion, which this lesson never does. C is a real situation but the lesson treats it as a reason to find out, not a licence. D mistakes a register for a virtue."},{"q":"Strunk gives a third pair this lesson has not shown you: \"Confirmation of these reports cannot be obtained\" against his preferred \"These reports cannot be confirmed\". What is the improvement?","options":["The preferred version is active, and the first one is passive, which is the change rule 10 asks for","The preferred version drops a word ending in -ion, and that ending is what the guidance warns about","The action has moved out of a noun and into the verb, and both versions are still passive throughout","The preferred version is shorter, and Strunk's objection in rule 10 is at bottom about economy"],"answer":2,"explain":"Read both aloud and ask who can obtain and who can confirm. Neither sentence says, and both are passive, so the passive is not what changed. What changed is that confirmation stopped being a noun and became the verb confirmed, which is C. A asserts a difference that is not there. B notices the ending, which is only a detector, and the lesson's whole point is that the ending is not the fault. D names a real side effect and mistakes it for the reason."},{"q":"A school newsletter says, \"Provision of after-school clubs will be made available to all year groups from September.\" A parent emails to ask who will actually be running them. What has the parent's question exposed?","options":["The sentence never names a character, and the parent has put her finger on what it omits","The sentence is simply too long, and cutting it down would have answered her question","Nothing worth acting on, since provision is a fixed term in schools and should be left","The passive voice, which is the construction that made the sentence hard for her to follow"],"answer":0,"explain":"Diagnose it and the character column comes out \"somebody unnamed\", which is the whole of the parent's question, so A is right. B treats length as the fault when a shorter version could hide the actor just as well. C invokes the leave-it-alone rule, but provision here is a buried verb with somebody behind it, not a fixed term like risk assessment. D blames the passive, and the lesson's Strunk pairs show a passive sentence can name its actor and an active one can hide it."}]},"04-keep-the-subject-next-to-the-verb":{"content":"<p>Read this sentence once, at normal speed, and notice what your mind does.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The application, which had been submitted in March, revised twice at the request of the highways officer, and held while the flood risk assessment was recommissioned, was refused.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You met the subject in the second word. Then you waited. Twenty-four words later the sentence finally told you what the application did, and by then you had been holding &quot;the application&quot; in your head, unresolved, through three separate pieces of history.</p>\n<p>Now the same content, same words, rearranged:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The application was refused. It had been submitted in March, revised twice at the highways officer&#39;s request, and held while the flood risk assessment was recommissioned.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Nothing has gone. The March date, the two revisions, the highways officer and the flood risk assessment are all still there. You just got the verb four words in instead of twenty-eight.</p>\n<h2>What the reader is doing while they wait</h2>\n<p>Gopen and Swan state this as the first of their <a href=\"https://wstomv.win.tue.nl/quotes/science-of-scientific-writing.html\">seven structural principles</a>, and their wording is worth having exactly:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&quot;Readers expect a grammatical subject to be followed immediately by the verb. Anything of length that intervenes between subject and verb is read as an interruption, and therefore as something of lesser importance.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Take the second half of that sentence with the first. They aren&#39;t only saying the interruption costs the reader something. They&#39;re saying the reader discounts it, files it as a footnote to the main event. That&#39;s worth knowing before you put twenty words of your best material in there, and it&#39;s why the three fixes below aren&#39;t interchangeable.</p>\n<p>And take their caveat with the principle, as this course does every time it quotes them: <strong>&quot;None of these reader-expectation principles should be considered &#39;rules.&#39;&quot;</strong> What follows is a description of what readers do, not a prohibition.</p>\n<p>The mechanism is the same one from lesson 2, at a smaller scale. A subject on its own is an unfinished thought. The reader can&#39;t do anything with &quot;the application&quot; until they know what happened to it, so they hold it, and everything arriving before the verb has to be held alongside it, because it might turn out to matter to whatever the verb says.</p>\n<p>That&#39;s why the cost isn&#39;t flat. One or two words of interruption cost almost nothing. Twenty-four words cost a great deal, and they cost it on top of the ordinary work of reading twenty-four words. The reader is doing two jobs at once, and one of them is bookkeeping. That rising cost is this course&#39;s reasoning from Gopen and Swan&#39;s &quot;interruption&quot;, not a measured curve from a study; take it as a way of thinking about the gap rather than as a finding.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><b>This is the other half of lesson 3</b><p>Lesson 3 got the right character into the subject slot. This lesson is about what happens next. If you move the character to the front and then make the reader wait thirty words for the verb, you&#39;ve spent the gain. The two moves are halves of one move, and it&#39;s worth checking both at the same time.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Measure it, do not feel it</h2>\n<p>You can&#39;t do this by ear on your own writing, and here&#39;s the reason.</p>\n<p>You already know what your sentence means. When you reread &quot;The application, which had been submitted in March...&quot; your mind supplies &quot;was refused&quot; before you get there, because you wrote it. The wait costs you nothing, so you can&#39;t feel it. That&#39;s the same problem lesson 1 raised about rereading your own work, and it has the same answer: replace the judgement with a count.</p>\n<p><strong>The count.</strong> Find the main subject. Find its verb. Count the words in between. That number is the size of the wait you&#39;re asking for.</p>\n<p>The chart below draws that count for the sentence at the top of this lesson, in both versions.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><b>There is no threshold, and here is the honest version of one</b><p>Nobody has measured a cutoff, and anyone who gives you one is selling something. What this course can offer is the drafter&#39;s working rule of thumb, reasoned from the principle rather than drawn from a study: under about five words the gap is invisible, five to ten is usually fine, ten to twenty is where readers start to notice, and past twenty you&#39;re asking for real work.</p>\n<p>Treat those as a way to sort your own sentences, not as findings. The number that matters isn&#39;t the boundary. It&#39;s that you now have a figure for each sentence, so you can rank them and fix the worst three instead of rewriting everything.</p>\n</div>\n\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 420 210\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:420px;display:block;margin:1.5em auto;font-family:system-ui,sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"gap-title gap-desc\">\n<title id=\"gap-title\">The wait between a subject and its verb, in two versions of one sentence</title>\n<desc id=\"gap-desc\">Two horizontal bars stand for the same sentence about a refused planning application. In the version as written, the subject sits near the left end and the verb sits far to the right, with a long shaded stretch of twenty four words between them that the reader must hold. In the rewritten version the verb follows the subject almost immediately, and the same twenty four words now sit after the verb, where the reader can take them one at a time.</desc>\n<text x=\"8\" y=\"20\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"var(--text, #111418)\">Same words. The wait is what changed.</text>\n<text x=\"8\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\">as written</text>\n<line x1=\"30\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"400\" y2=\"70\" stroke=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n<rect x=\"55\" y=\"62\" width=\"265\" height=\"16\" fill=\"var(--navy-soft, #e8eef6)\" stroke=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<circle cx=\"45\" cy=\"70\" r=\"6\" fill=\"var(--navy, #0f2a4a)\"/>\n<circle cx=\"332\" cy=\"70\" r=\"6\" fill=\"var(--oxblood, #8b1e2d)\"/>\n<text x=\"26\" y=\"98\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"var(--navy, #0f2a4a)\">subject</text>\n<text x=\"95\" y=\"98\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\">24 words the reader holds</text>\n<text x=\"312\" y=\"98\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"var(--oxblood, #8b1e2d)\">verb</text>\n<text x=\"8\" y=\"136\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\">rewritten</text>\n<line x1=\"30\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"400\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n<rect x=\"105\" y=\"152\" width=\"265\" height=\"16\" fill=\"var(--navy-soft, #e8eef6)\" stroke=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<circle cx=\"45\" cy=\"160\" r=\"6\" fill=\"var(--navy, #0f2a4a)\"/>\n<circle cx=\"90\" cy=\"160\" r=\"6\" fill=\"var(--oxblood, #8b1e2d)\"/>\n<text x=\"26\" y=\"188\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"var(--navy, #0f2a4a)\">subject</text>\n<text x=\"95\" y=\"188\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"var(--oxblood, #8b1e2d)\">verb</text>\n<text x=\"140\" y=\"188\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\">the same 24 words, one at a time</text>\n</svg>\n\n<h2>One sentence, three fixes, three meanings</h2>\n<p>Take this, from the sort of email that arrives on a Friday afternoon.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The new supplier, who was selected after a procurement exercise that ran for most of last year and involved eleven bidders, has missed its first delivery date.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<div class=\"think predict\"><b>Predict first</b><p class=\"think-q\">Before you read the three fixes: name them. How many ways are there to close a gap like this, and what are they?</p><details><summary>Show the answer</summary><p>Three, and you can derive them from the shape of the problem rather than remembering a list. The interruption is in the wrong place, so either it moves or the sentence does. It can go to the front, it can go to the end, or the sentence can split in two and the interruption becomes a sentence of its own.</p>\n<p>That&#39;s the whole set. What the next three sections add isn&#39;t a fourth option. It&#39;s that the three are not interchangeable, because each one leaves something different sitting at the end of the sentence.</p>\n</details></div>\n\n<p>Subject: the new supplier. Verb: has missed. Gap: eighteen words. Count them, because that&#39;s the method and it&#39;s worth doing once on a sentence somebody else wrote.</p>\n<p>There are three ways to close it, and they aren&#39;t interchangeable. Each one puts something different at the end of the sentence, and the end of a sentence is where a reader puts their weight. That&#39;s Gopen and Swan&#39;s second clause arriving in practice: whatever sits in the interruption gets read as the lesser thing, so which material you move is a decision about what the sentence is for.</p>\n<p><strong>Fix one, move the interruption to the front.</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>After a procurement exercise that ran for most of last year and involved eleven bidders, the new supplier has missed its first delivery date.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The gap is gone. What lands last is the missed delivery, so this version is about the failure. The procurement exercise has become scene-setting.</p>\n<p><strong>Fix two, move the interruption to the end.</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The new supplier has missed its first delivery date, after a procurement exercise that ran for most of last year and involved eleven bidders.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Also gone, but now what lands last is the year-long exercise with eleven bidders. This version is quietly about the waste, and a reader will finish it thinking about the procurement rather than the delivery.</p>\n<p><strong>Fix three, split it.</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The new supplier has missed its first delivery date. It was selected after a procurement exercise that ran for most of last year and involved eleven bidders.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Two sentences, two stress positions, and both facts land. This is usually the safest choice when both halves matter, and it is the one most writers reach for last.</p>\n<div class=\"think predict\"><b>Predict first</b><p class=\"think-q\">You are writing to the procurement team to ask what went wrong with the process. Which fix do you use, and why?</p><details><summary>Show the answer</summary><p>Fix two. You want the reader to finish the sentence thinking about the procurement exercise, because that is what your email is about, and the end of the sentence is where they will place their weight.</p>\n<p>If you were writing to the operations team about a supply problem, you would use fix one, so the missed delivery lands. If you were writing a report that has to record both fairly, you would split it.</p>\n<p>Notice that the question &quot;which fix?&quot; cannot be answered by looking at the sentence. It depends on what you want the reader to walk away with, which is a question about the document. That is the idea lesson 5 is built on, and this is the first place it bites.</p>\n</details></div>\n\n<h2>The wrinkle: length is a proxy, not the fault</h2>\n<p>Two sentences. One is thirty-six words and easy. One is eighteen words and hard.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Thirty-six words.</strong> The council agreed to fund the repairs, which had been costed at ninety thousand pounds by an independent surveyor, and asked the housing team to begin work in the spring, before the next round of inspections.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Eighteen words.</strong> The decision, given the circumstances and the timescale involved and the other matters raised at the meeting, stands.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The long one has its subject and verb together at the front, twice (&quot;the council agreed&quot;, &quot;asked&quot;). Everything after that arrives in order, and the reader can take it a clause at a time without holding anything.</p>\n<p>The short one makes you carry &quot;the decision&quot; through fifteen words of vagueness to reach &quot;stands&quot;. It&#39;s half the length and considerably harder.</p>\n<p>This matters because sentence length is the thing people measure, and it is a proxy. <a href=\"https://guidance.publishing.service.gov.uk/writing-to-gov-uk-standards/writing-guidelines/clear-language/\">GOV.UK&#39;s guidance</a> says to &quot;try to split up sentences that are over 25 words long&quot;, which is sensible advice precisely because it is a rule of thumb about where to look. Long sentences are where big gaps tend to live. But splitting a long sentence with no gap gains nothing, and a short sentence with a gap will pass the length check untouched.</p>\n<p>This is the same lesson as the readability formulas in lesson 1, arriving at a smaller scale. The countable thing is a signal. The thing you actually care about is what the reader has to hold.</p>\n<div class=\"think checkpoint\"><b>Check yourself</b><p class=\"think-q\">When does an interruption earn its place? Decide for each, then open this.</p><details><summary>Show the answer</summary><p><strong>&quot;The report, dated 4 March, recommends closure.&quot;</strong> Earns it. Two words, and they tell the reader which report, which is something they need before the verb can mean anything.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;The application, which had been the subject of considerable correspondence with the parish council over the preceding eighteen months, was refused.&quot;</strong> Does not earn it. The correspondence is background, and the reader can have it after they know the outcome.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;The tenant, not the landlord, is responsible for the internal decoration.&quot;</strong> Earns it. Three words, and they are doing the most important work in the sentence, which is ruling out the thing the reader probably assumed.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Mrs Okafor, who has lived at the property since 2011 and has never previously been in arrears, has now missed two payments.&quot;</strong> A judgement call. The clause is long enough to cost something, and it is also the whole reason the sentence is worth writing, since the point is that this is out of character. Splitting it would work: &quot;Mrs Okafor has missed two payments. She has lived at the property since 2011 and has never previously been in arrears.&quot; Whether that is better depends on whether you want the reader to meet her history before the bad news or after it, which is lesson 2&#39;s question about bad news, showing up inside a single sentence.</p>\n<p>The pattern across all four: <strong>a short interruption that tells the reader which thing the subject is usually earns its place. A long one that gives history usually does not.</strong></p>\n</details></div>\n\n<h2>What people get wrong</h2>\n<p><strong>&quot;Short sentences are clear sentences.&quot;</strong> Length correlates with the fault and isn&#39;t the fault. The eighteen-word example above is the counterexample to carry around.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Vary your sentence length.&quot;</strong> Common advice, usually given with no reason attached, and it&#39;s really advice about rhythm rather than clarity. Rhythm matters, and it isn&#39;t what this lesson is about. Don&#39;t let a rule about variety talk you into leaving a twenty-word gap for the sake of the music.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;I read it back and it sounded fine.&quot;</strong> It will. You know what the verb is going to be. That&#39;s the commonest reason writers miss gaps in their own work, and it&#39;s why the exercise below asks for a number.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Parentheses are the problem.&quot;</strong> The punctuation is innocent. A twenty-word parenthesis between subject and verb is a problem, and so is a twenty-word relative clause, and so is a twenty-word list, none of which uses brackets. Look for the distance, not the marks.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;This contradicts putting the character first.&quot;</strong> It completes it. Get the right subject, then let it act promptly. If you can only do one, do lesson 3, because a sentence with the wrong subject can&#39;t be rescued by putting its verb close to it.</p>\n<h2>Practice</h2>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><b>Count the gaps in a page of your own writing</b><p>Allow 45 minutes for this. Use the document you&#39;ve been carrying since lesson 1, or a fresh page of anything you wrote for someone else.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p>Take one page, about 300 to 400 words. For every sentence, find the main subject and its verb and count the words between them. Write the number in the margin. Do all of them, including the ones that are obviously fine, because you need the distribution and not just the outliers.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Rank them. You&#39;ll usually find that two or three sentences carry most of the trouble and the rest are already fine. That&#39;s normal, and it&#39;s why this is worth measuring rather than rewriting everything.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Take your worst sentence and fix it <strong>all three ways</strong>: interruption to the front, interruption to the end, and split into two. Write out all three versions in full. Don&#39;t choose yet.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Now choose, and write one line saying why. The question isn&#39;t which sounds best. It&#39;s what you want the reader to be thinking about when the sentence ends.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Fix the next two worst, one way each.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Last, check your three rewrites against lesson 3. Is the subject of each one a character, and is the verb its action? If you&#39;ve closed a gap but left an abstraction sitting in the subject slot, you&#39;ve done half the job.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Where this goes next</h2>\n<p>Two facts about readers, and they turn out to be one fact. Readers want a character doing something, and they want the doing to arrive quickly. Both are about the front of the sentence.</p>\n<p>The next lesson is about the other end, and it is the deepest idea in the course. You have already brushed against it twice: in the three fixes above, where the choice depended on what landed last, and in lesson 2, where a whole document had to be arranged around what the reader meets first. Lesson 5 gives you the principle underneath both, and with it the reason a paragraph of perfectly good sentences can still read like a list of unrelated facts.</p>\n<h2>Go deeper</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://wstomv.win.tue.nl/quotes/science-of-scientific-writing.html\">Gopen and Swan in full</a>. Their principle 1 is this lesson in one line, &quot;Follow a grammatical subject as soon as possible with its verb&quot;, and the passage around it explains the syntactic resolution the reader is waiting for.</li>\n<li>George D. Gopen, <em>Expectations: Teaching Writing from the Reader&#39;s Perspective</em> (2004), the book-length version of the paper, for anyone who wants the whole system rather than the seven principles.</li>\n<li>Joseph M. Williams, <em>Style: Lessons in Clarity and Grace</em>, whose clarity lessons are reported to be the fuller treatment of subjects and verbs. This course has not read it; see the Sources note.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://guidance.publishing.service.gov.uk/writing-to-gov-uk-standards/writing-guidelines/clear-language/\">GOV.UK on clear language</a>, for the 25-word rule of thumb in its original context, next to the other rules a government writes for itself.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>George D. Gopen and Judith A. Swan, <a href=\"https://wstomv.win.tue.nl/quotes/science-of-scientific-writing.html\">&quot;The Science of Scientific Writing&quot;</a>, <em>American Scientist</em> 78 (1990). Structural principle one, and the sentence quoted in full above, including its second clause, &quot;and therefore as something of lesser importance&quot;, which is what makes the choice among the three fixes a choice about emphasis. Their caveat that none of the principles are rules is quoted in the same section.</li>\n<li>GOV.UK, <a href=\"https://guidance.publishing.service.gov.uk/writing-to-gov-uk-standards/writing-guidelines/clear-language/\">&quot;Use clear language&quot;</a>, for the twenty-five word rule of thumb, treated here as a place to look rather than a definition of clarity.</li>\n<li>Joseph M. Williams, <em>Style: Lessons in Clarity and Grace</em>, <strong>reported</strong> to treat subjects and verbs more fully than this lesson does. This course has read the book&#39;s structure and central principles as others report them, not the text itself, so nothing here is quoted from it and no claim about its contents goes beyond that report.</li>\n<li>Every word count in this lesson was counted from the sentence printed beside it: the twenty-four-word gap in the opening, the eighteen-word gap in the worked example, and the thirty-six and eighteen-word pair in the wrinkle.</li>\n<li>The banding in the callout (&quot;under about five words is invisible...&quot;) is the drafter&#39;s working rule of thumb reasoned from the principle, not a measured threshold, and the callout says so.</li>\n</ul>\n","quiz":[{"q":"Consider: \"The report, which was commissioned after the second inspection and circulated to every member of the committee before the summer recess, recommends closure.\" Where is the reader's difficulty?","options":["In the vocabulary, since commissioned, circulated and recess are all formal words for ordinary things","In the length, because a sentence of this many words is beyond what an ordinary reader can follow","In the passive constructions, which hide who commissioned the report and who circulated it","In the nineteen word wait between the subject, the report, and the verb that resolves it, recommends"],"answer":3,"explain":"The subject arrives immediately and then the reader holds it for nineteen words before learning what it does, which is the fault this lesson names. Count them and you get the number, which is the point of counting. That is D. A points at words that are not the problem. B blames length, which the lesson treats as a proxy rather than a cause. C names a real feature of the sentence that is not what makes it hard here."},{"q":"A grant letter reads: \"The trustees, having reviewed the budget you submitted in January and the revised figures sent last week, have approved the award.\" A colleague rewrites it as \"The trustees have approved the award, having reviewed the budget you submitted in January and the revised figures sent last week.\" Has the rewrite done the job this lesson asks for?","options":["No, because the sentence is the same length and no words have actually been removed from it","Yes, the gap is closed, but it now ends on the budget review rather than on the award","No, the gap is unchanged, since the reviewing still sits between the trustees and their approving","Yes, and nothing else has changed, because moving a clause cannot affect what a sentence stresses"],"answer":1,"explain":"Count it. The original holds \"the trustees\" for eighteen words before \"have approved\"; the rewrite gets there in five, so the gap is closed and B and D are the only candidates. What separates them is the end of the sentence, which now lands on the budget review instead of the award, so B is right. A treats length as the criterion, which this lesson spends a section refusing. C is simply a miscount, and counting is the whole method here. D denies the stress position, which the three fixes above demonstrate."},{"q":"A colleague says a document is hard to read because its sentences average thirty words, and proposes cutting every sentence to under twenty. What does this lesson say about that plan?","options":["Length is a proxy; a thirty word sentence with its subject and verb adjacent can read easily","The plan is sound, because twenty words is the threshold at which comprehension reliably drops","Sentence length does not matter at all, so the plan will make no difference either way","The plan is too timid, since the guidance quoted in this course recommends fifteen words"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson's second worked example is a long sentence that reads easily and a short one that does not, and the difference is where the verb sits. That is A. B invents a threshold. C overcorrects into saying length is irrelevant, when it is a useful signal. D misreports the GOV.UK guidance, which suggests splitting sentences over twenty five words."},{"q":"Which of these interruptions between a subject and its verb is most likely to earn its place?","options":["A twelve word clause explaining the history of the policy the subject belongs to","Two words that identify which of several similar things the subject is referring to here","A parenthesis giving the reader background they will need later in the document","A relative clause naming everyone who was consulted before the decision was taken"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson's test is whether the reader needs the material in order to know what the subject is, and a two word identifier does exactly that at almost no cost. That is B. A, C and D are all material the reader could receive after the verb without losing the thread, and all three are long enough that the wait is expensive."},{"q":"A subeditor reads a colleague's draft aloud, hears nothing wrong, and passes it. The author then reads her own draft aloud, also hears nothing wrong, and she is the one who is mistaken. Why does this lesson expect that difference?","options":["Because reading aloud is unreliable for anyone who has not been trained as an editor","Because the subeditor is reading at a slower speed, and speed is what hides a long wait","Because the author supplies her own verb from memory before the sentence reaches it","Because the number of words in a gap can only be established by counting, never by ear"],"answer":2,"explain":"The author knows what the sentence is going to say, so the wait costs her nothing and she cannot feel what a first-time reader feels, which is C. It is the same reason lesson 1 gave for not trusting your own rereading, and it is why this lesson replaces the judgement with a count. A blames training, when the subeditor in the stem heard it correctly without needing any. B invents a mechanism about speed. D is close to the lesson's advice but does not answer the question asked, since the subeditor did hear it by ear."},{"q":"You have moved a character into the subject slot, as lesson 3 taught. What does this lesson add to that?","options":["That the character should be replaced by its action if the sentence is still hard to read","That the verb should follow that character quickly, or the gain from moving it is spent waiting","That the character belongs at the end of the sentence instead, where readers place emphasis","That characters and actions matter in short sentences but not in sentences over about thirty words"],"answer":1,"explain":"The two lessons are halves of one move, getting the right subject and then letting it act promptly, which is B. A undoes lesson 3. C confuses the subject slot with the stress position, which lesson 5 will separate properly. D invents a length condition neither lesson states."}]},"05-topic-and-stress-position":{"content":"<p>Read this paragraph. Every sentence in it is grammatical, none is longer than fifteen words, and there are no buried actions or long waits between subject and verb. It has passed every test this course has given you so far.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The council commissioned a structural survey in January. Corrosion in the main support beams was found by the survey. About two million pounds is the cost of replacing those beams. Four hundred thousand pounds remains in this year&#39;s capital budget. Closure until at least next spring is the consequence.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Read it and you can extract the facts. You can also feel that it is hard work, and that the effort is out of proportion to the content, which is a bridge, a survey, some rust and a shortage of money.</p>\n<p>Now the same paragraph again.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The council commissioned a structural survey in January. The survey found corrosion in the main support beams. Replacing those beams would cost about two million pounds. That cost is far more than the four hundred thousand pounds left in this year&#39;s capital budget. So the bridge stays closed until at least next spring.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Not one fact has been added or removed. The two versions are 49 and 53 words, and the second is barely longer: two of its sentences lost two words each and one gained seven. What did change is the order, and a handful of words that are links rather than facts.</p>\n<div class=\"think predict\"><b>Predict first</b><p class=\"think-q\">What changed between the two versions?</p><details><summary>Show the answer</summary><p>Every sentence after the first begins with something you already had.</p>\n<p>Sentence two opens with <strong>the survey</strong>, which sentence one just gave you. Sentence three opens with <strong>replacing those beams</strong>, which sentence two just gave you. Sentence four opens with <strong>that cost</strong>, from sentence three. Sentence five opens with <strong>so</strong>, which points at everything before it.</p>\n<p>In the first version, every sentence after the first opens with something new: corrosion, two million pounds, four hundred thousand pounds, closure. The reader is handed a fresh object four times in a row and has to work out what each one has to do with the last.</p>\n<p>Ten words are new in the second version and six have gone, and almost all of them are doing the linking rather than carrying a fact. &quot;That cost&quot;, &quot;far more than&quot;, &quot;so&quot;, &quot;the bridge&quot;: every one of those exists to attach a sentence to the one before it. The facts, the survey and the corrosion and the two figures and the closure, are word for word the same.</p>\n</details></div>\n\n<p>That is the whole idea, and it has a name.</p>\n<h2>The two positions</h2>\n<p>Gopen and Swan&#39;s account of this in <a href=\"https://wstomv.win.tue.nl/quotes/science-of-scientific-writing.html\">&quot;The Science of Scientific Writing&quot;</a> is the clearest statement in the literature, and the two claims are worth having exactly.</p>\n<p>On the beginning of a sentence:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&quot;The information that begins a sentence establishes for the reader a perspective for viewing the sentence as a unit: Readers expect a unit of discourse to be a story about whoever shows up first.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>They call this the <strong>topic position</strong>. It is not where you put the important thing. It is where you tell the reader what this sentence is <em>about</em>, and by extension how it connects to the one before.</p>\n<p>On the end:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&quot;It is a linguistic commonplace that readers naturally emphasize the material that arrives at the end of a sentence.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is the <strong>stress position</strong>. It is where the reader places their weight, and where they will still be when the sentence stops.</p>\n<p>And then the principle that joins them, which they call the given and new contract:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&quot;Put in the topic position the old information that links backward; put in the stress position the new information you want the reader to emphasize.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Old at the front. New at the end. Every sentence a bridge from something the reader has to something they don&#39;t.</p>\n<p>Their own caveat sits directly under the numbered list those principles come from, and it applies here as much as anywhere: <strong>&quot;None of these reader-expectation principles should be considered &#39;rules.&#39;&quot;</strong> What follows is a description of what readers do with order, not an instruction you owe obedience to. The section on conflicts below is where that matters most.</p>\n<p>The diagram below draws the contract across three sentences.</p>\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 420 268\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:420px;display:block;margin:1.5em auto;font-family:system-ui,sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"gn-title gn-desc\">\n<title id=\"gn-title\">The given and new contract across three sentences of the bridge paragraph</title>\n<desc id=\"gn-desc\">Three horizontal bars stand for sentences one to three of the repaired paragraph about the bridge. Each bar has a dark topic position block at its left end and a dark red stress position block at its right end, with the paragraph's own words in them. Bar one opens on the council and ends on a structural survey. Bar two opens on the survey and ends on corrosion. Bar three opens on those beams and ends on two million pounds. A gold line runs from the stress position of each bar down and back to the topic position of the bar below, showing that the new information ending one sentence is the given information opening the next.</desc>\n<text x=\"8\" y=\"20\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"var(--text, #111418)\">Old at the front, new at the end, then repeat</text>\n<text x=\"8\" y=\"38\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\">What ends one sentence is what opens the next.</text>\n<text x=\"22\" y=\"62\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--navy, #0f2a4a)\">topic</text>\n<text x=\"398\" y=\"62\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"var(--oxblood, #8b1e2d)\">stress</text>\n<rect x=\"22\" y=\"70\" width=\"376\" height=\"28\" fill=\"var(--navy-soft, #e8eef6)\" stroke=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<rect x=\"22\" y=\"70\" width=\"104\" height=\"28\" fill=\"var(--navy, #0f2a4a)\"/>\n<rect x=\"266\" y=\"70\" width=\"132\" height=\"28\" fill=\"var(--oxblood, #8b1e2d)\"/>\n<text x=\"30\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"var(--navy-ink, #ffffff)\">The council</text>\n<text x=\"274\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"var(--navy-ink, #ffffff)\">a survey</text>\n<rect x=\"22\" y=\"154\" width=\"376\" height=\"28\" fill=\"var(--navy-soft, #e8eef6)\" stroke=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<rect x=\"22\" y=\"154\" width=\"104\" height=\"28\" fill=\"var(--navy, #0f2a4a)\"/>\n<rect x=\"266\" y=\"154\" width=\"132\" height=\"28\" fill=\"var(--oxblood, #8b1e2d)\"/>\n<text x=\"30\" y=\"173\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"var(--navy-ink, #ffffff)\">The survey</text>\n<text x=\"274\" y=\"173\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"var(--navy-ink, #ffffff)\">corrosion</text>\n<rect x=\"22\" y=\"238\" width=\"376\" height=\"28\" fill=\"var(--navy-soft, #e8eef6)\" stroke=\"var(--text-2, #4a5260)\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<rect x=\"22\" y=\"238\" width=\"104\" height=\"28\" fill=\"var(--navy, #0f2a4a)\"/>\n<rect x=\"266\" y=\"238\" width=\"132\" height=\"28\" fill=\"var(--oxblood, #8b1e2d)\"/>\n<text x=\"30\" y=\"257\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"var(--navy-ink, #ffffff)\">Those beams</text>\n<text x=\"274\" y=\"257\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"var(--navy-ink, #ffffff)\">two million</text>\n<path d=\"M 332 98 L 332 124 L 74 124 L 74 148\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--gold, #b08d2a)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<polygon points=\"68,146 80,146 74,156\" fill=\"var(--gold, #b08d2a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M 332 182 L 332 208 L 74 208 L 74 232\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--gold, #b08d2a)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<polygon points=\"68,230 80,230 74,240\" fill=\"var(--gold, #b08d2a)\"/>\n</svg>\n\n<h2>Why the choppy version was choppy</h2>\n<p>Go back to the first paragraph and you can now say exactly what was wrong with it.</p>\n<p>Sentence two ended on &quot;by the survey&quot;. The survey was old information, and it was sitting in the stress position, where the reader was placing weight on it. Meanwhile &quot;corrosion&quot;, which was the news, was at the front, where the reader was looking for orientation and instead got a fresh object.</p>\n<p>Every sentence after the first had its information exactly backwards. Not one of them was ungrammatical, unclear, or too long. They were each pointing the reader&#39;s attention at the wrong end.</p>\n<p>That&#39;s why &quot;make your sentences clearer&quot; is useless advice for a choppy paragraph. Each sentence was already clear. <strong>Cohesion isn&#39;t a property of sentences. It&#39;s a property of the joins between them,</strong> and the joins live at the two ends.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><b>Why transitions do not fix this</b><p>The standard repair for a choppy paragraph is to bolt on however, therefore, in addition, furthermore. Try it on the first version and you will see what it buys you.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The council commissioned a structural survey in January. <strong>Subsequently,</strong> corrosion in the main support beams was found by the survey. <strong>Furthermore,</strong> about two million pounds is the cost of replacing those beams.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is not better. It is longer, and now it is choppy prose that has been <em>told</em> it is connected. A transition word announces a relation; the topic position creates one. Use transitions where the relation is genuinely not obvious from the order, and stop using them to paper over sentences that are facing the wrong way.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>The wrinkle: this is why the passive is sometimes right</h2>\n<p>You&#39;ve been carrying an argument since lesson 3, and this is where it settles.</p>\n<p>In 1918, Strunk gave this pair in <a href=\"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/37134/pg37134.txt\"><em>The Elements of Style</em></a>, inside the rule headed &quot;Use the active voice&quot;:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The dramatists of the Restoration are little esteemed to-day.</p>\n<p>Modern readers have little esteem for the dramatists of the Restoration.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The first is passive. The second is active. And Strunk doesn&#39;t tell you to use the second. He says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&quot;The first would be the right form in a paragraph on the dramatists of the Restoration; the second, in a paragraph on the tastes of modern readers. The need of making a particular word the subject of the sentence will often, as in these examples, determine which voice is to be used.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Read that last sentence again. <strong>The need to make a particular word the subject determines the voice.</strong> That&#39;s the topic position, described in 1918, seventy-two years before Gopen and Swan named it.</p>\n<p>Give Strunk the credit here, because this course has been hard on rule 10 and it&#39;s easy to leave the wrong impression. He isn&#39;t caught out by this example. One paragraph earlier, in the same rule, he writes:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&quot;This rule does not, of course, mean that the writer should entirely discard the passive voice, which is frequently convenient and sometimes necessary.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>So the man whose rule is headed &quot;Use the active voice&quot; says in the rule itself that the passive is frequently convenient and sometimes necessary, and then shows you a case where it&#39;s the right choice. What&#39;s misleading isn&#39;t Strunk. It&#39;s the heading, read on its own by people who never got to the paragraph underneath, and a century of advice that repeated the heading and dropped the rest.</p>\n<p>So the honest position on the passive isn&#39;t &quot;avoid it&quot;, and it isn&#39;t a replacement rule either. It&#39;s this: <strong>the passive is what lets you put something other than the actor in the topic position.</strong> If your paragraph is about the dramatists, the dramatists go at the front, and the passive is how you get them there.</p>\n<p>Be fair to the other side, which has a real case. Strunk&#39;s own reason for the rule stands: &quot;The active voice is usually more direct and vigorous than the passive.&quot; GOV.UK tells government writers to use the active voice because it &quot;is more direct and puts the focus on the user&quot;, and for most sentences most of the time that&#39;s right, which is why it survives as a default. The claim in this section is narrower than &quot;prefer the passive&quot;. It&#39;s that the choice is decided by what the paragraph is about, and that a writer who can&#39;t reach for the passive has lost the ability to write a paragraph about the thing something was done to.</p>\n<div class=\"think predict\"><b>Predict first</b><p class=\"think-q\">A paragraph is about a patient&#39;s recovery. Which of these two sentences belongs in it, and why?</p><details><summary>Show the answer</summary><p>(a) &quot;The consultant discharged her on the fourth day.&quot;</p>\n<p>(b) &quot;She was discharged on the fourth day.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong>, the passive one. The paragraph is about her, so she belongs in the topic position, and the passive is what puts her there. Version (a) shifts the story to the consultant, and a reader will take the next sentence to be about him.</p>\n<p>If the paragraph were about the consultant&#39;s decisions, (a) would be right and (b) would be the mistake. Neither sentence is better in the abstract, which is the whole point: a rule that bans one of them takes away the version you need whenever the paragraph is about the patient.</p>\n</details></div>\n\n<p>There&#39;s a second consequence, and it settles something people argue about endlessly. <em>The Elements of Style</em> says the split infinitive &quot;should be avoided unless the writer wishes to place unusual stress on the adverb.&quot; Geoffrey Pullum, in <a href=\"https://pullum.ppls.ed.ac.uk/50years.pdf\">&quot;50 Years of Stupid Grammar Advice&quot;</a>, points out that this has the stress exactly backwards:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&quot;Tucking the adverb in before the verb actually de-emphasizes the adverb, so a sentence like &#39;The dean&#39;s statements tend to completely polarize the faculty&#39; places the stress on polarizing the faculty. The way to stress the completeness of the polarization would be to write, &#39;The dean&#39;s statements tend to polarize the faculty completely.&#39;&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>He&#39;s applying the stress position, and he says so: the book&#39;s own earlier section, &quot;Place the emphatic words of a sentence at the end&quot;, already implies it.</p>\n<p>Two things to keep straight about whose claim this is. Pullum notes that the split-infinitive remark is <strong>White&#39;s</strong>, added in the 1959 revision: &quot;Strunk&#39;s original version never even mentioned split infinitives.&quot; This course has read Strunk&#39;s 1918 text, where the only mention of the construction says it &quot;is in disfavor&quot; and makes no claim about stress at all. So this is not a case of Strunk contradicting himself, and the 1918 book this lesson has otherwise been quoting is not the book making the error.</p>\n<p>And Pullum isn&#39;t the wholesale opponent he is sometimes made into. In the same essay he calls the style advice &quot;mostly harmless&quot; and says White &quot;often wrote beautifully&quot;. His quarrel is with the grammatical claims, not with the enterprise, which is worth knowing before lesson 8 takes up his argument at length.</p>\n<h2>What to do when they conflict</h2>\n<p>Sooner or later the two positions pull against each other. The old information that links backward is also the thing you most want emphasised.</p>\n<p>Take a case. A paragraph has just told the reader that the fire door was propped open. The next sentence has to say that this is what the inspector found, and &quot;the propped door&quot; is both the old information and the thing you want to land:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The propped door was what the inspector wrote up.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Old information is in the topic position, so the link holds, but the sentence ends on &quot;wrote up&quot;, which is not what you wanted emphasised. Turn it round:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What the inspector wrote up was the propped door.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Now the door lands, and the sentence opens on the inspector, who has not been mentioned and is not what the paragraph is about. The reader gets a fresh object where they were looking for orientation. Both versions cost something.</p>\n<p>When you have to choose, my own rule, and it&#39;s a judgement rather than something Gopen and Swan say, is that the <strong>topic position usually wins</strong>. A reader who has lost the thread can&#39;t be made to emphasise anything, so orientation comes first.</p>\n<p>But there&#39;s usually a better move than choosing, and it&#39;s the one to reach for: split the sentence.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The inspector wrote up the propped door. That door is now the only thing standing between the building and a prohibition notice.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Two sentences, two topic positions, two stress positions, and the door is in both. A sentence trying to do two jobs often just needed to be two sentences.</p>\n<div class=\"think checkpoint\"><b>Check yourself</b><p class=\"think-q\">Can you repair this paragraph by moving information only, adding and removing nothing but connective words?</p><details><summary>Show the answer</summary><blockquote>\n<p>&quot;A new booking system was introduced by the trust in April. Waiting times fell by nine days over the following quarter. Staff training had been completed in March. Complaints about the phone line rose sharply in the same period.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>One repair:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&quot;In April the trust introduced a new booking system. That system had been preceded by staff training, completed in March. Over the following quarter, waiting times fell by nine days. In the same period, complaints about the phone line rose sharply.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>What changed and why. Sentence one puts the date at the front as orientation and ends on the new booking system, which is the news and the thing every later sentence points back at. Sentence two opens on &quot;that system&quot; and lands on the training. Sentence three opens with the time frame and lands on &quot;nine days&quot;, the figure worth remembering. Sentence four opens on &quot;in the same period&quot;, which points back at that quarter, and lands on the sharp rise in complaints.</p>\n<p>Notice what the repair does not do. It doesn&#39;t say the system caused the complaints. The original says the two happened in the same period and no more, and a repair that moves information is not allowed to add a causal claim that wasn&#39;t there. That temptation is worth naming, because it&#39;s where this move goes wrong: arranging sentences into a chain can make a sequence look like an argument. Keep the chain and leave the claim alone.</p>\n<p>If your repair differs from mine, that&#39;s expected. Check it against one question: does every sentence after the first begin with something the reader already has?</p>\n</details></div>\n\n<h2>What people get wrong</h2>\n<p><strong>&quot;Never use the passive.&quot;</strong> The passive is how you control the topic position. Banning it costs you the ability to write a paragraph about the thing that had something done to it, which is a great many paragraphs.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Put the important word first.&quot;</strong> This is the single most common misreading of this material, and it is backwards. The front of a sentence is for orientation. The end is for emphasis. If you front-load the important word, the reader arrives at the full stop with their weight on whatever happened to be lying around at the end.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Front-loading a document means front-loading every sentence.&quot;</strong> Lesson 2 was about where the point of a <em>document</em> goes, and the reason was the reading budget. This lesson is about the internal shape of a <em>sentence</em>, and the reason is how readers process order. They are different scales with different rules, and this is the one place in the course where the advice at two levels genuinely differs.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Transitions make writing flow.&quot;</strong> They label flow. Cohesion comes from the joins, and transitions sit on top of the joins rather than making them.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;This is stylistic.&quot;</strong> Gopen and Swan&#39;s claim is about what readers expect of order, not about a stopwatch, and nothing here has been timed. But you read both versions of the bridge paragraph, with the same facts in them, and one of them cost you more. That&#39;s the evidence available, and it&#39;s the evidence that matters, because it&#39;s the same evidence your reader will have.</p>\n<h2>Practice</h2>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><b>Mark the ends of your own sentences</b><p>Allow 40 minutes for this, and do it slowly. It&#39;s the exercise in this course that changes how you read.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p>Take three consecutive paragraphs from the document you have been carrying since lesson 1.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>For every sentence, underline the first few words and the last few words. Just those. You are marking the topic and stress positions.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Read only the underlined openings, in order, ignoring everything else. <strong>If that list of openings tells a coherent story, the paragraph coheres.</strong> If it reads as a set of unrelated objects, you have found your problem, and you have found it without judging any sentence.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Now read only the underlined endings, in order. Ask whether those are the words you would choose to have a reader remember. Very often two or three of them will be dates, references, or someone&#39;s job title that drifted to the end because that was where it fitted.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Repair one paragraph by moving information only. No new facts, no deletions, connective words allowed. Then run step 3 again on the repair.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Finally, find one sentence in your own writing where the passive is the right choice, and write one line explaining why in terms of what the paragraph is about. If you cannot find one, look for a paragraph about a thing rather than a person, because that is where they live.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Where this goes next</h2>\n<p>You&#39;ve now met the whole of the sentence. Lesson 3 gave you its subject and its verb, lesson 4 the distance between them, and this lesson its two ends. Everything else in this course is either larger than a sentence or about deciding what to cut.</p>\n<p>Cutting is next, and this lesson has just made it possible. Lesson 6 is about concision, and it couldn&#39;t have come earlier, because until you can see the topic position you can&#39;t tell padding from the repetition that holds a paragraph together. The commonest way to wreck a passage is to cut the words that were doing the linking.</p>\n<h2>Go deeper</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://wstomv.win.tue.nl/quotes/science-of-scientific-writing.html\">Gopen and Swan in full</a>. This lesson takes three of their seven principles. The paper works them through a real scientific paragraph, rewriting it four times, and the demonstration is better than any summary of it.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://pullum.ppls.ed.ac.uk/50years.pdf\">Pullum&#39;s &quot;50 Years of Stupid Grammar Advice&quot;</a> in full, which is short, funny, and more careful than its title. Lesson 8 takes up its argument properly.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/37134/pg37134.txt\">Strunk&#39;s 1918 text</a>, rule 10 in particular. Reading the rule rather than its heading is the point this lesson makes, and it takes two minutes.</li>\n<li>Joseph M. Williams, <em>Style: Lessons in Clarity and Grace</em>, whose lesson on cohesion and coherence is reported to cover this ground at greater length. This course has not read it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>George D. Gopen and Judith A. Swan, <a href=\"https://wstomv.win.tue.nl/quotes/science-of-scientific-writing.html\">&quot;The Science of Scientific Writing&quot;</a>, <em>American Scientist</em> 78 (1990). Structural principles two, four and seven, and the given and new contract. The three quotations attributed to them in this lesson are from that article, as is their caveat that none of the principles are rules.</li>\n<li>William Strunk Jr., <a href=\"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/37134/pg37134.txt\"><em>The Elements of Style</em></a> (1918), rule 10, for the Restoration dramatists pair, for the sentence about the need to make a particular word the subject determining the voice, and for his own qualification that the rule &quot;does not, of course, mean that the writer should entirely discard the passive voice, which is frequently convenient and sometimes necessary.&quot; The 1918 text was read for this lesson, and its only mention of the split infinitive says the construction &quot;is in disfavor&quot;, with no claim about stress.</li>\n<li>Geoffrey K. Pullum, <a href=\"https://pullum.ppls.ed.ac.uk/50years.pdf\">&quot;50 Years of Stupid Grammar Advice&quot;</a>, <em>The Chronicle of Higher Education</em> (2009), quoted verbatim, for the split infinitive and the stress position, for the fact that the stress claim is White&#39;s 1959 addition rather than Strunk&#39;s, and for &quot;mostly harmless&quot; and &quot;often wrote beautifully&quot;. <strong>E. B. White&#39;s 1959 revision is not read by this course and nothing is cited to it</strong>; the split-infinitive wording is quoted as Pullum reports it. Lesson 8 takes up his wider argument.</li>\n<li>Joseph M. Williams, <em>Style: Lessons in Clarity and Grace</em>, whose lesson on cohesion and coherence is <strong>reported</strong> to cover the same ground at greater length. This course has read the book&#39;s structure and central principles as others report them, not the text itself.</li>\n<li>The two versions of the bridge paragraph were recounted for this review: 49 and 53 words, with two sentences two words shorter and one seven words longer, ten words added and six dropped.</li>\n</ul>\n","quiz":[{"q":"A charity's annual report contains this paragraph. \"We opened the Leeds centre in March. Two hundred families used the service in its first quarter. Funding from the lottery runs until 2027. A second centre is planned for Hull.\" Each sentence is clear, and the paragraph reads as four unrelated facts. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The sentences are too short, so the reader has no time to build connections between them","Every sentence after the first opens with something new, so none of them links back","The paragraph lacks transition words such as however, therefore and in addition at its joins","The paragraph covers four separate topics and should be split into four separate paragraphs"],"answer":1,"explain":"Read the openings in order and you get \"We\", \"Two hundred families\", \"Funding\", \"A second centre\", which is four fresh objects in a row with nothing pointing back, so B is the cause. A blames length, and this lesson's own demonstration shows a paragraph repaired without its sentences getting shorter. C is the patch the lesson warns about, since a transition announces a relation rather than making one. D assumes a structural problem, when the four facts are plainly one story about one charity."},{"q":"A conservation report has a section about a listed mill. The next sentence has to say that the contractor removed the original windows. Which version belongs in that section?","options":["\"The contractor removed the original windows\", because the active voice is more direct and vigorous","Either, because the two sentences carry the same information and the choice comes down to taste","\"The contractor removed the original windows\", because naming who performed an action is always clearer for a reader","\"The original windows were removed by the contractor\", since this section is about the mill"],"answer":3,"explain":"The section is about the building, so the windows belong in the topic position and the passive is what puts them there, which is D. A and C both state real merits of the active voice, and both apply a default without asking what the paragraph is about; C's \"always\" is the giveaway. B treats the two as interchangeable, when the whole lesson is that context decides which one is right here."},{"q":"You want a reader to remember one figure from a sentence. Where should it go?","options":["At the front, because readers give the most attention to the opening of a sentence","In the middle, where it is surrounded by the context that makes it meaningful","At the end, because readers naturally place their weight on whatever arrives last","Wherever it falls naturally, since a reader will notice a number anywhere in a sentence"],"answer":2,"explain":"Gopen and Swan call the end of a sentence the stress position and say readers naturally emphasise what arrives there, so C is right. A confuses the topic position, which sets perspective, with emphasis. B has no support in the lesson. D is the assumption this lesson is written against, that a figure carries its own weight regardless of where it sits."},{"q":"A writer fixes a choppy paragraph by adding however, therefore and in addition at the start of three sentences. What has this achieved?","options":["It labels connections without making them, so the reader is told there is a link rather than shown one","It repairs the paragraph properly, since transition words are the standard tool for cohesion","It makes the paragraph worse in every case, because transition words should never be used in prose","It changes the emphasis of each sentence, moving the weight from the end to the beginning"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson treats transitions as a patch over a topic-position problem, announcing a relation the sentences do not themselves establish. That is A. B is the belief the lesson is written against. C overstates, since the lesson does not ban transitions but says they cannot substitute for cohesion. D describes something transitions do not do."},{"q":"A policy note reads \"The new rules are expected to significantly reduce delays.\" A colleague says the split infinitive is fine here because it puts unusual stress on \"significantly\". What should you tell her?","options":["She is right, since placing an adverb before its verb is exactly what gives the adverb stress","She has it backwards; the adverb tucked in there is de-emphasised and the weight falls on reducing delays","She is right about the stress but wrong to allow it, because a split infinitive should always be avoided","Neither of you can tell, since where a reader places emphasis is a matter of individual taste"],"answer":1,"explain":"Put \"significantly\" last and you stress it; leave it before the verb and the sentence lands on reducing delays, so B is right and the note should be rewritten only if the significance is the point. A is the claim Pullum shows is backwards. C accepts the false premise and adds a prohibition this course does not hold. D retreats into taste, when the stress position is a regularity in how readers read."},{"q":"Which change is most likely to make a passage cohere, without adding or removing any fact?","options":["Replacing every long word in it with a shorter word that means roughly the same thing","Splitting every one of its sentences into two, so that no sentence carries more than a single idea","Moving the most important information to the front of every sentence in the passage","Reordering each sentence so it opens with something the previous sentence has already given"],"answer":3,"explain":"The given and new contract is exactly this, old information in the topic position to link backward, which is D. A is vocabulary and does not touch cohesion. B may help elsewhere but does nothing about linkage. C sounds right and is the common misreading, since the front of a sentence is for orientation rather than for emphasis."}]},"06-concision-that-is-teachable":{"content":"<p>A paragraph of the kind that circulates in every organisation. It&#39;s 111 words.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>It should be noted at the outset that, due to the fact that the previous system was not able to handle the volume of applications that we were receiving during the course of the busy summer period, a decision was taken by the management team in order to procure a replacement. In the event that the new system is not fully and completely operational by the end of September, there is a possibility that we may possibly need to revert back to the old one on a temporary basis for a short period of time. It is important to note that this would not be a desirable outcome for anyone concerned.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>And here it is at 46 words, with nothing removed but words.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The old system could not handle the summer volume of applications, so the management team bought a replacement. If the new one is not fully working by the end of September, we may have to go back to the old one for a while. Nobody wants that.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Every fact survives. The old system, the volume, the summer, the management team, the replacement, September, the temporary reversion, and the fact that this would be unwelcome are all still there. Fifty-nine percent of the words are not.</p>\n<p>Note &quot;not fully working&quot; rather than &quot;not working&quot;. The original said &quot;not fully and completely operational&quot;, and a system that isn&#39;t <em>fully</em> operational may still be partly operational, which is a different fact from one that isn&#39;t working at all. <em>Fully and completely</em> is a doubled pair and one of them goes; <em>fully</em> is not padding, and dropping it would have been a cut that changed what the sentence claims. That distinction is the whole discipline of this lesson.</p>\n<h2>What the famous advice actually says</h2>\n<p>Strunk&#39;s version of this, in <a href=\"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/37134/pg37134.txt\"><em>The Elements of Style</em></a> of 1918, is the most quoted piece of writing advice there is. It&#39;s also routinely quoted at a quarter of its length, and the quarter that gets quoted is the least useful part.</p>\n<p>The whole of rule 13&#39;s first paragraph reads:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&quot;Vigorous writing is concise. A sentence should contain no unnecessary words, a paragraph no unnecessary sentences, for the same reason that a drawing should have no unnecessary lines and a machine no unnecessary parts. <strong>This requires not that the writer make all his sentences short, or that he avoid all detail and treat his subjects only in outline, but that he make every word tell.</strong>&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The analogy is exactly right. A machine part that does nothing isn&#39;t neutral; it&#39;s weight, and something has to carry it. And the bolded sentence, which almost nobody quotes, is Strunk answering the misreading his own headline invites, sixty years before anyone accused him of it. He is not telling you to be brief. He is telling you to make every word tell.</p>\n<p>Then there&#39;s the part that surprises people who only know the four words. Rule 13 doesn&#39;t stop at the principle. It gives you a list:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>the question as to whether → <strong>whether</strong></p>\n<p>there is no doubt but that → <strong>no doubt</strong></p>\n<p>used for fuel purposes → <strong>used for fuel</strong></p>\n<p>he is a man who → <strong>he</strong></p>\n<p>in a hasty manner → <strong>hastily</strong></p>\n<p>owing to the fact that → <strong>since</strong></p>\n<p>in spite of the fact that → <strong>though</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is instruction, not a slogan, and two of this lesson&#39;s six kinds are sitting in it already.</p>\n<p>So where&#39;s the objection? Geoffrey Pullum puts it this way in <a href=\"https://pullum.ppls.ed.ac.uk/50years.pdf\">&quot;50 Years of Stupid Grammar Advice&quot;</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&quot;Many are useless, like &#39;Omit needless words.&#39; (The students who know which words are needless don&#39;t need the instruction.)&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Read fairly, that lands on the <strong>headline</strong> and not on the rule. Four words on their own tell you what the finished state looks like and nothing about how to reach it, rather like telling someone to sculpt an elephant by removing everything that doesn&#39;t look like an elephant. Strunk knew that, which is why he wrote three more paragraphs and a substitution list. What happened over a century is that the headline travelled and the rule stayed behind.</p>\n<p>Pullum is careful about this too, and the care usually gets dropped when he&#39;s quoted. In the same essay he calls the style advice &quot;mostly harmless&quot; and writes: &quot;Even so, it doesn&#39;t hurt to lay such well-meant maxims before novice writers.&quot; His quarrel is with the book&#39;s grammatical claims, which lesson 8 takes up, not with the idea of telling a beginner to cut.</p>\n<p>So this lesson isn&#39;t supplying what Strunk left out. It&#39;s doing what he did, at more length and with the kinds named, because a list you can hold in your head is what makes the cut repeatable.</p>\n<h2>The six kinds</h2>\n<p>Don&#39;t cut by feel. Name the kind of every cut, and you&#39;ll be able to do it again next week and on someone else&#39;s writing.</p>\n<p><strong>This list is mine.</strong> It was assembled from the sources at the foot of this lesson and from the pattern of the examples; Strunk&#39;s list is shorter, Williams&#39;s catalogue is longer. Use this one because six is few enough to remember at the moment you need it.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>Example, and the cut</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Doubled words</strong></td>\n<td>Two words joined by <em>and</em> where either would do</td>\n<td><em>full and complete</em> → <strong>complete</strong>. Also <em>each and every</em>, <em>first and foremost</em>, <em>hopes and aspirations</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Already implied</strong></td>\n<td>The sentence has said it once in another word</td>\n<td><em>period of time</em> → <strong>period</strong>. Also <em>advance planning</em>, <em>revert back</em>, <em>large in size</em>, <em>in the month of May</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Metadiscourse</strong></td>\n<td>Writing about the writing</td>\n<td><em>It should be noted that</em>, <em>I would like to say that</em>, <em>It is important to note that</em>. Cut the whole phrase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>A phrase for a word</strong></td>\n<td>Four words doing one word&#39;s job</td>\n<td><em>due to the fact that</em> → <strong>because</strong>. Also <em>in the event that</em> → <strong>if</strong>, <em>at this point in time</em> → <strong>now</strong>, <em>in order to</em> → <strong>to</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Negative for positive</strong></td>\n<td>A not that has a word of its own</td>\n<td><em>was not able to</em> → <strong>could not</strong>. Also <em>did not remember</em> → <strong>forgot</strong>, <em>not many</em> → <strong>few</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hedge stacking</strong></td>\n<td>Two or three hedges where one is honest</td>\n<td><em>there is a possibility that we may possibly</em> → <strong>we may</strong>. Also <em>it seems likely that it could perhaps</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>None of these is a rule about long words, and every cut above leaves the vocabulary alone.</p>\n<div class=\"think predict\"><b>Predict first</b><p class=\"think-q\">Name the kind for each of these three, before you read on. &quot;In the event that&quot;, &quot;each and every&quot;, &quot;did not remember&quot;.</p><details><summary>Show the answer</summary><p><em>In the event that</em> is a phrase for a word, and the word is <em>if</em>. Four words to one.</p>\n<p><em>Each and every</em> is a doubled pair joined by <em>and</em>, where either half carries the whole meaning. Pick one.</p>\n<p><em>Did not remember</em> is a negative for a positive, and the positive is <em>forgot</em>. Three words to one.</p>\n<p>If you found those easy, that&#39;s the point of naming kinds. The hard part of concision isn&#39;t spotting that something is wrong, it&#39;s knowing what to do about it, and a named kind comes with its own fix attached.</p>\n</details></div>\n\n<div class=\"callout\"><b>Two of these are not really about concision</b><p><em>Metadiscourse</em> and <em>hedge stacking</em> are usually symptoms of something else. Metadiscourse piles up when a writer is nervous about the content and warms up before saying it. Hedges stack when the writer is not sure of the claim. If you find a lot of either, the cut is worth making, and it is also worth asking what the writing is being cautious about, because that is often the real problem with the document.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Down the paragraph, cut by cut</h2>\n<p>The 111-word paragraph again, with every cut named. Work down it and you&#39;ll see nothing left over that the six kinds and lesson 3 can&#39;t account for.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original</th>\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>It should be noted at the outset that</td>\n<td>metadiscourse</td>\n<td><em>(gone)</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>due to the fact that</td>\n<td>phrase for a word</td>\n<td>so</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>was not able to</td>\n<td>negative for positive</td>\n<td>could not</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the volume of applications that we were receiving</td>\n<td>already implied, since a volume you handle is one you receive</td>\n<td>the volume of applications</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>during the course of the busy summer period</td>\n<td>phrase for a word, twice over</td>\n<td>the summer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a decision was taken by the management team in order to procure</td>\n<td>buried action, from lesson 3</td>\n<td>the management team bought</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>In the event that</td>\n<td>phrase for a word</td>\n<td>If</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>fully and completely operational</td>\n<td>doubled words</td>\n<td>fully working</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>there is a possibility that we may possibly need to</td>\n<td>hedge stacking</td>\n<td>we may have to</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>revert back</td>\n<td>already implied, since revert means to go back</td>\n<td>go back</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>on a temporary basis for a short period of time</td>\n<td>doubled and a phrase for a word</td>\n<td>for a while</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>It is important to note that this would not be a desirable outcome for anyone concerned</td>\n<td>metadiscourse plus a negative for a positive</td>\n<td>Nobody wants that.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Notice the row that isn&#39;t one of the six. &quot;A decision was taken in order to procure&quot; is a buried action from lesson 3, and undoing it is a concision move as well as a clarity move. Count it as a seventh kind when you tally your own cuts, because it&#39;s the one that returns the most words for a single change. That overlap isn&#39;t a coincidence: an action buried in a noun always needs extra words to hold it up.</p>\n<div class=\"think predict\"><b>Predict first</b><p class=\"think-q\">How much of this can go, and what kind is each cut?</p><details><summary>Show the answer</summary><blockquote>\n<p>&quot;At this point in time, it would appear that there may possibly be a need for us to give consideration to a review of the current arrangements that are presently in place.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>One answer: <strong>&quot;We may need to review the current arrangements.&quot;</strong> Eight words from thirty-two, which is three quarters of it gone.</p>\n<p>The cuts, in order. <em>At this point in time</em> is a phrase for a word, and the word is <em>now</em>, which then turns out to be implied by the present tense and goes entirely. <em>It would appear that there may possibly be a need for us to</em> is hedge stacking, three hedges deep, and becomes <em>we may need to</em>. <em>Give consideration to a review of</em> is a buried action twice over, and becomes <em>review</em>. <em>That are presently in place</em> is already implied by <em>current</em>, and <em>current</em> and <em>presently</em> are themselves a doubled pair, so one of them goes.</p>\n<p>If you kept a hedge, that can be right. If you genuinely are unsure, <em>we may need to review</em> keeps one and is honest. What you shouldn&#39;t keep is all three.</p>\n</details></div>\n\n<h2>The wrinkle: some repetition is holding the passage together</h2>\n<p>This lesson couldn&#39;t have come any earlier, and here&#39;s why.</p>\n<p>Take two sentences from lesson 5&#39;s bridge paragraph:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Replacing those beams would cost about two million pounds. That cost is far more than the four hundred thousand pounds left in this year&#39;s capital budget.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A concision pass will find &quot;That cost is far more than&quot; and see six words repeating something the reader was told half a second ago. Cutting looks obvious:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Replacing those beams would cost about two million pounds. Only four hundred thousand pounds is left in this year&#39;s capital budget.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Five words shorter, both figures intact, no fact lost. And the paragraph is worse, because the second sentence now opens on a fresh number instead of on something the reader already has. The chain from lesson 5 is broken, and the reader has to work out the relation between two million and four hundred thousand for themselves.</p>\n<p><strong>Repetition in the topic position is not padding. It is the join.</strong> It looks exactly like padding, which is why concision passes damage prose so often, and why &quot;cut anything you&#39;ve already said&quot; is a dangerous rule for anyone who hasn&#39;t met the given and new contract.</p>\n<p>So ask what the repeated words are doing. If they&#39;re carrying the reader from the previous sentence into this one, they&#39;re load-bearing. If they&#39;re just there, they&#39;re not.</p>\n<div class=\"think checkpoint\"><b>Check yourself</b><p class=\"think-q\">Which of these repetitions is padding and which is the join?</p><details><summary>Show the answer</summary><p><strong>&quot;The trust introduced a new booking system in April. <em>The system</em> had been in development for two years.&quot;</strong> A join. It puts old information in the topic position, and cutting it to &quot;It had been in development for two years&quot; would be fine too, since <em>it</em> does the same job in fewer words. Either keeps the link.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;The report recommends closure. <em>The report</em> was commissioned in January. <em>The report</em> runs to ninety pages.&quot;</strong> Padding, in the second and third instances, and something worse: three sentences in a row opening on the same words is a list, not a chain. This wants restructuring, not cutting.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;We received your application on 3 March. <em>Your application</em> is now with the assessment team.&quot;</strong> A join, and worth keeping over <em>it</em> in this case, because the letter is about the reader&#39;s application and the phrase is doing reassurance as well as linkage.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;The committee met on Tuesday. <em>At that meeting on Tuesday</em>, the following matters were discussed.&quot;</strong> Padding. <em>At that meeting</em> would be the join; adding <em>on Tuesday</em> again repeats what the reader has, without carrying anything forward.</p>\n</details></div>\n\n<h2>What people get wrong</h2>\n<p><strong>&quot;Shorter is always better.&quot;</strong> A passage that&#39;s been cut below the point where it makes sense is shorter and useless. The target isn&#39;t fewer words; it&#39;s no words that fail to carry something. Strunk says this himself in the rule, which is the sentence nobody quotes: not that you make all your sentences short, &quot;but that he make every word tell.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Cut every adjective and adverb.&quot;</strong> This one isn&#39;t Strunk&#39;s. &quot;Write with nouns and verbs, not with adjectives and adverbs&quot; was added in the 1959 revision, and Pullum notes that the very next sentence of the book breaks it. This course hasn&#39;t read that revision and takes the wording from Pullum. Either way the advice is wrong: adjectives that carry information aren&#39;t padding. <em>A twelve-page report</em> and <em>a report</em> are different facts.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Never repeat a word.&quot;</strong> This one causes real damage. It sends writers to a thesaurus to find a different word for the same thing, and now the reader has to work out whether the <em>scheme</em> in this sentence is the same as the <em>programme</em> in the last one. Use the same word for the same thing, every time. Lesson 9 comes back to it: in Nancy Sommers&#39;s study of student and experienced revisers, one student described his whole revision strategy as reaching for a thesaurus. It&#39;s a recognisable habit, though that was one writer in a study of fifteen, so take it as a thing to watch for in yourself rather than as a measured difference between novices and experts.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Concision is about vocabulary.&quot;</strong> Nearly every cut in this lesson&#39;s table left the vocabulary untouched. Replacing <em>utilise</em> with <em>use</em> is worth doing and saves you two letters. Replacing <em>there is a possibility that we may possibly need to</em> with <em>we may</em> saves you nine words.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;I&#39;ll know padding when I see it.&quot;</strong> You won&#39;t, in your own writing, for the reason lesson 4 gave: you know what you meant, so every word looks necessary. Name the kind. If you can&#39;t name the kind, you&#39;re guessing.</p>\n<h2>Practice</h2>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><b>Cut something of yours by a third, and label every cut</b><p>Take 45 minutes over the cutting. Use the document you&#39;ve carried since lesson 1, in whatever state lessons 2 to 5 have left it. Step 6 needs another person, so it happens later.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p>Count the words. Write the number at the top. Your target is two thirds of it. A third is a working target, not a law: this lesson&#39;s own paragraph lost nearly sixty percent, and a document that&#39;s already been through lessons 2 to 5 may have much less to give.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Work through it once, cutting only what you can name. Keep a tally as you go, in seven columns: the six kinds, plus buried actions from lesson 3. <strong>If you can&#39;t say which kind a cut is, don&#39;t make it yet.</strong> Put a question mark in the margin and move on.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Now go back to the question marks. For each, read the sentence without the words and ask what a reader has lost. If the answer is nothing, cut it and work out afterwards which kind it was. If the answer is a link back to the previous sentence, put it back, and mark it as a join.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Look at your tally. Most people find one kind dominates, and knowing which one is yours is worth more than this whole exercise. Metadiscourse tends to mean you&#39;re warming up before you say things. Hedge stacking tends to mean you&#39;re not sure. Phrases for words tends to mean you learnt to write in an institution.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Check your cuts against lesson 5. Read only the opening words of each sentence, in order, as you did there. If the chain broke anywhere, you cut a join.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Then the test that settles it, whenever you can get it. Give the original and the cut version to one person, in that order, and ask which is clearer and where they got lost. If the shorter one is harder, you&#39;ve cut joins, and step 5 will tell you where.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Where this goes next</h2>\n<p>You have now finished the sentence and the paragraph. Everything so far has been about what happens inside a passage of prose.</p>\n<p>The next lesson goes up a level, to the parts of a document that aren&#39;t prose at all: headings, lists and tables. Those are what a reader uses when they&#39;re not reading but looking, and lesson 1&#39;s figures are about exactly that reader, the one who takes in about a fifth of a page they haven&#39;t yet decided to commit to. It&#39;s the quarter of the subject that writing courses usually leave to designers.</p>\n<h2>Go deeper</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/37134/pg37134.txt\">Strunk&#39;s rule 13 in full</a>, which is two pages, and most of it is the substitution list. Reading the rule instead of the slogan is this lesson&#39;s first point, and it takes three minutes.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://pullum.ppls.ed.ac.uk/50years.pdf\">Pullum&#39;s &quot;50 Years of Stupid Grammar Advice&quot;</a>, for the objection in its own context, including the concessions that usually get dropped when he&#39;s quoted.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://guidance.publishing.service.gov.uk/writing-to-gov-uk-standards/writing-guidelines/clear-language/\">GOV.UK&#39;s word list</a>, which is a working substitution table of the same kind as Strunk&#39;s, written a century later for people who have to get it right at scale.</li>\n<li>Joseph M. Williams, <em>Style: Lessons in Clarity and Grace</em>, Part Four, whose catalogue of redundancy is longer than the six kinds here. This course has not read it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>William Strunk Jr., <a href=\"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/37134/pg37134.txt\"><em>The Elements of Style</em></a> (1918), rule 13. The first paragraph is quoted in full above, including the sentence, usually dropped, that the rule &quot;requires not that the writer make all his sentences short... but that he make every word tell&quot;; the substitution pairs quoted are Strunk&#39;s own, from the same rule. The 1918 text was read for this lesson.</li>\n<li><strong>E. B. White&#39;s 1959 revision is not read by this course and nothing is cited to it.</strong> The &quot;write with nouns and verbs&quot; advice discussed in the misconceptions is quoted as Pullum reports it, and attributed to that revision rather than to Strunk. The string does not appear in the 1918 text.</li>\n<li>Geoffrey K. Pullum, <a href=\"https://pullum.ppls.ed.ac.uk/50years.pdf\">&quot;50 Years of Stupid Grammar Advice&quot;</a>, <em>The Chronicle of Higher Education</em> (2009), for the objection that the four-word maxim is not usable instruction, and for the qualifications quoted with it: &quot;mostly harmless&quot; and &quot;it doesn&#39;t hurt to lay such well-meant maxims before novice writers&quot;. His wider argument is lesson 8.</li>\n<li>Nancy Sommers, <em>Revision Strategies of Student Writers and Experienced Adult Writers</em> (report to the National Institute of Education, 1982), for Michael, the student whose &quot;strategy is to use a thesaurus&quot;. Eight freshman students and seven experienced adult writers. The lesson claims nothing about novices in general from it, because the study does not support that.</li>\n<li>Joseph M. Williams, <em>Style: Lessons in Clarity and Grace</em>, on concision. <strong>This course has read the book&#39;s structure and central principles as others report them, not the text itself</strong>, so the six kinds are this course&#39;s own list, assembled from the sources above and from the pattern of the examples. The lesson says so where the list is introduced.</li>\n<li>Every word count in this lesson was recounted from the passage printed beside it: 111 words to 46, which is 59 percent; the predict block&#39;s 32 words to 8; and the six-word repetition in the bridge pair.</li>\n</ul>\n","quiz":[{"q":"\"It should be noted that, due to the fact that the deadline has passed, we are not able to accept the form.\" Which cut removes metadiscourse specifically?","options":["Changing due to the fact that into because, since that is a phrase doing the work of one word","Changing are not able to into cannot, because a negative has been used where a positive one exists","Removing it should be noted that, because it comments on the writing rather than saying anything","Removing the deadline has passed, since a reader who is being refused can infer the reason"],"answer":2,"explain":"Metadiscourse is writing about the writing, and it should be noted that tells the reader only that a sentence is coming. That is C. A and B are real cuts of different kinds, a phrase for a word and a negative made positive. D is not a cut of any kind, since it removes a fact rather than words that carry none."},{"q":"A manager circulates the four words \"omit needless words\" to her team and expects their reports to improve. On this lesson's account, what is wrong with that, and what is not?","options":["The four words describe the finished state; Strunk's rule around them supplies the method she left out","Nothing is wrong; the maxim is the whole of concision and a team that reads it has what it needs","The maxim is false, and she should have told them that some needless words make prose pleasant","The maxim is sound, but concision matters far less than arrangement, so she has picked the wrong topic to push"],"answer":0,"explain":"The headline tells a writer what the result looks like and nothing about how to get there, which is Pullum's objection, so A is right, and its second half matters as much as its first, because Strunk's rule 13 does supply a method and she circulated four words of it. B treats the slogan as sufficient. C disputes the maxim's truth, which neither this lesson nor Pullum does. D ranks the topics, which is not the objection."},{"q":"A newsletter reads \"The trust replaced its phone system in March. That replacement has already cut the average wait to under two minutes.\" An editor cuts the second sentence to \"The average wait is now under two minutes.\" What has been lost?","options":["Nothing has been lost, since the cut removed four words and kept the figure the reader needs","A fact has been lost, because the shorter version no longer says what the average wait used to be","The tone has been lost, because the shorter version now reads as abrupt rather than explanatory","The link has been lost, because the second sentence no longer opens on something already given"],"answer":3,"explain":"The words \"that replacement\" were old information sitting in the topic position, holding the two sentences together, so cutting them leaves the second sentence opening on a fresh figure and the reader joining it up themselves. That is D, and it is why this lesson comes after lesson 5. A counts words and misses the join. B names a fact that was never in either version. C names a real difference that is not the important one."},{"q":"Which of these is a doubled word rather than another kind of redundancy?","options":["In the near future, where a four word phrase is standing in for the single word soon","Aid and abet, where two words are joined and either one would carry the meaning alone","Close proximity, where the word proximity already tells the reader that closeness is meant","Free gift, where a gift is by definition something the reader does not have to pay for"],"answer":1,"explain":"A doubled word is a pair joined by and where either half would do, which is B. A is a phrase doing the work of one word. C and D are both cases where the sentence already implies the cut word, which is a different kind again. Naming the kind is the skill, because it is what makes the cut repeatable next week and on someone else's writing."},{"q":"You cut a document by a third and a reader who tests both versions finds the shorter one harder to follow. What is the most likely explanation?","options":["The reader is unrepresentative, since most readers prefer shorter documents in nearly every case","The cut went too far and should be reversed, because a third is more than any document can safely lose","Some of what was cut was doing cohesive work rather than padding, most likely in the topic positions","The document was already concise, so any cut at all would have made it harder to follow"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson's wrinkle is that repetition in the topic position holds a passage together and looks exactly like padding, so C is the explanation to check first. A dismisses the only evidence available. B fixes a symptom by undoing everything rather than finding which cuts hurt. D assumes a conclusion the test does not support."},{"q":"You are cutting a colleague's report and you reach the phrase \"on an ongoing basis\". You cannot decide whether it is padding. What should you do next?","options":["Read the sentence without it, and ask what a reader has lost when it is gone","Keep it, since the risk of cutting something load bearing outweighs a slightly longer report","Count the syllables in both versions and prefer whichever version has fewer of them overall","Cut it, because a phrase whose purpose you cannot name is by definition not doing any work"],"answer":0,"explain":"The test is to remove it and see what the reader loses, which is A, and it produces an answer about this phrase rather than a policy about all of them. Here the answer is usually that nothing is lost and the phrase is a phrase for a word, but the point is that you found out. B and D are opposite blanket rules, and both replace the test with a default. C measures a surface feature, which lesson 1 already established is not what clarity is made of."}]},"07-shape-at-the-document-level":{"content":"<p>Lesson 1 established that a reader takes in about a fifth of an average page, and gets through half the information only on pages of about 111 words or fewer. That finding has been driving everything since, but it has been driving it at the level of sentences and paragraphs, which quietly assumes the reader is reading.</p>\n<p>Often they are not. They are looking. They have opened a document to find one thing, and they are moving down it at speed, and prose is not what they are using to navigate.</p>\n<p>What they are using is the part of writing that most writing courses do not teach, on the grounds that it belongs to somebody else. The US federal plain-language guide does not treat it that way: one of its four parts is <a href=\"https://digital.gov/guides/plain-language/design\">Design for understanding</a>, a quarter of the whole subject, and its opening claim is blunt. <strong>&quot;Dense, cluttered writing deters people from taking the time to read.&quot;</strong></p>\n<h2>Headings are the document a skimmer actually reads</h2>\n<p>Here is the test for a heading, and it disqualifies most of the headings you have written.</p>\n<p><strong>A heading has to give a reader enough to decide whether to stop.</strong></p>\n<p>Now look at the ones everybody uses. <em>Background. Context. Introduction. Next steps. Considerations. About this document.</em> Every one of them is a category label. Every one of them would fit almost any document in the world, which means none of them tells a particular reader anything about this one.</p>\n<p>The federal guidance asks for two things: &quot;use heading levels consistently and appropriately to create hierarchy&quot;, and &quot;write heading terms that are clear and descriptive&quot;. The second is the one people fail. Descriptive means it describes <em>this content</em>, not the kind of content.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Category label</th>\n<th>Same section, described</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Background</td>\n<td>Why the old system was replaced in April</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Next steps</td>\n<td>What we need from you by 14 March</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Considerations</td>\n<td>Three things that could delay this</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Analysis</td>\n<td>Costs came in eleven percent over budget</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>About this scheme</td>\n<td>Who can apply, and who cannot</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Read down the right-hand column on its own. You now know roughly what that document says, which is a thing you cannot do with the left-hand column at any length.</p>\n<div class=\"think predict\"><b>Predict first</b><p class=\"think-q\">A report has these headings: Introduction, Methodology, Findings, Discussion, Conclusion, Appendices. Is that wrong?</p><details><summary>Show the answer</summary><p>Not necessarily, and this is worth being careful about. In a scientific paper those headings are a convention that readers know how to use, and a reader looking for the method knows exactly where to go. The labels are doing navigational work precisely because the structure is standardised across thousands of papers.</p>\n<p>The failure is when those headings are imported into a document with no such convention behind it, which is where most of them live: internal reports, proposals, briefing notes, project updates. There, &quot;Findings&quot; tells a reader nothing, because they have no idea what kind of finding to expect.</p>\n<p>So the rule is not that category headings are banned. It is that a category heading is only doing work when the category is a convention your reader already navigates by. Otherwise it is a word occupying the most valuable line on the page.</p>\n</details></div>\n\n<h2>Prose, list, or table</h2>\n<p>These three are not stylistic choices. Each one makes a different claim about the content, and choosing the wrong one tells the reader something untrue.</p>\n<p><strong>Prose</strong> says: these things are related, and the relations matter. Because, therefore, although, unless. Prose is the only form that can carry an argument, because an argument is made of joins.</p>\n<p><strong>A list</strong> says: these items are parallel and independent. Each one stands on its own and could be reordered without loss. The federal guide&#39;s advice is to &quot;use lists for easy scanning&quot;, and that is exactly what parallel independent items are good for.</p>\n<p><strong>A table</strong> says: this has two dimensions. Every item is described by the same set of properties, and the reader will want to compare across them. The guidance is to &quot;where appropriate, use tables to make complex relationships clear&quot;, and the operative word is relationships, in the plural.</p>\n<p>The choice follows from asking one question: <strong>what relationship does this content actually have?</strong> Not what would look tidier.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><b>One extra distinction inside lists</b><p>A bulleted list claims the items are parallel and unordered. A numbered list claims they are in a sequence that matters.</p>\n<p>Numbering a set of unordered options implies a priority that is not there, and a reader will look for it. Bulleting a set of steps that must happen in order throws away the part that mattered most. Neither is a formatting preference; each is a claim about the content.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Worked example: same words, restructured</h2>\n<p>Here is a note as it arrived. Every word of it is fine.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The consultation on the parking scheme closed on 30 April. We received 412 responses, which is roughly four times what we normally see, and about a third of them came in during the final week after the local paper covered it. The main objection, raised in 260 responses, was the cost of a residents&#39; permit. The second most common, in 140 responses, was the proposed boundary, particularly on the east side. A smaller number, 38, objected to the enforcement hours. We are proposing to reduce the permit charge from £90 to £60, to move the eastern boundary to exclude Mill Lane, and to leave the enforcement hours as consulted. The revised scheme will go to committee on 12 June. If members want a different option on the permit charge, we would need to know before 28 May in order to model it.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Now the same words, given shape. Nothing is rewritten.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>The consultation closed on 30 April with 412 responses, four times the usual</strong></p>\n<p>About a third arrived in the final week, after the local paper covered it.</p>\n<p><strong>What people objected to</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Objection</th>\n<th>Responses</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Cost of a residents&#39; permit</td>\n<td>260</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The proposed boundary, particularly the east side</td>\n<td>140</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enforcement hours</td>\n<td>38</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>What we propose to change</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reduce the permit charge from £90 to £60</li>\n<li>Move the eastern boundary to exclude Mill Lane</li>\n<li>Leave the enforcement hours as consulted</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What we need from members, and by when</strong></p>\n<p>The revised scheme goes to committee on 12 June. If members want a different option on the permit charge, we need to know <strong>before 28 May</strong> so it can be modelled.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Three things happened, and each was a decision about content rather than about looks.</p>\n<p>The three objections became a <strong>table</strong> because they are one kind of thing measured on one axis, and the reader&#39;s question is which is biggest. The three proposals became a <strong>bulleted list</strong> because they are parallel and independent, and reordering them would lose nothing. The last section stayed as <strong>prose</strong> because it contains a condition and a consequence, which is a relation, and a list would have severed it.</p>\n<p>And the headings tell the story on their own. Read only the four of them and you know what the note says.</p>\n<h2>The wrinkle: bullet points can destroy an argument</h2>\n<p>Bullets are the default in a great many workplaces, applied to everything, and here is what they cost when applied to the wrong thing.</p>\n<p>Original:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>We should delay the launch until July. The regional teams have flagged capacity problems over the summer, and although the Q2 figures are strong, they are strong in exactly the regions that would be short-staffed. A May launch would therefore put our thinnest support cover against our highest demand.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Bulleted:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>We should delay the launch until July.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Regional teams have flagged capacity problems over the summer</li>\n<li>Q2 figures are strong</li>\n<li>The strong regions are the ones that would be short-staffed</li>\n<li>A May launch puts thin support cover against high demand</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Every fact survives. The argument does not.</p>\n<p>In the original, <em>although</em> tells you the second fact cuts against the case and is being answered. <em>Exactly</em> tells you the third fact is the answer. <em>Therefore</em> tells you the fourth is the conclusion. In the bulleted version those three words are gone, and four claims sit side by side as if they were four separate observations of equal weight. A reader now has to reconstruct which one is the objection and which is the reply.</p>\n<p><strong>A list is a claim that the items are parallel and independent.</strong> When they are not, the list is a false statement about the content, and the falsehood is invisible because it lives in the formatting rather than in any sentence.</p>\n<div class=\"think checkpoint\"><b>Check yourself</b><p class=\"think-q\">For each of these, decide the form, and say what relationship the content has.</p><details><summary>Show the answer</summary><p><strong>The five documents someone must attach to an application.</strong> A bulleted list. Parallel, independent, order does not matter, and the reader is going to use it as a checklist.</p>\n<p><strong>How three pension options compare on charges, access and risk.</strong> A table. Two dimensions, three options against three properties, and the reader&#39;s whole purpose is to compare across.</p>\n<p><strong>Why a project was late.</strong> Prose. The reasons interact, some caused others, and one of them is probably the real one while the rest are context. All of that lives in the joins, and a list would flatten it into a set of equally weighted excuses.</p>\n<p><strong>The steps to reset a password.</strong> A numbered list. Parallel in form, strictly ordered in content, and the numbering is the part the reader uses when they lose their place halfway.</p>\n<p><strong>A single caveat that applies to everything above it.</strong> Prose, and probably one sentence. A one-item bulleted list is a bullet that has nothing to be parallel to, which is a common tic and always worth deleting.</p>\n</details></div>\n\n<h2>What people get wrong</h2>\n<p><strong>&quot;Bullets are always clearer.&quot;</strong> They are clearer for parallel independent items and worse for everything else. The bulleted argument above is the standard demonstration.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Headings are for long documents.&quot;</strong> A 300-word email with two informative headings is easier to act on than the same email in one block. The threshold is not length; it is whether the reader might want to go to one part.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Formatting is the designer&#39;s job.&quot;</strong> Deciding that three things are a table and three others are a list is deciding what relationship the content has. Nobody applying formatting afterwards can make that decision for you, because it is a claim about meaning.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;A table is for numbers.&quot;</strong> A table is for two dimensions. Plenty of good tables contain no numbers at all, including the heading table earlier in this lesson.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;White space is wasted space.&quot;</strong> The guide&#39;s own claim is that dense, cluttered writing deters people from reading at all, so space that gets someone to start is not waste.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;I will add the headings at the end.&quot;</strong> Headings written last tend to be category labels, because by then you are describing sections rather than telling the reader what is in them. Write them as you go and rewrite them at the end.</p>\n<h2>Practice</h2>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><b>Restructure without rewriting, then read the headings alone</b><p>Take the document you have carried since lesson 1, or a longer one if that has become short.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p><strong>Change no sentences in this exercise.</strong> You may move text, split it, and add headings. If you catch yourself rewriting, you are avoiding the exercise, because the point is to show how much can be gained without touching the prose.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Go through and mark every stretch where the content is a set of parallel independent items, and every stretch where it has two dimensions. Convert the first kind to lists and the second to tables. Leave everything with a <em>because</em> or an <em>although</em> in it as prose.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Give every section a heading that would let a reader decide whether to stop. Then check each one against the test: could this heading sit unchanged on somebody else&#39;s document about something else? If yes, it is a category label, and rewrite it.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Now the test.</strong> Write out your headings, in order, on their own, with nothing else. Read them. Do they tell the story? A reader who saw only those, which is close to what a skimming reader gets, would come away with what?</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>If the heading list does not tell the story, the problem is usually one of two things. Either a heading is a label, or the document&#39;s sections are not the shape of its argument. The second is more common and more useful to find.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Last, count. How many words did you move, and how many did you change? If the second number is not close to zero, do step 1 again on a fresh copy.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Where this goes next</h2>\n<p>Everything in the course so far has been a claim about readers, and you have taken those claims on trust from research this course has read for you. The next lesson turns round and looks at where writing rules come from in the first place, using the most famous writing book in English and the most famous attack on it.</p>\n<p>It is not a detour. The reason this course teaches what it teaches, and ignores a great deal of advice you have probably been given, is a claim about which kinds of rule are worth following. Lesson 8 makes that claim explicit and shows you how to test a rule yourself.</p>\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Digital.gov, <a href=\"https://digital.gov/guides/plain-language/design\">&quot;Design for understanding&quot;</a>, part of the US federal plain-language guide series. The quoted guidance on headings, lists and tables, and the claim that dense, cluttered writing deters people from reading. The guide also covers visuals and links, which this lesson does not.</li>\n<li>GOV.UK, <a href=\"https://guidance.publishing.service.gov.uk/writing-to-gov-uk-standards/writing-guidelines/clear-language/\">&quot;Use clear language&quot;</a>, on front-loading and on writing so that a reader can find what they need.</li>\n<li>The reading budget behind this lesson&#39;s premise comes from lesson 1, which derives it from Jakob Nielsen&#39;s <a href=\"https://www.nngroup.com/articles/how-little-do-users-read/\">&quot;How Little Do Users Read?&quot;</a> together with the caveats stated there.</li>\n</ul>\n","quiz":[{"q":"Which pair of headings would help a skimming reader most?","options":["Background and context, then Considerations and next steps for the coming period","Why we missed the deadline, then What we are changing so it does not happen again","Section one, introduction and scope, then Section two, analysis of the situation","About this document, then Further information and contact details for enquiries"],"answer":1,"explain":"A heading has to carry enough information for a reader to decide whether to stop, and B does that while A, C and D name categories that would fit almost any document. The federal guidance the lesson quotes asks for heading terms that are clear and descriptive, which is a test about content rather than about labelling."},{"q":"You have four items that must be done in a fixed order, where each depends on the one before. What form suits them?","options":["A bulleted list, since bullets are the standard way to present any set of items","A numbered list, because numbers carry the order that bullets would throw away","A table with four rows, so that the dependencies can be set out in a second column","Prose, because lists of any kind cannot express a dependency between two items"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson's test is what relationship the content has, and ordered dependency is exactly what numbering carries, so B fits. A discards the order, which is the content. C is heavier than four sequential steps need. D overstates, since a numbered list expresses sequence perfectly well."},{"q":"A writer turns three linked paragraphs of argument into eight bullet points. What is the most likely cost?","options":["The reader will find it harder to skim, because bullets slow a reader down","Nothing is lost, since the same sentences appear in both versions of the document","The relations between the points are lost, because a list presents items as parallel","The document becomes longer, since bullet formatting takes up more space on the page"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson treats a list as a claim that its items are parallel and independent, so bulleting an argument throws away the because and therefore that were the content. That is C. A has it backwards, since bullets aid skimming. B ignores that the connective tissue was in the joins. D names a real but trivial effect."},{"q":"What does this lesson say a table is for?","options":["Presenting numbers, which are hard for readers to compare when written out in prose","Making a document look more professional and considered than plain paragraphs do","Breaking up long stretches of text so that a page is less daunting to approach","Showing a relationship with two dimensions, where each item is described on the same axes"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson's test is whether the content has two dimensions, so that every row is described by the same columns, which is D and matches the guidance about using tables to make complex relationships clear. A is one common case rather than the principle. B and C treat a table as decoration."},{"q":"How does this lesson suggest you test a restructured document?","options":["Read the headings on their own, in order, and see whether they tell the story","Count the words before and after, and check that the restructure did not lengthen it","Read it aloud from start to finish, listening for places where the rhythm breaks","Check that no section runs longer than the roughly 111 words a reader half-finishes"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lesson's test is the headings read alone, because that is close to what a skimming reader actually receives, which is A. B measures the wrong thing for a restructure that moves rather than cuts. C is a useful habit for prose and does not test structure. D misapplies lesson 1's figure, which is about a whole page rather than a section."},{"q":"A colleague says formatting is the designer's job and the writer should hand over plain paragraphs. What does this lesson say?","options":["Agreed, since a writer who formats their own work usually produces something inconsistent","Agreed in principle, though writers should still suggest where headings might go","Disagreed, because deciding what is a list and what is a heading is deciding what the content is","Disagreed, because designers rarely have time to restructure documents properly before publication"],"answer":2,"explain":"Choosing between prose, a list and a table is a claim about what relationship the content has, so it cannot be delegated to someone applying formatting afterwards. That is C. A and B concede the premise. D disagrees for a practical reason rather than the one the lesson gives, which is that the decision is about meaning."}]},"08-rules-and-where-they-come-from":{"content":"<p>Everything in this course so far has asked you to trust it. Readers take in about a fifth of a page. Readers expect a subject to be followed by its verb. Readers emphasise what arrives last. Those are claims about people, and you have taken them from research this course read on your behalf.</p>\n<p>Meanwhile you have spent your life being handed a different kind of writing advice. Never split an infinitive. Never start a sentence with <em>but</em>. Never use the passive. Those usually arrive with no evidence attached, and sometimes with the suggestion that a person who breaks them is careless.</p>\n<p>This lesson is about the difference, and about how you check for yourself. It uses the most famous writing book in English and the most famous attack on it. The outcome is messier than either side would like, which is the point.</p>\n<h2>The passive, since it is the one everyone thinks they know</h2>\n<p>Start here, because the passive is the rule most often repeated by people who cannot apply it.</p>\n<p><strong>A passive clause takes the thing acted upon and makes it the subject.</strong> In English it is built from a form of <em>be</em> (occasionally <em>get</em>) plus a past participle, and it can optionally name the actor in a <em>by</em> phrase. &quot;The bill was paid&quot; is passive. &quot;The bill was paid by an anonymous benefactor&quot; is the same clause with the actor restored.</p>\n<p>The test: could you add <em>by someone</em> without breaking the sentence? If yes, it is passive.</p>\n<p>Geoffrey Pullum, in <a href=\"https://pullum.ppls.ed.ac.uk/50years.pdf\">&quot;50 Years of Stupid Grammar Advice&quot;</a>, reports what has happened to that distinction:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&quot;It is typical for college graduates today to be unable to distinguish active from passive clauses. They often equate the grammatical notion of being passive with the semantic one of not specifying the agent of an action. (They think &#39;a bus exploded&#39; is passive because it doesn&#39;t say whether terrorists did it.)&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>&quot;A bus exploded&quot; is as active as a sentence can be. The bus is the subject and it is doing the exploding. What people react to is that nobody is blamed, which is a different complaint, often a fair one, and not about voice.</p>\n<div class=\"think predict\"><b>Predict first</b><p class=\"think-q\">Which of these four are passive? &quot;The window was broken.&quot; &quot;The window was old.&quot; &quot;The report was written by the committee.&quot; &quot;The committee got criticised.&quot;</p><details><summary>Show the answer</summary><p>The first, third and fourth. <em>Was broken</em> is <em>be</em> plus a past participle, and you could add <em>by someone</em>. <em>Was written by the committee</em> is the textbook case with its actor named. <em>Got criticised</em> is the <em>get</em> passive, which is informal and perfectly real.</p>\n<p>&quot;The window was old&quot; is not passive. <em>Old</em> is an adjective and you cannot add <em>by someone</em>. This is the same trap as <em>his health became impaired</em>: <em>impaired</em> looks like a participle and is working as an adjective, and <em>become</em> cannot take a passive at all.</p>\n</details></div>\n\n<h3>And when the passive is right</h3>\n<p>Lesson 5 gave you this already. <strong>The passive exists so that you can put something other than the actor in the topic position.</strong> If the paragraph is about the patient, &quot;she was discharged on the fourth day&quot; is correct, and the active version quietly changes the subject to the consultant.</p>\n<p>Pullum&#39;s illustration shows the complaint is about information order rather than voice:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&quot;For me to report that I paid my bill by saying &#39;The bill was paid by me,&#39; with no stress on &#39;me,&#39; would sound inane. (I&#39;m the utterer, and the utterer always counts as familiar and well established in the discourse.) But that is no argument against passives generally. &#39;The bill was paid by an anonymous benefactor&#39; sounds perfectly natural.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>The book, and the attack</h2>\n<p><strong>The book.</strong> <em>The Elements of Style</em> began as a slim volume William Strunk printed privately for his Cornell students in 1918. E. B. White, who had taken the class in 1919, revised and expanded it for commercial publication in 1959. It has sold millions.</p>\n<p>Its defenders hold that it is a compact set of maxims that has demonstrably helped generations of novices, that its brevity is precisely its virtue, and that a beginner who follows it writes better than a beginner who follows nothing. That case is not silly, and nothing below refutes it.</p>\n<p><strong>Strunk&#39;s own aim</strong>, from <a href=\"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/37134/pg37134.txt\">the 1918 text</a>, is modest: the book &quot;aims to give in brief space the principal requirements of plain English style&quot;, concentrating on &quot;essentials, the rules of usage and principles of composition most commonly violated&quot;. It does not claim to be a grammar.</p>\n<p><strong>The attack.</strong> Pullum&#39;s essay marked the book&#39;s fiftieth anniversary in 2009. He is head of linguistics and English language at the University of Edinburgh and co-author of <em>The Cambridge Grammar of the English Language</em>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&quot;The Elements of Style does not deserve the enormous esteem in which it is held by American college graduates. Its advice ranges from limp platitudes to inconsistent nonsense. Its enormous influence has not improved American students&#39; grasp of English grammar; it has significantly degraded it.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<div class=\"callout\"><b>Two things Pullum says that are usually cut</b><p>Quote the paragraph above alone and you get a cruder argument than the one he made.</p>\n<p><strong>First, he is not attacking the style advice.</strong> What he objects to &quot;is not the style advice in <em>Elements</em>, which might best be described the way The Hitchhiker&#39;s Guide to the Galaxy describes Earth: mostly harmless.&quot; He calls some of it vapid, some tautologous, some useless, then says &quot;it doesn&#39;t hurt to lay such well-meant maxims before novice writers.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Second, he says which half of the case is his.</strong> After noting that writing tutors &quot;simply red-circle everything that looks like a passive&quot;, he writes: &quot;That overinterpretation is part of the damage that Strunk and White have unintentionally done. <strong>But it is not what I am most concerned about here.</strong> What concerns me is that the bias against the passive is being retailed by a pair of authors so grammatically clueless that they don&#39;t know what is a passive construction and what isn&#39;t.&quot;</p>\n<p>So his charge is about competence, and the damage done by tutors is the part he sets aside. A response that concedes the tutors and disputes the competence has answered the half he disclaimed.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>His evidence, in two parts</h2>\n<p><strong>Part one, the misdiagnosed examples.</strong> Of the four pairs under the heading &quot;Use the active voice&quot;, he says &quot;a staggering three out of the four are mistaken diagnoses&quot;, and works through them. On one, note his hedge: &quot;&#39;The reason that he left college was that his health became impaired&#39; is <strong>presumably</strong> fingered as passive because of &#39;impaired,&#39; but that&#39;s a mistake.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Part two, and this is the stronger half.</strong> He argues the book cannot keep its own rules, on the same page as it states them:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&quot;&#39;Write with nouns and verbs, not with adjectives and adverbs,&#39; they insist. (The motivation of this mysterious decree remains unclear to me.) And then, in the very next sentence, comes a negative passive clause containing three adjectives: &#39;The adjective hasn&#39;t been built that can pull a weak or inaccurate noun out of a tight place.&#39;&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>He does the same to &quot;Keep related words together&quot;, whose own statement of the rule separates a subject from its verb, and concludes: &quot;the grammatical advice proffered in <em>Elements</em> is so misplaced and inaccurate that counterexamples often show up in the authors&#39; own prose on the very same page.&quot;</p>\n<p>That is the argument to answer, and this course cannot check it, because those sentences are White&#39;s 1959 text and this course has not read it.</p>\n<h2>Opening the primary text, and what it actually shows</h2>\n<p>So take the half that can be checked. Strunk&#39;s 1918 rule 10 is out of copyright and available in full.</p>\n<p>Read it and the first thing you find is that Pullum has read it too, and says so:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&quot;After this unpromising start, there is some fairly sensible style advice: The authors explicitly say they do not mean &#39;that the writer should entirely discard the passive voice,&#39; which is &#39;frequently convenient and sometimes necessary.&#39; They give good examples to show that the choice between active and passive may depend on the topic under discussion.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>He quotes the qualification and credits it. He also grants the topic-dependence point, which is the same observation lesson 5 built on. <strong>Anyone who tells you Pullum overlooked the moderation in the passive rule has not read his essay.</strong></p>\n<p>What the 1918 text does add is the sentence that introduces the disputed examples:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&quot;Many a tame sentence of description or exposition can be made lively and emphatic by substituting a verb in the active voice for <strong>some such perfunctory expression as <em>there is</em>, or <em>could be heard</em>.</strong>&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Strunk names the fault himself, and it is the limp existential <em>there is</em> and the perfunctory <em>could be heard</em>, not the passive. That is consistent with Pullum&#39;s own &quot;presumably&quot;: he is inferring what the examples are offered as, from their position under an active-voice heading, rather than reporting a label the book applies. Both readings are available from the page. His is the natural one for a reader who trusts headings, which is most readers, and that is the whole mechanism of the damage he describes.</p>\n<div class=\"think predict\"><b>Predict first</b><p class=\"think-q\">Two of Pullum&#39;s specific charges are about rules that are not in the 1918 book at all. Which, and what follows?</p><details><summary>Show the answer</summary><p>The rule that <em>none</em> takes a singular verb, and the ban on <em>which</em> in restrictive relative clauses. Neither appears in Strunk&#39;s original. Rule 3 simply distinguishes restrictive from non-restrictive clauses and punctuates them.</p>\n<p>What follows is that those two charges land on White rather than on Strunk. Pullum says as much about the second, that White &quot;not only added the anti-&#39;which&#39; rule to the book but also revised away the counterexamples that were present in his old professor&#39;s original text&quot;, and the 1918 text is consistent with that.</p>\n<p>It also means the book people argue about is a collaboration between a teacher and his student, forty years apart, and &quot;Strunk and White&quot; is doing a lot of work as a single name.</p>\n</details></div>\n\n<h3>The finding that cuts the other way</h3>\n<p>Opening the text is only worth doing if you report what you find, including the parts that go against the side you started on.</p>\n<p>Pullum writes that &quot;Strunk&#39;s original version never even mentioned split infinitives.&quot; <strong>It does.</strong> The 1918 glossary carries an entry:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&quot;<strong>Split Infinitive.</strong> There is precedent from the fourteenth century downward for interposing an adverb between <em>to</em> and the infinitive which it governs, but the construction is in disfavor and is avoided by nearly all careful writers.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>And the <em>however</em> rule, which this lesson is about to use as its specimen of an untested prohibition, is Strunk&#39;s too: &quot;<strong>However.</strong> In the meaning <em>nevertheless</em>, not to come first in its sentence or clause.&quot;</p>\n<p>So the primary text corrects the critic as well as complicating the charge. Neither of these is fatal to Pullum&#39;s case, which does not rest on them. Both are the kind of thing you only ever find by opening the book, which is the actual lesson here.</p>\n<p><strong>Where that leaves the dispute.</strong> Pullum&#39;s central charge rests mainly on the same-page self-contradictions, which are quoted from a text this course has not read and which look serious as quoted. On the examples, the 1918 wording complicates the charge without refuting it, and he hedged it himself. Two of his sharpest specific hits belong to White. One of his incidental claims is wrong. The damage he documents, which he does not claim as his main point, is real and is not in dispute.</p>\n<p>If that is unsatisfying, good. It is what the evidence supports.</p>\n<h2>The method, which is the actual lesson</h2>\n<p>Notice what moved the argument. Not authority, not intuition, not asking which writer is more distinguished. The text, opened, by someone willing to publish what they found.</p>\n<p>Pullum uses the same move on the <em>none</em> rule. He searches three books by writers publishing while Strunk was teaching and finds plural agreement in each: Wilde&#39;s <em>The Importance of Being Earnest</em> has &quot;None of us are perfect&quot;; Stoker&#39;s <em>Dracula</em> has &quot;I think that none of us were surprised when we were asked to see Mrs. Harker a little before the time of sunset&quot;; Montgomery&#39;s <em>Anne of Avonlea</em> has &quot;None of us ever do&quot;. His question is the one to steal: &quot;Is the intelligent student supposed to believe that Stoker, Wilde, and Montgomery didn&#39;t know how to write?&quot;</p>\n<p>The method in four steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>State the rule precisely.</strong> Not &quot;however is wrong at the start of a sentence&quot; but &quot;however, meaning nevertheless, should not begin a sentence or clause&quot;. Vague rules cannot be tested, and vagueness is how bad rules survive.</li>\n<li><strong>Find a corpus.</strong> Pullum names Project Gutenberg, which is free, searchable, and full of writers nobody accuses of illiteracy.</li>\n<li><strong>Count, both ways.</strong> Mark Liberman checked half a dozen of Mark Twain&#39;s books and found roughly seven sentence-initial <em>howevers</em> for each three placed after the subject; in five books by Henry James the ratio was one to fifteen.</li>\n<li><strong>Say what the numbers can and cannot show.</strong> A rule contradicted by the usage of good writers of its own period is not a rule of English. It may still be a house style, which is a different and legitimate thing.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>What people get wrong</h2>\n<p><strong>&quot;Never split an infinitive.&quot;</strong> The split infinitive has always been grammatical. The reason usually given is also backwards: the claim is that splitting places unusual stress on the adverb, and Pullum shows that tucking the adverb in before the verb de-emphasises it. That is lesson 5&#39;s stress position doing the work.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Never start a sentence with however, or but, or and.&quot;</strong> Contradicted by counting, which is why counting is in this lesson.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Never use which for a restrictive clause.&quot;</strong> Pullum: &quot;There was never a period in the history of English when &#39;which&#39; at the beginning of a restrictive relative clause was an error.&quot; Some publishers enforce it as house style, which is their right.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;A style book contains the rules of English.&quot;</strong> <em>Elements</em> contains one teacher&#39;s list of what his students most often got wrong in 1918, plus a later writer&#39;s additions. Strunk said as much himself.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;So the book is worthless.&quot;</strong> The evidence does not support that. Its style advice survives the attack, its most famous maxim is quoted approvingly in lesson 6 of this course, and its own author states the topic-position principle in 1918.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;So the attack is unfair.&quot;</strong> Nor that. Pullum&#39;s central evidence is quoted from the text and has not been answered here, and the damage he describes is real.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Following the rules makes you a fool.&quot;</strong> Nothing in this lesson supports that either. Most people who repeat these rules were taught them by someone they trusted, which is how nearly everybody learns nearly everything.</p>\n<h2>Practice</h2>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><b>Test a rule you were taught</b><p>Pick a writing rule somebody gave you that you have never checked. Everyone has three or four. Not one from this course.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p><strong>Write it out precisely</strong>, in a form that could be true or false.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Find its source.</strong> Where did you get it, and where did they get it? Often the trail ends in somebody&#39;s memory of a rule, which is itself a finding.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Choose a corpus and search it.</strong> Project Gutenberg for older writing, or a body of contemporary writing you respect. Pick writers nobody would call careless.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Count, both ways.</strong> How often is the rule followed, and how often broken? By whom?</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Write your verdict in one paragraph</strong>, and say which of three things you found: a rule of English, a house style, or a superstition. All three are real categories and only the third is worthless.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Report anything that went against you.</strong> If you set out to debunk and found the rule holds, say so. A method that only ever debunks is not a method, and this lesson&#39;s own case study turned up a finding against the critic.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Where this goes next</h2>\n<p>You now have the craft this course teaches, and a way of judging advice you meet later, including advice from here.</p>\n<p>One thing remains, and almost no writing course includes it. Everything so far has been about producing a better document. The last lesson is about finding out whether it worked, which is a different activity with different tools, and it involves another person.</p>\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>William Strunk Jr., <a href=\"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/37134/pg37134.txt\"><em>The Elements of Style</em></a> (privately printed, 1918), public domain and read in full for this course. Rule 10 for the active voice material, its qualification about the passive, the &quot;perfunctory expression&quot; sentence and the Restoration dramatists pair; rule 3 for restrictive and non-restrictive clauses; the glossary for the split infinitive and <em>however</em> entries.</li>\n<li>Geoffrey K. Pullum, <a href=\"https://pullum.ppls.ed.ac.uk/50years.pdf\">&quot;50 Years of Stupid Grammar Advice&quot;</a>, <em>The Chronicle of Higher Education</em>, 17 April 2009. Every Pullum quotation in this lesson is from that essay, including his concession that the style advice is mostly harmless, his statement of which half of the case is his, the same-page self-contradiction evidence, the <em>none</em> and <em>which</em> material, and Mark Liberman&#39;s counts of <em>however</em> in Twain and James.</li>\n<li><strong>E. B. White&#39;s 1959 revision has not been read by this course</strong>, and is in copyright. Every statement above about what White added or wrote rests on Pullum&#39;s account or on the absence of the material from the 1918 text. That also means White&#39;s defenders are unheard here, not only his book unread.</li>\n<li>George D. Gopen and Judith A. Swan, <a href=\"https://wstomv.win.tue.nl/quotes/science-of-scientific-writing.html\">&quot;The Science of Scientific Writing&quot;</a> (1990), the model of the other kind of rule, the kind that describes what readers do. Their own caveat, that &quot;none of these reader-expectation principles should be considered &#39;rules&#39;&quot;, is the sentence this lesson is built around.</li>\n</ul>\n","quiz":[{"q":"Which of these is a passive clause?","options":["A bus exploded outside the station shortly after nine in the morning","The reason he left college was that his health became impaired that year","There were a great number of dead leaves lying on the ground by then","At dawn the crowing of a rooster could be heard from across the valley"],"answer":3,"explain":"D has a form of be with a past participle, heard, and you could add by someone, which is the test. That is a passive. A names no agent and many people call it passive for that reason, but it is a plain active clause. B has become plus an adjective, and become cannot take a passive. C is an existential there sentence with no passive anywhere in it."},{"q":"What is Pullum's central charge against the book, in his own account of it?","options":["That writing tutors overinterpret its advice and red-circle anything resembling a passive","That its authors condemn constructions they are not themselves able to identify correctly","That its style advice is vapid, tautologous and useless to any novice writer who reads it","That its brevity forces it into rules that a longer treatment would have properly qualified"],"answer":1,"explain":"Pullum names the tutors' overinterpretation and then says plainly that it is not what he is most concerned about, so A is the half he sets aside. His concern is B. C misstates his position, since he calls the style advice mostly harmless and says it does not hurt to put such maxims before novices. D is an argument he never makes."},{"q":"Which two of Pullum's specific charges turn out to land on White rather than on Strunk?","options":["The rule that none takes a singular verb, and the ban on which in restrictive clauses","The bias against the passive, and the instruction to omit every needless word from a draft","The claim about split infinitives, and the rule against however at the start of a sentence","The instruction to write with nouns and verbs, and the advice not to inject opinion"],"answer":0,"explain":"Neither the none rule nor the anti-which rule is in the 1918 edition, so both belong to White's revision. That is A. B names material Strunk wrote. C is the trap, since 1918 does carry a split infinitive entry and does carry the however rule, which is the finding that cuts against Pullum. D names two items that are indeed White's, so a learner who picks it has the right idea and the wrong pair."},{"q":"A rule says never begin a sentence with however meaning nevertheless. How would this lesson have you test it?","options":["Ask several professional editors whether they follow the rule in their own writing","Check whether the rule appears in more than one respected style guide of its period","Count how writers of that period actually placed however, in texts you can search","Consider whether sentences that begin with however sound awkward when read aloud"],"answer":2,"explain":"The method the lesson teaches is Pullum's own, which is to take the rule to a corpus and count, and the lesson names Project Gutenberg as the corpus. That is C. A and B both substitute authority for evidence, which is the habit the lesson exists to break. D substitutes the writer's own ear, which cannot settle a claim about how a language is used."},{"q":"When is the passive the right choice, on the account this course gives?","options":["When the writer would rather not say who was responsible for the action described","When the thing acted on is what the paragraph is about, so it holds the topic position","When a document is formal, because the passive is the register that formality calls for","When the sentence would otherwise be too short to carry the weight of what it reports"],"answer":1,"explain":"Lesson 5 established that the passive is how you put something other than the actor in the topic position, and Strunk says the same thing in 1918. That is B. A is the misuse lesson 3 examined, where the vagueness was doing work. C and D treat the passive as a matter of register or of padding rather than of what the paragraph is about."},{"q":"What does this lesson conclude about the dispute over The Elements of Style?","options":["That the book should be ignored, since its authors could not identify a passive clause","That its critics are unfair, because its rules hold up whenever they are properly tested","That opening the primary text moved the argument in both directions rather than one","That the question cannot be settled, so a writer may follow whichever side they prefer"],"answer":2,"explain":"The lesson finds two of Pullum's charges belong to White rather than Strunk, and also finds Strunk's 1918 text contradicting Pullum on split infinitives, so the evidence cuts both ways. That is C. A takes only the attack and B only the defence. D declines to weigh evidence the lesson has just weighed, which is the move the whole lesson argues against."}]},"09-revising-and-testing-it":{"content":"<p>You have taken a document through eight lessons. You know where its point should go, what its sentences should look like, which of its words are doing nothing, and how it should be shaped for someone who is scanning.</p>\n<p>You still do not know whether anyone understands it.</p>\n<p>That sounds like a small remaining gap and it is the whole thing. Every judgement you have made so far has been made by the one person in the world who cannot read the document fresh: you know what it was supposed to say, and your own knowledge fills every hole as you reread. Lesson 4 used that fact to explain why you cannot feel a long wait between a subject and its verb. It applies to everything else too.</p>\n<p>So this last lesson is about the two activities that come after writing, which are not the same activity, and about the step almost nobody takes.</p>\n<h2>Revising is not editing</h2>\n<p>These get used interchangeably, and treating them as one thing is the commonest way a draft gets worse instead of better.</p>\n<p><strong>Revising is structural.</strong> What is this document for, is that still what it is doing, is the argument in the right order, does that whole section need to exist. Revising can delete a third of a draft.</p>\n<p><strong>Editing is local.</strong> The sentence, the word, the comma. Everything lessons 3 to 6 taught you.</p>\n<p><strong>Revising comes first</strong>, and the reason is not taste. If you edit first, you will spend an hour polishing sentences in a section you are about to cut. The work is not merely wasted; it makes the cut harder, because nobody likes deleting a paragraph they have just perfected.</p>\n<p>Nancy Sommers made the same complaint about how the subject was taught. In her <a href=\"https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED220839.pdf\">1982 report to the National Institute of Education</a> she examined fifteen composition textbooks and found they &quot;yield 23 terms used synonymously with revision&quot;. Her diagnosis of what those books teach is worth having exactly:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Revision is equated with cleanliness: &quot;to groom, to polish, to order, and to tidy-up one&#39;s writing.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>And structurally, that revision is &quot;that interlude after you finish writing the first draft and before you type the paper.&quot;</p>\n<p>Both halves are wrong, and between them they describe what most people actually do.</p>\n<h2>What experienced writers do differently</h2>\n<p>Sommers studied this directly. Eight first-year students and seven experienced adult writers each wrote three compositions, rewrote each of them twice, suggested revisions to a composition by an anonymous author, and were interviewed three times. The students wrote in class; the adults wrote at home or in their offices.</p>\n<p>Her central finding, in her own words:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&quot;The student writers have operational procedures for revising and reasons to explain their procedures, but ... they have not codified or synthesized the procedures into a theory of the revision process. The experienced writers, on the other hand, have a codified set of principles about how their revision process works.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Read that carefully, because it is not the finding people expect. The students were not lazy and they were not without method. They had procedures and they could explain them. What they lacked was any account of what revision is <em>for</em>, which meant they could not tell which procedure a given draft needed.</p>\n<p>The second half is sharper still:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&quot;For the experienced writers revision was not a stage but rather a process that occurred throughout their writing. Their first drafts were already the results of an elaborate revision process.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>So the neat sequence, write then revise then edit, describes neither group well. Sommers says the evidence &quot;calls for a rejection of the linear stage theory&quot;, and proposes instead that writers &quot;sense dissonance, tolerate dissonance, and resolve dissonance&quot;.</p>\n<div class=\"think predict\"><b>Predict first</b><p class=\"think-q\">Two of her subjects, in their own words. What is the difference between them?</p><details><summary>Show the answer</summary><p><strong>Michael</strong>, a student. He rewords when a word is &quot;too childish&quot; or when he has repeated it, and his &quot;strategy is to use a thesaurus to improve the &#39;sophistication&#39; and thus the appropriateness of his writing&quot;.</p>\n<p><strong>Debra</strong>, an experienced writer. &quot;Rewriting means the deep structural changes, the content. Revising means the surface changes, word and phrases. Rewriting is the global process, revising the local process. In rewriting, I am more audience oriented.&quot;</p>\n<p>Michael is working at the level of the word, and his test is how the word sounds. Debra has two named activities with different scopes, and the larger of them is oriented to a reader. She is not more talented in that quotation. She has a distinction he does not have, and the distinction tells her what to do next.</p>\n<p>Note also what Michael&#39;s thesaurus habit costs, which lesson 6 warned about. Swapping a repeated word for a synonym leaves the reader working out whether the two words mean the same thing.</p>\n</details></div>\n\n<div class=\"callout\"><b>Sommers' coding scheme, which is useful on your own drafts</b><p>To analyse what her writers did, she classified every change by two things at once: the <strong>operation</strong>, which is &quot;deletion, substitution, addition, and reordering&quot;, and the <strong>level</strong>, which is &quot;word, phrase, sentence, and thema&quot;, a thema being &quot;an extended statement of one idea&quot;.</p>\n<p>Use it as a description rather than a checklist. Go through a revision you have made and mark each change. If nearly all of them are substitutions at the word level, you have edited and called it revising, and that is Michael. If a draft needed structural work and got a hundred word-level substitutions, the scheme shows you that in about two minutes.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>The step almost nobody takes</h2>\n<p>Now the part that no popular writing course includes, and it is the only one that produces evidence rather than judgement.</p>\n<p><strong>Ask a real person what they think it says.</strong></p>\n<p>The US federal plain-language guide sets this out as <a href=\"https://digital.gov/guides/plain-language/test/paraphrase-testing\">paraphrase testing</a>, and it is deliberately cheap. What it gives you: it &quot;will tell you what a reader thinks a piece of writing means and will help you know if they&#39;re interpreting the message as you intended.&quot;</p>\n<p>How to run one:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Find between six and nine people.</strong> The guidance is explicit that this is enough: &quot;With only 6 to 9 participants, paraphrase testing will not take a lot of time, and the time invested is worth it.&quot; For a first attempt, one person is enough to change how you write.</li>\n<li><strong>Give them a section and let them read it.</strong> Not the whole document. One section at a time.</li>\n<li><strong>Ask them to tell you in their own words what that section means.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Write down what they say, in their words.</strong> The guidance is firm: &quot;Take notes, writing down the participant&#39;s explanation in the participant&#39;s words. <strong>Do not correct the participant.</strong>&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Ask what they would do</strong>, and what they think the writer was trying to achieve. The guidance warns off the obvious alternative: &quot;You won&#39;t get much usable information from questions that can be answered with &#39;yes&#39; or &#39;no&#39;.&quot; So never ask whether it was clear.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>That fourth step is the one people cannot do. Someone misreads your sentence, and every instinct says explain it. The moment you explain, the evidence is gone: you now have a reader who understands your document because you told them what it says, which is not a thing you can ship.</p>\n<p><strong>The misreading is the finding.</strong> It is the entire point of running the test.</p>\n<div class=\"think checkpoint\"><b>Check yourself</b><p class=\"think-q\">A reader paraphrases your section fluently, confidently, and wrong. Which of these is the right response, and why are the others wrong?</p><details><summary>Show the answer</summary><p><strong>(a) &quot;Ah, no, what it means is...&quot;</strong> Wrong, and it is the natural one. You have just destroyed the finding and taught your next question&#39;s answer.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Write down what they said, verbatim, and move to the next section.</strong> Right. You have the finding. Analysis comes later, when you look at your notes and find that three of your six readers made the same mistake, which points at a sentence rather than at a person.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) &quot;Was that section confusing?&quot;</strong> Wrong twice. It is answerable with yes or no, which the guidance warns against, and people who have just confidently misunderstood something do not report being confused, because they were not. Confusion is what a reader feels when they know they are lost.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Ask them to read it again more carefully.</strong> Wrong. Your document does not get a second attempt in the world. The first reading is the one you are testing.</p>\n</details></div>\n\n<h2>What a readability score cannot tell you</h2>\n<p>Given how cheap paraphrase testing is, it is worth being clear about why the popular alternative is not a substitute.</p>\n<p>Readability formulas count surface features: syllables per word, words per sentence. That is what they are made of, so a better score means those counts went down. It does not mean a reader understood more, and you can improve a score by splitting a sentence at a comma while changing nothing about what anyone takes away.</p>\n<p>Lesson 1 gave the deeper reason. Gooding, Berzak, Mak and Sharifi&#39;s <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.06354\">study of 518 readers</a> found that reader background shapes both how people read and what makes a text feel difficult to them. Difficulty is a relation between a text and a reader. A formula sees only one side of that relation, so it cannot in principle report the thing you wanted to know.</p>\n<p>None of which makes formulas useless. A score that suddenly jumps is a signal worth investigating, in the same way sentence length was a signal in lesson 4. A signal is not an outcome.</p>\n<h2>What people get wrong</h2>\n<p><strong>&quot;Revision means finding better words.&quot;</strong> This is Michael&#39;s position and it is the misconception this lesson exists to break. Word substitution is editing, and it is the last thing you do.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;I read it through again and it was fine.&quot;</strong> You are the one reader who cannot test this document. Rereading your own work tests your memory of what you meant.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Getting a colleague to read it is the same thing.&quot;</strong> Better than nothing and not the same. A colleague on your team shares your assumptions, your jargon and your knowledge of the project, which are exactly the things you are trying to test for.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;I do not have six to nine people.&quot;</strong> Use one. The difference between one reader and none is far larger than the difference between one and nine.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Testing is for websites and forms.&quot;</strong> Paraphrase testing works on anything short enough to read in sections, which includes almost every document you write.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;If it needs testing, it was badly written.&quot;</strong> Every document is written by someone who already knows what it says. That is not a flaw in the writer; it is the condition of writing.</p>\n<h2>Practice</h2>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><b>Run one paraphrase test, on one page, with one person</b><p>This is the last exercise in the course and the only one that requires another human being. Do not substitute imagining what they would say.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p><strong>Take one page</strong> of the document you have carried since lesson 1, in the state the last eight lessons have left it. Split it into two or three sections.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Find one person</strong> who does not know the subject and did not help you write it. Tell them you are testing the writing, not them, and that there are no wrong answers because you are the one being tested.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>One section at a time</strong>, let them read it, then ask them to tell you in their own words what it means. Then ask what they would do if they received this document.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Write down what they say in their words.</strong> Do not correct them. Do not explain. Do not defend a sentence. If this is hard, that difficulty is worth noticing, because it is the same instinct that made you sure the document was clear.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Afterwards</strong>, for every gap between what they said and what you meant, find the sentence that caused it. Usually it is one sentence, and usually it is not the one you would have guessed. Diagnose it with the tools from lessons 3 to 6: is the character missing, is the point in the wrong place, did a cut remove a join?</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Fix those sentences and nothing else</strong>, then write two or three lines recording what you learned about your own writing rather than about this document. That is the part that transfers.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>If nothing needed changing, say so, and say how you know.</strong> A test that always finds a problem is not a test. But before you record that result, check that you asked for a paraphrase and not for a verdict.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Where this leaves you</h2>\n<p>The course ends here, and it ends deliberately with the only tool in it that does not depend on trusting the course.</p>\n<p>Everything else you have been given is a claim about readers, from research this course read for you and named so that you can check it. Lesson 8 showed you how to check a claim like that, including these ones. But paraphrase testing does something the rest cannot: it tells you about <strong>your</strong> writing and <strong>your</strong> readers, this week, and it will go on telling you long after you have forgotten which principle was number four.</p>\n<p>The project pulls all of it together. One real document, taken through diagnosis, rewriting and a paraphrase test, with a record of what the reader actually said, including the parts that were inconvenient.</p>\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Nancy Sommers, <em>Revision Strategies of Student Writers and Experienced Adult Writers</em>, report to the National Institute of Education, 12 June 1982, <a href=\"https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED220839.pdf\">ERIC ED220839</a>. The method and sample, the central finding about procedures against a codified theory, the rejection of the linear stage theory, the dissonance model, the four operations and four levels, the textbook analysis, and the quotations from Michael and Debra. <strong>A note on this citation:</strong> Sommers&#39; better-known 1980 article of the same title has not been read by this course, because the freely available copy is a scan with no extractable text. Secondary summaries of that article commonly say the study used twenty students and twenty experienced writers. The 1982 report&#39;s own abstract says eight and seven, so this course reports the figures it has read and not the ones in circulation.</li>\n<li>Digital.gov, <a href=\"https://digital.gov/guides/plain-language/test/paraphrase-testing\">paraphrase testing</a>, part of the US federal plain-language guide&#39;s &quot;Test for understanding&quot; section. All quoted guidance on how to run a test, how many participants, and what not to do.</li>\n<li>Sian Gooding, Yevgeni Berzak, Tony Mak and Matt Sharifi, <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.06354\">&quot;Predicting Text Readability from Scrolling Interactions&quot;</a> (2021), 518 participants, on reader background shaping perceived difficulty. Read at abstract level only.</li>\n</ul>\n","quiz":[{"q":"On Sommers' account, what most separates how experienced writers revise from how student writers revise?","options":["Experienced writers make many more changes to each draft than student writers make","Experienced writers hold a codified theory of revision, where students have procedures only","Experienced writers revise only after finishing, while students revise while they are writing","Experienced writers work more slowly, giving each draft considerably more time than students do"],"answer":1,"explain":"Her finding is that students have operational procedures and reasons for them but have not synthesized these into a theory, while experienced writers have a codified set of principles. That is B. A makes it a matter of quantity, which the study does not. C reverses her finding, since for experienced writers revision was not a stage but ran throughout. D introduces time, which is not the distinction she draws."},{"q":"A colleague says the document is finished because they have read it through twice and fixed everything they noticed. What does this course say is missing?","options":["A readability score, which would settle whether the writing sits at the right level for them","Another pass, since a third reading usually finds what the first two passes missed","A colleague from the same team to read it, since a second opinion catches more","Evidence from someone outside the writing about what they took the document to mean"],"answer":3,"explain":"The writer already knows what they meant, so rereading tests recall rather than comprehension, and only another person can supply what a reader actually took from it. That is D. A trusts a surface measure the course has argued against twice. B repeats the flawed test. C is closer but a colleague who shares the writer's knowledge shares the writer's blind spot."},{"q":"In a paraphrase test, a participant explains a section back to you and gets it wrong. What should you do at that moment?","options":["Write down their explanation in their own words, and do not correct them","Explain what the section actually means, then ask them to read it again","Note that the participant was unsuitable and continue with the next section","Ask whether they found the section confusing, so the difficulty is on record"],"answer":0,"explain":"The federal guidance is explicit, take notes in the participant's words and do not correct the participant, because the misreading is the finding. That is A. B destroys the evidence by teaching the participant the answer. C blames the reader for the result you were testing for. D asks a question the guidance warns against, since it invites a yes or no rather than an explanation."},{"q":"Which is the better use of the four operations Sommers identifies, deletion, substitution, addition and reordering?","options":["As a checklist of the four things that should be done to every draft, in that fixed order","As a target, since a good revision should use all four operations at least once","As a description of what changed, so you can see which level you actually worked at","As a ranking, from the weakest operation, deletion, up to the strongest, reordering"],"answer":2,"explain":"She pairs the four operations with four levels, word, phrase, sentence and thema, precisely to describe what a writer did, which lets you see whether you worked at the level the draft needed. That is C. A turns a coding scheme into a procedure. B invents a quota. D ranks operations she does not rank."},{"q":"A rewrite improves a document's readability score. What have you learned?","options":["That readers will now understand the document better than they did before","That the words or the sentences are shorter, and nothing about comprehension","That the reading level is appropriate for the audience the document is aimed at","That the document is now suitable for readers with lower levels of literacy"],"answer":1,"explain":"Formulas count surface features such as syllables per word and words per sentence, so a better score records that those counts fell. That is B. A, C and D all treat the proxy as the outcome, and lesson 1 gave the reason that will not do, since research on reader background shows difficulty is a relation between a text and a reader rather than a property of the text."},{"q":"Why does this lesson say revision must come before editing?","options":["Because editing is more enjoyable, so it works better as a reward for finishing","Because most of the style guides recommend that order, for the sake of consistency","Because editing takes longer, so leaving it late reduces the risk of running out of time","Because polishing sentences you may then cut spends effort on text that will not survive"],"answer":3,"explain":"Revising is structural and can remove whole sections, so any sentence-level work done first may be thrown away, which is D. A appeals to enjoyment. B appeals to authority, which lesson 8 argued against. C has the reasoning backwards, since the order is about waste rather than about scheduling."}]}},"assessments":{"final-test":{"content":"<p>Twenty questions covering the whole course. Twelve or more are application rather than recall, which\nmeans most of them present a case the lessons did not discuss and ask you to do something with it.</p>\n<p>The pass mark is 80 percent, which is sixteen. If you do not reach it, the explanations name which\nlesson each item comes from, and the items you missed are the lessons worth rereading rather than\nthe whole course.</p>\n<p>One thing worth saying before you start. Several items have two defensible answers and one best\nanswer, and the explanation says which is second best and why. That is deliberate. Almost nothing in\nthis course is a rule, so the useful skill is choosing well rather than recalling a law.</p>\n","quiz":[{"q":"Using the figures from lesson 1, roughly how much of an ordinary 600-word web page does a visitor have time to take in?","options":["About a third of it, and the share falls as the page gets longer, because attention grows far more slowly than length does","All of it, since six hundred words is well inside what a reader will finish before they decide whether to continue","About four fifths of it, since readers give roughly four seconds of attention for every hundred words that a page contains","None of it can be estimated, because the study measured only how long people stayed and never what they actually understood"],"answer":0,"explain":"A reader gives about twenty five seconds plus four point four seconds per hundred words, while reading six hundred words at two hundred and fifty words a minute would take about one hundred and forty four seconds. That is roughly a third, and the ratio worsens with length. B ignores the crossover at about a hundred and thirty words. C misreads the four point four seconds as generous rather than as a fifth of what reading costs. D is right that the study measured time and wrong that nothing follows, since the arithmetic is what the finding is made of."},{"q":"You are writing to residents about a change to bin collections. Which statement of the audience would actually change a sentence in the letter?","options":["Residents of the borough, since that is who will receive the letter through their letterboxes over the course of next week","Adults living in urban areas, who are the group most affected by changes to the way household waste is collected and sorted","People whose collection day is moving, who need to know the new day and what to do with the bin they have already put out","Council taxpayers, because they are funding the service and therefore have the strongest claim on being told about changes"],"answer":2,"explain":"Naming an audience means naming what they came needing, which is what tells you the first sentence. C does that. A and D name categories that could not change a word of the letter. B is a demographic description, which is a different thing again from a reader with a question."},{"q":"Which of these is the clearest case for NOT putting the point in the first sentence?","options":["A notice telling season ticket holders that prices rise by four percent in May, sent out three months before the change takes effect","A proposal to a committee that has rejected the same idea twice before and is expected to arrive at the meeting opposed to it","An email asking a colleague to send a file by Friday, where the colleague is busy and receives a great many emails every day","A safety notice on a chemical store, which has to be understood in a hurry by someone who is not expecting to read anything"],"answer":1,"explain":"Front-loading is a default with real exceptions, and the clearest is a reader who arrives disagreeing, since a conclusion offered first invites them to argue rather than read. That is B. A, C and D are all cases where the reader wants the fact immediately, and D is the strongest case for front-loading in the list rather than against it."},{"q":"\"Consideration of the proposal was undertaken by the panel in March.\" What are the character and the action?","options":["The character is consideration and the action is undertaking, since those fill the subject and verb positions in the sentence","The character is the proposal and the action is being considered, because the proposal is what the sentence is really about","There is no character, because the sentence is in the passive voice and passive sentences do not have anyone performing an action","The character is the panel and the action is considering, though neither of them is currently in the slot it should occupy"],"answer":3,"explain":"Ask who is doing something. The panel is, and what it did was consider, which is D. A names the grammatical subject and verb, and the mismatch between those and the real character and action is exactly the fault. B picks out a participant that is not the actor. C confuses the passive with having no agent, and the agent is named here after by."},{"q":"Which rewrite of that sentence best follows the course?","options":["A consideration of the proposal took place within the panel during the early part of the month of March that year","The proposal was given consideration by the panel in March, which was when the matter first came before its members","The panel considered the proposal in March","It was in March that consideration of the proposal by the panel was undertaken, as the records of the meeting confirm"],"answer":2,"explain":"C puts the character in the subject and the action in the verb, which is the move. A buries the action twice and adds took place, a verb that names nothing. B keeps the action in a noun and only rearranges. D adds a cleft construction on top of the original fault. Note that C is much the shortest, and that this is a consequence of the fix rather than the aim of it."},{"q":"Which of these should usually be left as a nominalisation?","options":["Inflation fell to three percent","An assessment of the risks was carried out before the work began on site","The implementation of the new policy will take place at some point in April","There was a failure of communication between the two departments involved"],"answer":0,"explain":"When the abstraction is genuinely the topic of the sentence, it belongs in the subject, and A is a sentence about inflation. Rewriting it as prices rose more slowly changes what the sentence is about. B, C and D all hide an actor the reader would benefit from, and in each case undoing the nominalisation forces a useful question about who acted."},{"q":"A colleague proposes cutting every sentence in a report to under twenty words. What is the best objection?","options":["Sentence length has no effect on how easily a document is read, so the exercise would make no difference to anyone either way","Twenty words is too generous a limit, and the guidance this course draws on recommends a maximum of fifteen words per sentence","Short sentences read as abrupt, so a document written entirely in them would strike most readers as rude or unhelpfully terse","Length is a proxy, and a long sentence whose subject and verb sit together can read more easily than a short one where they do not"],"answer":3,"explain":"The fault is the wait between a subject and its verb, and length only correlates with it, which is D. A denies that length signals anything, which overcorrects. B invents a figure, since the guidance suggests splitting sentences over twenty five words. C raises tone, which is a real issue and not the one at stake."},{"q":"\"The report, which was commissioned after the inspection and circulated to every member before the recess, recommends closure.\" You want the reader to finish thinking about the recommendation. Which fix?","options":["Split it into two sentences, putting the recommendation first and the history second, so that both facts land in their own stress position","Move the interrupting material to the front, so the sentence closes on the recommendation rather than on the report's history","Leave it as it stands, because the interruption tells the reader which report is meant and therefore earns the place that it occupies","Move the interrupting material to the end, so the sentence closes on the commissioning and the circulation to members before the recess"],"answer":1,"explain":"The end of a sentence is where the reader places weight, so putting the history first leaves the recommendation last, which is B. D produces the opposite emphasis. A is a sound fix that gives two stress positions rather than concentrating on one, so it is second best here. C misapplies the earns-its-place test, which covers short identifying material rather than twenty words of history."},{"q":"A paragraph's five sentences are each clear, yet the paragraph reads as a list of unrelated facts. What should you check first?","options":["Whether each sentence after the first opens with something the reader has already been given by the sentence before it","Whether the paragraph would be better as five separate paragraphs, since each sentence appears to introduce a separate topic","Whether the sentences need transition words such as however and therefore added at their joins to signal the relations between them","Whether the vocabulary is too varied, so that the reader cannot tell that two different words are referring to the same thing"],"answer":0,"explain":"Cohesion lives in the topic position, so the first check is whether old information opens each sentence, which is A. C is the standard patch, and it labels a connection rather than making one. D names a real fault, the thesaurus habit, which is not what produces this particular effect. B treats a joining problem as a structural one."},{"q":"A paragraph is about a patient's recovery. Which sentence belongs in it?","options":["The consultant discharged her on the fourth day, having reviewed the results of the second set of blood tests that morning","On the fourth day, following a review of the results, the consultant made the decision to discharge her from the ward","She was discharged on the fourth day","The discharge took place on the fourth day, after the consultant had reviewed the results of the second set of blood tests"],"answer":2,"explain":"The paragraph is about her, so she belongs in the topic position, and the passive is how you put her there. That is C. A and B both move the story to the consultant, which a reader will carry into the next sentence. D removes the character altogether and buries the action in a noun."},{"q":"\"The dean's statements tend to completely polarize the faculty.\" What does the course say this sentence stresses?","options":["The dean, since the sentence opens with the dean and the opening is where readers place the greatest weight in any sentence","Nothing in particular, because a split infinitive is grammatically neutral and does not affect emphasis in either direction","The completeness, because placing the adverb before the verb gives that adverb an unusual degree of stress in the sentence","The polarizing, because tucking the adverb in before the verb has the effect of de-emphasising the adverb rather than stressing it"],"answer":3,"explain":"Pullum's correction is that the split de-emphasises the adverb, so the stress falls on polarizing at the end, which is D. C repeats the claim he shows is backwards. A confuses the topic position, which sets perspective, with the stress position. B denies there is an effect at all."},{"q":"Which cut is best described as removing metadiscourse?","options":["Changing due to the fact that the deadline has now passed into because the deadline has now passed","Removing it should be noted at the outset that from the beginning of a sentence reporting a decision","Changing there is a possibility that we may possibly need to into we may need to, within a longer sentence","Changing the previous system was not able to handle the volume into the old system could not handle it"],"answer":1,"explain":"Metadiscourse is writing about the writing, and it should be noted that tells a reader only that a sentence is coming, so B is the case. A is a phrase doing the work of one word. C is hedge stacking. D is a negative replaced by a positive. Naming the kind is the skill, because it is what makes the cut repeatable."},{"q":"\"Replacing those beams would cost two million pounds. That cost is far more than the four hundred thousand left in the budget.\" A colleague cuts the four words that cost is. What is the cost of the cut?","options":["Nothing, because both figures survive and the sentence is four words shorter than it was before the change was made","A fact, because the comparison between the two figures no longer appears anywhere in the passage after the words are removed","The link, because the second sentence no longer opens with something the reader was given by the sentence before it","The emphasis, because the two million now falls at the end of the first sentence rather than at the start of the second"],"answer":2,"explain":"That cost is old information in the topic position, holding the two sentences together, so cutting it leaves the second opening on a fresh figure. That is C. A counts words and misses the join. B is close but wrong, since the comparison survives in far more. D describes something the cut does not change."},{"q":"Which heading does the job this course asks a heading to do?","options":["Why the eastern boundary is moving to exclude Mill Lane","Background and context to the current proposals","Update on the parking scheme consultation exercise","Further considerations and possible next steps"],"answer":0,"explain":"A heading has to let a skimming reader decide whether to stop, which needs it to carry the information rather than name a category. A does that. B and D are labels that would fit almost any document. C names a topic but not what happened, so a reader still has to enter the section to find out whether it matters."},{"q":"You have three reasons that interact, where one is an objection and another answers it. Which form suits them?","options":["A bulleted list, since three items is the standard length at which a list becomes easier to scan than a paragraph of prose","Prose, because the relations between the three are the content, and a list would present them as parallel and independent","A numbered list, because numbering will show the reader which of the three reasons is the most important of them","A table, so that each reason can be set out in one column with its answer beside it in a second column of the same row"],"answer":1,"explain":"A list claims its items are parallel and independent, and an objection and its answer are neither, so the although and the therefore would be thrown away. That is B. A treats bullets as a default. C uses numbering to mean importance, when it means sequence. D would work if every reason had an answer, which is not what the question describes."},{"q":"What did opening Strunk's 1918 text establish about two of Pullum's specific charges?","options":["That both charges were mistaken, since the rules he attacks do not appear anywhere in the book in the form that he reports","That both charges understated the problem, because the 1918 text states the rules more strongly than the later edition does","That both charges were about style advice rather than grammar, which is the half of the book Pullum says is mostly harmless","That the rule about none and the ban on which are not in the 1918 edition, so they belong to White's later revision"],"answer":3,"explain":"Neither rule appears in Strunk's original, so those two charges land on White, which is D. A overstates, since the rules do exist in the book people read. B reverses the finding. C misdescribes both charges, which are about grammar and not style."},{"q":"You want to test a rule you were taught. Which step comes first?","options":["Write the rule out precisely, in a form that could turn out to be either true or false","Search a corpus of writing from the period in which the rule was first being taught","Count how often good writers follow the rule and how often they depart from it","Decide whether the rule is a rule of English, a house style, or simply a superstition"],"answer":0,"explain":"A vague rule cannot be tested at all, and vagueness is how bad rules survive, so stating it precisely comes first. That is A. B and C are the second and third steps and both depend on knowing exactly what is being counted. D is the verdict, which comes last."},{"q":"In a paraphrase test, a reader confidently explains your section back to you and gets it wrong. What do you do?","options":["Explain what the section was meant to say, then ask them to read it once more and describe it again in their own words","Ask whether they found that section confusing, so that the difficulty they had is recorded alongside their explanation","Write down what they said in their own words, without correcting them, and move on to the next section of the document","Note that this reader is not representative of the audience, and repeat the section with a different participant instead"],"answer":2,"explain":"The misreading is the finding, and the guidance says to take notes in the participant's words and not to correct them. That is C. A destroys the evidence by supplying the answer. B asks a question answerable with yes or no, and a confident misreader did not feel confused. D discards a result for being inconvenient."},{"q":"A rewrite moves a document from grade twelve to grade eight on a readability formula. What follows?","options":["That readers with lower literacy will now be able to use the document, which is what the grade level scale was designed to report","That the words or the sentences got shorter, and nothing at all about whether anyone understands the document any better","That comprehension has improved by a measurable amount, since grade level is a validated measure of how well text is understood","That the rewrite was successful, provided the target audience matches the grade level the document has now been brought to"],"answer":1,"explain":"Formulas count surface features such as syllables per word and words per sentence, so a changed score records changed counts. That is B. A, C and D all treat the proxy as the outcome, and research on reader background gives the reason that will not do, since difficulty is a relation between a text and a reader rather than a property of the text."},{"q":"Which of these best describes what this course says clear writing is?","options":["A set of rules about grammar and word choice which, once learned, can be applied consistently to any piece of writing at all","Writing in the simplest possible words, so that the largest possible number of readers can follow what has been written","Arranging what you have to say so that it matches what readers are known to do with order, structure and emphasis","A matter of taste on which informed people differ, so that any consistent style is as defensible as any other style"],"answer":2,"explain":"The course teaches the reader-expectation tradition, which describes what readers do and lets the writer draw conclusions, which is C. A is the rule tradition the course argues against. B is the misreading answered in lesson 1, where high-literacy readers are shown to prefer plain English. D abandons the evidence the whole course rests on."}]},"project-one-document":{"content":"<p>The test measures whether you can recognise the moves. This project measures whether you can make them on writing that matters to you, and then find out from another human being whether any of it worked. That last part is what separates this from every writing exercise you have done before, and it is the part people skip.</p>\n<p>Budget about four hours, spread over a week rather than done in one sitting. The gap matters. On Tuesday you will see things in your own prose that were invisible on Sunday, for the same reason lesson 4 gave: you cannot feel a wait when you already know what is coming.</p>\n<h2>Choose the document</h2>\n<p>One real document of your own, between 400 and 1,200 words, that somebody actually had to act on. An email that asked for something, a report, a proposal, a letter, a set of instructions, a funding application.</p>\n<p>Three rules about the choice.</p>\n<p><strong>It must be real and already sent.</strong> Not something written for this project. The whole exercise depends on the document having been produced under the conditions you actually write under.</p>\n<p><strong>Somebody must have had to do something with it.</strong> Writing whose only job was to exist cannot be tested, because there is no right answer for a reader to arrive at.</p>\n<p><strong>Not your best piece.</strong> Choose one you were slightly uneasy about, or one that produced a reply asking a question you thought you had answered. That reply is a finding, and it is the closest thing to free evidence you will get.</p>\n<p>If you have been carrying a document since lesson 1, use that one.</p>\n<h2>Part 1: the original, untouched</h2>\n<p>Submit it exactly as it went out, with the date it was written and one line saying who received it and what you needed them to do.</p>\n<p>Do not tidy it first. The urge to fix a typo before anyone sees the original is strong and it defeats the purpose, which is to have an honest before.</p>\n<h2>Part 2: the diagnosis</h2>\n<p>One page, and this is the part that carries the most weight in the rubric. <strong>Name specific sentences.</strong> A diagnosis that says &quot;the tone is a bit formal&quot; or &quot;it could be clearer&quot; is not a diagnosis; it is an impression, and impressions are what the whole course was built to replace.</p>\n<p>Cover these six, in writing:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The reader.</strong> Who they are and what they came needing. Test it against lesson 1: if knowing this would not change a sentence, you have not answered it.</li>\n<li><strong>Where the point currently sits</strong>, counted in words from the start. Then the reading budget arithmetic from lesson 1 for the document&#39;s length.</li>\n<li><strong>Three sentences whose character and action are buried.</strong> Quote each one and fill in the two columns from lesson 3, character and action, in ordinary words, before proposing any rewrite.</li>\n<li><strong>The worst gap between a subject and its verb.</strong> Quote the sentence and give the number.</li>\n<li><strong>One paragraph that does not cohere.</strong> Quote it, then list the opening words of each of its sentences, as lesson 5 taught. Say which sentence breaks the chain.</li>\n<li><strong>Your headings, listed on their own.</strong> If the document has none, say what they would be. Then say whether that list alone tells the story.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Part 3: the rewrite</h2>\n<p>Rewrite the document. Then, underneath, a short table with one row per significant change:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>What I changed</th>\n<th>Which principle</th>\n<th>What it cost</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n</table></div>\n<p>The third column is not decoration. Most changes cost something: a front-loaded decision loses a gentle opening, a cut sentence takes a join with it, a split sentence loses a rhythm. Saying what a change cost is how you show you made a judgement rather than applied a rule.</p>\n<p><strong>At least one row must be a place where you decided a principle did not apply here.</strong> Every lesson in this course had a wrinkle, and a rewrite that applied nine principles nine times without exception has almost certainly damaged something. If you genuinely cannot find one, say so and say why, and expect that to be the weakest part of your submission.</p>\n<h2>Part 4: the paraphrase test</h2>\n<p>One reader. Not a colleague on the same project, not someone who helped write it, and not someone who will try to be encouraging.</p>\n<p>Follow lesson 9. Section by section, ask them to tell you in their own words what it means, and what they would do if they received it. <strong>Write down what they say in their words. Do not correct them.</strong> Then submit:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>What you asked, and what they said, in their words. Verbatim where you can.</li>\n<li>Every gap between what they said and what you meant, with the sentence you think caused each one.</li>\n<li>What you changed as a result.</li>\n<li><strong>If nothing needed changing, say so and say how you know.</strong> That is a legitimate result, and it is much more convincing when you can point to the questions you asked. Before you record it, check that you asked for a paraphrase rather than for a verdict.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Rubric</h2>\n<p>Marked on five things, in this order of weight.</p>\n<p><strong>1. The diagnosis names sentences, not impressions.</strong> This is the commonest failure and the heaviest. Every claim in part 2 should quote the text it is about. A diagnosis you could paste onto somebody else&#39;s document is not a diagnosis of yours.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Changes are traceable to named principles, with their costs.</strong> Anyone can improve a document by fiddling with it. The point is to be able to say why each change is an improvement, in terms that will still work next month on a different document.</p>\n<p><strong>3. At least one considered exception.</strong> A place where you decided front-loading was wrong, or a nominalisation should stay, or a repetition was a join rather than padding, with the reason. This is the single clearest signal that the course has been understood rather than memorised, because every principle in it is a default.</p>\n<p><strong>4. The paraphrase test reports what happened, including the inconvenient parts.</strong> A record in which the reader understood everything perfectly is either a lucky document or, far more often, a test where the writer explained as they went. If your reader misread something, that is the most valuable line in your submission.</p>\n<p><strong>5. The rewrite is better, and you can say how you know.</strong> Note the order. This is last, because a rewrite that is better by accident teaches you nothing, and the first four are what make it repeatable.</p>\n<h2>What a strong submission looks like</h2>\n<p>The diagnosis is uncomfortable to write. It quotes three or four sentences the writer was previously happy with and says exactly what is wrong with each.</p>\n<p>The change table has a row that reads roughly: <em>moved the deadline to the first sentence; front-loading, lesson 2; cost is that the letter now opens with a demand and the thanks come afterwards, which I judged worth it because two people missed the deadline last time.</em></p>\n<p>There is an exception row, and it is specific.</p>\n<p>The paraphrase record contains at least one sentence beginning &quot;she thought it meant&quot;, followed by something the writer did not mean.</p>\n<p>And the final paragraph says what the writer learned about their own writing rather than about this document. That is the part that transfers, and it is why the project is worth four hours.</p>\n","quiz":[]}}};
